I can help you get organized and prepare the right questions for Amy so you know whether you need more evidence before the hearing. Below is a focused checklist of evidence to consider, plus a set of clear questions you can ask your attorney (grouped by topic). At the end I’ll suggest next practical steps and offer to draft an email to Amy with these questions if you’d like.

If anything below isn’t quite right (which case is primary, whether you’re the tenant or the landlord, how the immigration proceedings tie in, or dates), tell me and I’ll tailor it.

1) Quick clarifying questions for you (so I can be more specific)
- Are you the tenant or the landlord in the eviction case?
- Is the custody case and the eviction case directly related (same opposing party) or separate matters that may affect one another?
- Do you already have a hearing date? Any upcoming deadlines?
- Do you want me to draft a concise email to Amy that asks these questions?

2) Evidence checklist — what courts commonly want in landlord-tenant / eviction matters (and related custody/immigration issues)
General documents
- Lease or rental agreement (signed pages and any amendments)
- Rent ledger / payment records (receipts, bank transfers, checks, money orders)
- All notices (eviction notices, cure/quit notices, 30/10-day notices, termination notices)
- Correspondence (texts, emails, messages) between you and the landlord/tenant/opposing parent
- Photographs and videos showing condition of property, repairs, damage, or conditions at relevant dates (with timestamps if possible)
- Repair/maintenance requests and responses (work orders, invoices)
- Move-in / move-out condition reports, inspection reports, security deposit statements
- Repair receipts and estimates (if habitability is at issue)
- Police reports, incident reports, or 911 calls relevant to safety, disturbance, or alleged illegal activity
- Witness contact info and short statements (neighbors, roommates, contractors)
- Utility bills, mail receipts, or other proof of residence

Custody-related evidence (if custody overlap)
- Child’s school records, attendance, teacher statements
- Medical records for child (appointments, diagnoses, treatment)
- Documented parenting time logs (dates/times you had custody or the other parent did)
- CPS / child welfare records, if any
- Communications showing parental cooperation or conflict about child care

Immigration-related documentation (if immigration court considers these facts)
- Proof of residence and length of residence (lease, bills)
- Family and community ties (letters from employers, community leaders, clergy)
- Any documents relevant to credibility or admissibility (arrest records, criminal case dispositions, immigration filings)
- Evidence of hardship or country conditions (if relevant for relief)

3) Evidence quality & admissibility items to ask about
- Do the documents need to be authenticated (signed affidavits, notarizations, or witness testimony)?
- Do any items risk being excluded as hearsay? If so, how should we present them (e.g., witness to testify about them)?
- Are photos/videos sufficient as-is or do they need a witness to verify when/how they were taken?
- Chain of custody for any physical evidence (documents, devices)

4) Questions to ask Amy Foster — copy/paste these or use them as prompts
Case status & strategy
- Can you summarize the current case posture and what issues the judge is likely to decide at the next hearing?
- Based on discovery results, what are our strongest and weakest pieces of evidence?
- What is our overall strategy for the hearing (e.g., focus on habitability, nonpayment, parental fitness, immigration relief)?

Evidence-specific
- Which documents or pieces of evidence do you consider essential to have at the hearing?
- Are there any obvious gaps in our evidence that you recommend filling now?
- Which items will we need an attending witness for (e.g., landlord, property manager, neighbor, contractor, school official)?
- Do you recommend getting sworn affidavits from witnesses or do you want live testimony?
- Do photographs or videos need metadata or witness verification for admissibility?

Witnesses and experts
- Should we depose or subpoena any witnesses before the hearing?
- Do you recommend expert testimony (property condition expert, accountant, psychologist, pediatrician) and when would we need to engage them?
- Will the opposing party present witnesses we need to rebut? If so, which?

Discovery & motions
- Are there remaining discovery tools available (subpoenas, depositions, requests for admission, additional document requests)?
- Do you recommend filing any pre-hearing motions now (motion in limine, motion to exclude evidence, motion to compel)?
- If the opposing party withheld documents in discovery, do you recommend a motion to compel or sanctions?

Procedural / logistics (Maricopa County & Immigration Court)
- Are there local rules or immigration-court rules that affect exhibit lists, filing deadlines, or how exhibits must be marked?
- What is the deadline to exchange/submit exhibit lists and witness lists to the court and to opposing counsel?
- Will the hearing be in person, by teleconference, or video (and will we need interpreters)?
- How many copies of exhibits should I bring, and do you need originals or certified copies?

Admissibility & preparation
- Which documents must we lay foundation for and how should we do that?
- Should I prepare a timeline or a one-page summary of events for the judge? If so, what format is most helpful?
- Are there authenticity concerns (e.g., deleted messages, altered documents) we should address now?

Interaction between cases / immigration implications
- Could the eviction or custody facts harm my immigration case? If so, how should we document / mitigate that risk?
- What immigration-specific evidence would strengthen my position (proof of family ties, hardship, employment)?
- Do we need to coordinate filings across the courts to avoid contradicting statements?

Costs & timing
- How long will it take to obtain any additional evidence you recommend (affidavits, expert reports, subpoenas)?
- What are estimated costs for additional discovery or experts?
- What should be prioritized if time or budget is limited?

Settlement & alternatives
- Are settlement or alternative dispute options realistic at this stage?
- If we settle, what evidence or terms should we obtain in writing to protect my immigration or custody interests?

5) How to organize materials for Amy and the court
- Create a short case summary (1 page) with: parties, claims, key dates, relief requested, current status, and main evidence bullets.
- Make an exhibit index: Exhibit #, short description, date, and file name. Keep originals in a folder; provide copies to counsel.
- Prepare a timeline (chronological) with supporting exhibit numbers next to each entry.
- Prepare witness list with short bullet of what each witness will testify to and their contact info.
- Flag sensitive or sealed materials and ask about protective orders.

6) Practical next steps I recommend you take now
- Inventory what you already have (use the evidence checklist above) and note what’s missing.
- Prepare the one-page case summary and exhibit index for Amy.
- Ask Amy the questions above. If you want, I can draft a concise email to Amy listing these key questions and attaching a template exhibit index you can fill in.
- If witnesses are crucial, start contacting them to get written statements and contact info (and preserve their availability).

7) Offer: draft the email or checklist for you
If you want, I can:
- Draft a short email to Amy Foster that includes the most important questions and attaches a checklist/exhibit index template; or
- Produce a fillable exhibit index and hearing-prep checklist you can complete and send to Amy.

Which would you prefer? And please answer the clarifying questions at the top so I can tailor the email/checklist precisely to your situation.

