I can definitely help — below is a focused list of questions to bring to Amy Foster plus a practical checklist of evidence you may need (grouped by issue), and tips for organizing everything before your next court date in Maricopa County. If you’d like, I can also draft an email to Amy with these questions or help you build an exhibit binder.

Suggested questions to ask your attorney (Amy Foster)
- Case status & strategy
  - What is our current theory/strategy for the eviction and for any immigration issues that are connected to it?
  - Which specific facts does the court need to find in our favor at the hearing?
  - Which issues are likely to be disputed by the other side?
- Evidence sufficiency & gaps
  - Do we already have enough evidence to prove each element of our claims/defenses? If not, which elements need more support?
  - Which specific documents or types of proof would you like me to obtain before the hearing?
- Discovery follow-up & formal steps
  - Are there any outstanding discovery responses or documents we should compel from the opposing party?
  - Do we need to subpoena records (e.g., landlord ledger, ISP records, employer payroll, medical records, school records)? If so, what should I authorize you to subpoena?
- Witnesses & witness statements
  - Which witnesses should we call (neighbors, co-tenants, property manager, police, school officials, medical providers)?
  - Do you want signed affidavits or written witness statements before the hearing? Should they be notarized?
- Authentication & admissibility
  - Which documents need to be certified/notarized or otherwise authenticated (rent ledgers, official notices, government records, translations)?
  - Are there any hearsay or evidentiary issues I should be aware of with my documents (e.g., text messages, third-party emails)?
- Experts & evaluations
  - Would an expert (housing inspector, pediatrician/child psychologist, social worker, immigration expert) be helpful? If so, what issues should the expert address and by when?
- Immigration-specific documents
  - Which immigration documents and country/medical/community documentation will the immigration court or counsel expect to see at the hearing?
- Custody overlap (if relevant)
  - Are custody orders or parental conduct issues relevant to the eviction/immigration matter? If yes, what custody documents or records should we include?
- Logistics & court procedure
  - What filings do we need to submit before the hearing (exhibit lists, witness lists, pre-hearing brief, motions in limine)?
  - What are the Maricopa County/immigration court local rules and deadlines I should follow (copies, number of exhibits, format)?
  - How many copies of exhibits should I bring and should anything be uploaded to e-filing in advance?
- Timeline & contingency
  - If we need more time to gather evidence, should we request a continuance? What is the process and likelihood of success?
- Settlement options
  - Are there any settlement or alternative options we should pursue before going to hearing?

Practical checklist: documents & evidence to consider gathering
(Adapt to which issues actually apply to your situation.)

Landlord-tenant / eviction evidence
- Lease or rental agreement (all pages, including renewal or termination letters)
- Rent payment records: bank statements, canceled checks, money orders, receipts, rent ledger
- Eviction notices served (3-day/notice to quit, 30/60 day notices) and proof of service
- Any written communications with landlord/tenant (texts, emails, letters); print and date-stamp
- Photographs and videos of the property (showing condition, damage, habitability issues), with dates
- Repair requests and maintenance records (dates submitted and any follow-up)
- City code violation or inspection reports (housing/health department)
- Receipts for repairs you paid for or that demonstrate condition
- Police reports, incident reports, or calls for service related to the property
- Witness contact info and short statements (neighbors, building staff)
- Move-in/move-out condition documents, inventory lists
- Prior eviction filings or judgments (if any)

Custody-related evidence (if it intersects with the eviction or immigration matter)
- Existing custody or parenting orders and any modification petitions
- School enrollment and attendance records for children
- Medical records for children showing appointments or issues
- Communication logs about custody/visitation (texts, emails, call logs)
- Records of child support payments or arrears
- Any protective orders, police reports, or child welfare reports
- Witness statements relevant to parental fitness or child safety

Immigration-related evidence (for immigration court)
- Notices from immigration court (NTA, hearing notices) and all prior filings
- Passport, visa, I-94, birth certificate(s), marriage certificate(s), naturalization documents
- Any prior removal/deportation orders and related records
- Criminal history records, charging documents, dispositions (if applicable)
- Medical records or expert reports supporting hardship claims
- Affidavits or declaration(s) from family, employers, community leaders about ties and hardship
- Employment records, tax returns, pay stubs, and proof of residence
- Country conditions documentation (if relevant) and certified translations of foreign documents
- Any pending immigration applications (forms and receipts)

Authentication, formatting, and reliability
- Certified or original copies of government records when possible
- Certified translations for non-English documents
- Signed affidavits from witnesses summarizing their testimony
- Metadata or origin evidence for digital items (emails, text logs); print with header info or include screenshots showing timestamps
- Chain-of-custody documentation for any physical evidence

How to organize what you already have (so Amy can review quickly)
- Create a short timeline (one page) listing key dates and events in chronological order
- Prepare an exhibit index: Exhibit #, short description, date, and what fact it supports
- Put documents in a binder or folder by exhibit number and bring copies (electronic and paper)
- Prepare a 1–2 page summary of disputed facts and what you believe needs proving
- Create short bullet-point witness summaries (what each witness will say)

Questions to ask about concrete next steps
- Which documents should I prioritize collecting in the next 7–14 days?
- Do you want me to get signed affidavits from X, Y, Z people? If yes, what should those affidavits say?
- If we need official records (school, medical), what release forms or authorizations do you want me to sign?
- Do you recommend any experts? If so, how quickly do we need to retain them?
- Will you prepare an exhibit list/witness list or should I draft one for your review?

Evidentiary and procedural tips specific to court readiness
- Ask Amy whether anything needs to be certified (e.g., public records) ahead of time.
- Confirm whether the immigration court requires originals or copies and whether they accept electronic filings.
- Pre-mark exhibits and have multiple copies (judge, opposing counsel, yourself, and file copy).
- If texts/emails are important, export them with timestamps or take clear screenshots including metadata.
- Prepare short, plain English summaries of complex documents for the judge.
- Practice a concise statement of facts (30–60 seconds) so your lawyer can present your key points quickly.

Offer to help
- I can draft a concise email to Amy with the above questions and attach an exhibit list template if you want.
- I can help you build the timeline, prepare an exhibit index, or prepare affidavit templates for witnesses.

Would you like me to:
- Draft the email to Amy with these questions? (If yes, tell me whether you want it formal or conversational and I'll draft it.)
- Start an exhibit index or timeline based on documents you already have? If so, tell me what documents you currently have and I’ll create the index.

