I can help you get organized. Below are (A) a short checklist of clarifying facts I need from you, (B) a prioritized list of specific questions to ask Amy Foster, grouped by topic with suggested phrasing you can use, (C) a practical evidence checklist (what to gather/prepare), and (D) how to organize exhibits and witness info so you’re ready for the next steps.

Note: this is organizational help, not formal legal advice. Your lawyer will confirm what is required under Arizona/local and immigration-court procedure.

A — Quick clarifying questions for you
- Is the next hearing in state eviction/civil court or in immigration court? (You mentioned both.)
- Are you the landlord or the tenant? (That changes what evidence is needed.)
- Do you know whether the hearing will be a trial, status conference, or preliminary hearing?
- What key documents you already have (lease, notices, messages, receipts, police reports, medical/custody documents)?
Answering these will let me tailor the checklist further.

B — Prioritized questions to ask Amy Foster (with suggested wording)
Write these down and bring them to your meeting or call.

1) Case status & overall strategy
- “What is our current case status and the likely timeline to the next hearing or trial?”
- “What is our best-case and worst-case outcome at the next hearing?”
- “Are there any procedural or jurisdictional issues we should be concerned about (especially with immigration court involvement)?”

2) What additional evidence is needed / useful
- “Given the discovery we completed, what specific additional evidence would strengthen our position before the next hearing?”
- “Which facts does the court care most about in this eviction/custody overlap, and what proof will satisfy the court on those issues?”
- “Are there weaknesses in our evidence you think we should fix now?”

3) Admissibility & foundation
- “Which types of documents will the court accept without extra steps, and which need authentication or a sponsoring witness (business records, emails, text messages, receipts)?”
- “For written communications, do we need to get witness declarations to authenticate them?”
- “Do we need business-record certifications, notarized copies, or original documents for any items?”

4) Witnesses & declarations
- “Who should we call as witnesses (e.g., myself, neighbors, property manager, repair person, medical providers)?”
- “Should we get signed, sworn declarations from witnesses now? If so, what should those declarations say and who must sign them/notarize them?”
- “Do we need expert witnesses (property inspectors, pediatricians, social workers, economists) and when would we disclose them?”

5) Subpoenas & discovery follow-up
- “Should we issue subpoenas now for records (landlord/tenant records, phone records, police reports, school records)?”
- “If we need a document from a third party, what’s your plan/timing for getting it?”

6) Motions and pre-hearing filings
- “Are there any pre-hearing motions we should file (motions in limine, to exclude evidence, to compel documents, or to bifurcate issues)?”
- “What are the deadlines for exhibit lists, witness lists, and any pre-trial statements?”

7) Immigration/custody intersection
- “Does anything in the eviction/custody facts affect the immigration proceedings (e.g., evidence that will be shared, timing conflicts, or impact on credibility)? How should we coordinate?”
- “Will anything raised in immigration court affect admissibility or strategy in the eviction/custody matter?”

8) Practical preparation & trial logistics
- “How should I prepare my testimony? Are there specific facts I should focus on or avoid?”
- “How many copies of exhibits do you want me to bring, and do you want them pre-marked?”
- “Do you recommend we prepare a one-page timeline or exhibit index for the judge — and if so, can you draft a template?”

9) Settlement & alternatives
- “Should we attempt settlement/mediation now or wait until after the hearing? What are the pros and cons?”
- “What settlement terms would be acceptable to you, and what would you consider a deal-breaker?”

10) Costs & next steps
- “What costs/time will additional evidence gathering (experts/subpoenas) incur? Are these costs justified given the likely outcome?”
- “What should I do next week and before our next meeting to move things forward?”

C — Evidence checklist: what to gather and why
Divide materials into categories. For each item, note date, source, and how it supports a claim.

Identity of case & basic documents
- Lease or rental agreement (signed copy)
- Move-in/move-out inspection reports
- Any custody orders or family-court documents that the opposing parent has filed
- Court pleadings, discovery responses, deposition transcripts (if any)

Notices & legal/official communications
- Notices to pay/quit, eviction notices, or cure notices (copies with proof of delivery)
- Notices from the court, filings, and proof of service

Payment / financial records
- Rent ledgers, bank statements, money order receipts, canceled checks, Venmo/PayPal records
- Receipts for security deposit deductions and repair bills

Communications & documentation of problems
- All emails and text messages between parties, property managers, neighbors, repair vendors (export or screenshot with timestamps)
- Maintenance requests and landlord responses
- Photos/videos showing property condition (include timestamp metadata if available)
- Repair estimates, invoices, receipts, contractor reports

Corroborating witnesses & third-party records
- Names and contact info for potential witnesses (neighbors, repair persons, property managers)
- Police reports, incident reports, 911 calls (certified copies if possible)
- Medical records (if injuries or health issues are relevant), school records (if custody impacts children)

Business & official records
- Utility bills, municipal code/inspection records, housing department citations
- Certified records from hospitals, police, or schools where needed

Immigration/custody-specific
- Any immigration filings or notices that overlap with timing or credibility
- Custody-related evidence: parenting plans, custody evaluations, CPS records (if applicable)

D — How to prepare and organize exhibits and witness information
- Create an exhibit index: number each exhibit, give a one-line description, date, and source.
- Make a hearing binder (and copies for opposing counsel and the court if required): tabbed exhibits, witness list, timeline, and a one-page summary of your key facts/relief sought.
- Prepare a concise one-page timeline of major events (dates + one-line note) to hand to Amy and the court.
- Get witness declarations: short sworn statements from each witness describing facts they personally observed; include contact info and availability.
- Authentication/foundation: label originals and copies; note who can authenticate each document (author/sender, business custodian).
- Electronic evidence: save files with original metadata, export text threads as PDFs that show timestamps and sender/receiver; include screenshots only as backup.
- Make backup copies on a flash drive and cloud storage; keep originals in a safe place.

E — Tactical tips & next actions you can take now
- Prioritize the “lowest-effort, highest-impact” items: lease, payment records, key communications, photos, and a one-page timeline.
- Draft short witness declarations for people who are cooperative — have Amy review them for content/form.
- Ask Amy if you should seek subpoenas ASAP for any records (phone, bank, school) that might be slow to produce.
- Schedule a short prep session with Amy before the hearing to run through your testimony and cross-examination exposures.

If you’d like, I can:
- Turn the questions above into a short email to Amy Foster requesting a meeting or quick guidance.
- Help you build the one-page timeline or an exhibit index from the documents you have (you can list or upload what you already possess).

Which of the clarifying facts in section A can you answer now, and would you like me to draft that email to Amy or a one-page timeline template?

