Quick clarification before I dive in: you mention a landlord–tenant eviction, immigration court, and an opposing party who’s “the other parent in the custody case.” Are these three separate matters or are some of them related (for example: an eviction that interacts with custody or immigration issues)? If they’re separate, tell me which one you want to focus on first. I’ll proceed assuming you want a general, ready-to-use checklist and questions to ask Amy Foster that cover likely needs for all three (so you don’t miss anything).

Questions to ask your attorney (organized by topic)
- Case status & next steps
  - What is the current case posture? When will the next hearing be scheduled and what is its purpose (status conference, trial, preliminary hearing)?
  - Are there any upcoming deadlines I must meet (exhibit lists, witness lists, pre-hearing statements, motions)?
  - What standard of proof applies and what is the court likely to expect at the next hearing?

- Discovery & outstanding evidence
  - Is any discovery still outstanding or subject to motion (motions to compel, motions for sanctions)?
  - Did the opposing side produce everything they were supposed to? If not, should we move to compel or ask the judge to exclude that evidence?
  - Do we need any follow-up discovery (subpoenas, deposition transcripts, expert reports)?

- Evidence & admissibility
  - Which documents do you consider essential for the next hearing? Which are merely supportive?
  - Are any documents likely to be excluded as hearsay or require authentication? If so, how should we authenticate them (witness testimony, certifications, business‑records affidavit)?
  - Do any documents require certified copies or translations? Who will arrange certified translations (if needed)?

- Witnesses
  - Which witnesses do you plan to call and which are necessary for foundational testimony?
  - Should I prepare witness statements or affidavits in advance? Do we need notarized affidavits?
  - Do we need subpoenas for reluctant witnesses (landlord, property manager, school official, employer, medical provider)?

- Expert witnesses
  - Would an expert (e.g., property damages, child psychologist, immigration country‑conditions expert) strengthen our case? If yes, what’s the deadline for expert disclosures and reports?

- Strategy & motions
  - Are there pre-hearing motions we should file (motions in limine, motions to exclude evidence, summary judgment/motion to dismiss)?
  - What are our key themes and the single strongest piece of evidence we should emphasize?
  - Are settlement or alternative dispute resolution options advisable before the hearing?

- Logistics & format
  - How do you prefer I organize and deliver documents to you (binders, digital folder, PDF + exhibit index, flash drive)?
  - Do we need original documents at the hearing or are certified copies acceptable?
  - What courtroom technology is available (projector, electronic exhibits) and how should we prepare exhibits for presentation?

- Special considerations (Maricopa County / immigration court)
  - Are there local Maricopa County or immigration court rules or practices I should be aware of (exhibit deadlines, page limits, how filings must be made)?
  - For immigration court: are there country‑conditions reports, affidavits, or corroborating documents we should add? Any form documentation the court expects?
  - For custody matters: are school records, medical records, CPS records, and parenting-time logs admissible? Do we need certified records?

- Practical & administrative
  - Do you recommend I obtain any additional documentation now (bank statements, photos, repair invoices, communications)?
  - How many copies of the exhibit notebook do I need for the judge, opposing counsel, and witnesses?
  - Would you like me to prepare a one‑page chronology and evidence index summarizing the facts and where each fact is supported?

Evidence checklist (by case type)
- For eviction / landlord–tenant
  - Lease or rental agreement (signed copy)
  - Rent ledgers / payment receipts / bank statements showing payments
  - Notices (notice to pay or quit, notice of termination) and proof of service
  - Communication log (texts, emails, letters) between tenant and landlord
  - Photographs/videos of the property condition (with dates, file metadata if possible)
  - Repair invoices, estimates, and receipts
  - Police reports or incident reports, if any
  - Move-in/move-out inspection reports and keys/lock change receipts
  - Previous court filings and judgments (if any)

- For custody
  - Child’s school records, attendance, teacher notes
  - Medical records, mental health records
  - Parenting calendar / custody log (dates, times, missed handovers)
  - Communication records with the other parent (texts, emails)
  - Witness statements (family, neighbors, childcare providers)
  - Any protective orders, police reports, or CPS records
  - Evidence of the child’s living conditions and primary caregiver responsibilities

- For immigration
  - Government IDs, passports, I-94, immigration filings and receipt numbers
  - Arrest records, court dispositions, or criminal records (if applicable)
  - Affidavits from friends/family verifying residence, character, or hardship
  - Country condition documents or expert reports (if relevant)
  - Certified translations of documents not in English

How to prepare documents for your lawyer and the court
- Make a single exhibit index (number or letter each exhibit) and include a one-sentence description and date for each exhibit.
- Produce both originals and color-scanned PDFs; keep a master binder and an identical digital folder (clearly labelled).
- Prepare a one- or two-page chronology of key events (dates + short description) that cross-references exhibit numbers.
- Redact highly sensitive information not relevant to the issues (SSNs, account numbers) but keep an unredacted master with your attorney.
- For digital files, keep file names with ExhibitNumber_Description_Date (e.g., Ex3_Lease_2019-06-01.pdf).
- If you plan to use text messages, screenshot them with timestamps and, if possible, export a print-friendly version (include sender/recipient and dates).

Questions specific to discovery having just ended
- Should we seek leave to reopen discovery for anything that surfaced late (new documents, newly identified witnesses)?
- Were any discovery responses incomplete or evasive such that we should file a motion to compel or a motion to exclude?
- Do we need to subpoena third-party records (bank, phone, school) that were not produced voluntarily?
- If depositions occurred, do we need to use deposition excerpts at the hearing? Who will prepare those excerpts?

Suggested short email/message you can send Amy Foster
(You can copy/paste and adjust as needed)
- Subject: Next steps & evidence before hearing — quick questions
- Body: Hi Amy — discovery just closed and I want to make sure we’re fully prepared for the next hearing. Could we confirm the next hearing type and timeline, and please advise which documents/exhibits you view as essential? I’ve started organizing:
  1) Lease, rent payment history, notices, communications (texts/emails), photos, repair invoices;
  2) School/medical records (for custody issues);
  3) Immigration/identity records and any country‑conditions materials.
  Specifically, do you recommend any subpoenas, expert reports, certified translations, or additional discovery requests before we reach the hearing date? Also, what exhibit format does the court require in Maricopa County/immigration court, and when is our exhibit/witness list due? I can prepare a chronology and exhibit index if that helps. Thanks — I’m available to meet or call to go through details. — [Your name]

Practical next steps I can help with right now
- Draft the email to Amy (done above) or a longer checklist tailored to whichever matter you want to prioritize.
- Create a printable exhibit index template and chronology outline you can fill in.
- Make a prioritized list of missing documents based on which case you confirm is the focus.

Tell me which case to prioritize (eviction, custody, or immigration) and whether you want a ready-to-send email to Amy, an exhibit index template, or a checklist of specific documents to collect.

