I can help you get organized and make sure you ask Amy the right questions before the next hearing. A few quick clarifying questions so I tailor this better:
- Which hearing are you waiting for next — an eviction hearing, an immigration hearing, a custody hearing, or a combined/pretrial for multiple matters?
- Is Amy Foster your attorney for all of these matters (eviction, immigration, custody)?
- Do you already have an exhibit list or witness list that was exchanged during discovery?

Below is a practical plan: (1) a prioritized list of specific questions to ask Amy, (2) a checklist of evidence/documents you may need to gather, and (3) short next steps you can take right away.

1) High‑priority questions to ask your attorney (copy/paste these to Amy)
- Case status & strategy
  - Which specific hearing(s) are we preparing for next and what are the realistic goals for that hearing?
  - Based on discovery, do you consider the current record sufficient to meet our burden, or are there obvious gaps we should fill?
  - What is our overall litigation strategy going forward (settlement, mediation, full hearing/trial)?

- Evidence & exhibits
  - Which documents from discovery do you plan to use at the hearing? Is there an exhibit list I can review?
  - Are there specific documents the court typically expects in this kind of case in Maricopa County that we don’t yet have?
  - Do you recommend any certified copies or notarized affidavits (e.g., certified rent ledger, school records, medical records)?

- Witnesses & testimony
  - Which witnesses will you call and what will each witness testify about?
  - Should I (or any witnesses) prepare written declarations or affidavits in advance?
  - Do we need to subpoena any witnesses or records (landlord, employer, school, police reports)?

- Experts & specialized evidence
  - Would an expert or professional report strengthen our case (e.g., property condition inspector, mental health evaluator, custody evaluator, immigration country-conditions expert)?
  - If so, what is the deadline to retain and get the expert’s report submitted?

- Procedural & deadlines
  - Are there upcoming deadlines for exchanging exhibit lists, filing pre-hearing statements, or motions in limine?
  - What is the format of the hearing (in-person, remote)? How long will each side have for presentation?
  - Do we need to prepare and file any pre-hearing briefs, witness lists, proposed orders, or exhibit binders for the judge?

- Authentication & foundation
  - Do any of our key documents require additional foundation or authentication (e.g., business records affidavits, receipts with metadata, chain-of-custody for video or recordings)?
  - Are translations or certified translations needed for any documents?

- Immigration-specific (if immigration hearing is coming up)
  - Are there particular immigration documents/records we must present (passports, I-94, past immigration filings, evidence of continuous residence, identity documents)?
  - Does the immigration judge expect evidence regarding country conditions or hardship? Do we need expert affidavits or reports?

- Custody-specific (if custody issues are active)
  - Are there particular records we should secure (school records, medical records, CPS reports, police reports, parenting time logs)?
  - Do you recommend parenting evaluations, custody investigators, or school witness statements?

- Practical and cost/timing
  - What is the timeline you recommend for collecting additional documents and getting them to you?
  - Are there costs I should expect (e.g., fees for certified copies, expert fees, subpoena fees)?

2) Checklist of documents & evidence to consider (gather, organize, label)
Note: pick items relevant to your matters — eviction, custody, immigration.

- General / organizational
  - Chronological timeline of events (one‑page summary)
  - Master exhibit index (number each exhibit, short description, date)
  - Copies of all discovery responses and exchanged exhibits
  - Contact list for all witnesses (name, phone, email, summary of expected testimony)

- Landlord-tenant / eviction
  - Lease/rental agreement (all pages, addenda)
  - Rent ledger and receipts showing payments and dates (bank records if necessary)
  - Notice(s) served (e.g., 5/10/30-day notices) with proof of service
  - Communication logs: texts, emails, written notices between parties (exported, printed with dates)
  - Photos and videos of the property (with dates/timestamps), condition on move-in and now
  - Repair requests and landlord responses; service/maintenance invoices
  - City code/inspection reports, housing authority documents
  - Police reports related to the property or trespass/complaints
  - Utility bills, eviction filings by landlord, prior court orders

- Custody/family
  - Court orders, pleadings, and custody-related pleadings
  - School attendance and performance records
  - Medical records for child or parent relevant to custody
  - Childcare records, therapist notes, or counselor reports
  - Communication records with the other parent (texts, emails, call logs)
  - Parenting time logs (dates/times missed or denied)
  - Witness statements from family, teachers, doctors
  - Any CPS or police reports and resolutions

- Immigration
  - Passport(s), visa pages, I-94, prior immigration filings and receipts
  - Birth, marriage, or identity certificates (certified where possible)
  - Proofs of residence, employment, tax returns, pay stubs
  - Criminal records and disposition documents, if any
  - Affidavits from friends/family/community about residence, good character
  - Country conditions documentation (news articles, human-rights reports) if relevant
  - Translations and certified copies for any foreign-language docs

- Authentication/technical
  - Originals or certified copies of any official records you intend to use
  - For electronic evidence: printouts with metadata where possible, or a log showing how files were obtained
  - Signed, dated declarations from witnesses to avoid the need for live testimony when appropriate

3) How to organize and present materials to Amy
- Create a one-page timeline, then a 1-2 page summary of what you think the strongest evidence is and what you think is missing.
- Number all documents as proposed exhibits and provide a digital folder (PDFs named with exhibit numbers) and a physical binder with tabs.
- Prepare short witness summary sheets (one paragraph per witness: who they are, what they will say, estimated length of testimony).
- If you have a lot of messages/texts, export and print them in readable threads with dates. Highlight the lines you believe are important.

4) Immediate next steps you can do now
- Send Amy the timeline and a proposed exhibit index asking which exhibits she wants to prioritize.
- Start collecting or requesting certified copies for anything official (school, medical, court records) — these can take time.
- Make a short list of potential witnesses and whether they can provide a written declaration.
- Ask Amy whether she wants you to get any expert retained right away and about potential deadlines for expert disclosure.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short email to Amy with the key questions above.
- Help you build the timeline or an exhibit index from the documents you have (if you tell me what you already have).
- Create a one‑page checklist you can print and use while collecting records.

Tell me which of the above you’d like help with (drafting the email to Amy, organizing your documents, or making a timeline/exhibit index) and whether the immediate next hearing is eviction, immigration, or custody so I can tailor the email/questions exactly.

