I can help you get organized. Below is a focused checklist of questions to ask Amy Foster and a prioritized evidence checklist and practical next steps so you’re prepared for the hearing and can find out whether you need to gather anything more.

First: quick clarification (to make the checklist as accurate as possible)
- Are these all part of one case or are there multiple proceedings (eviction, immigration court matter, and a custody case) running separately? If separate, tell me which hearing you’re preparing for now.
- Do you already have a tentative hearing date or only that discovery just ended?

Questions to ask Amy Foster (use these in a call or email)
- Case scope & strategy
  - Which specific hearing are we preparing for and what is the goal at that hearing (trial, status/setting, settlement conference, removal hearing, etc.)?
  - What is our case theory/primary legal argument and what evidence is essential to prove it?
  - What burdens/standards of proof apply at this stage?
- Evidence & discovery follow-up
  - Based on discovery, is anything missing or weak that I should collect now?
  - Which documents or physical evidence do you need me to obtain, authenticate, or prepare before the hearing?
  - Do you anticipate needing expert witnesses, inspections, or third‑party records (e.g., utility companies, banks, phone records)?
  - Are there outstanding discovery requests or motions we should file now (motions to compel, subpoenas)?
- Witnesses & testimony
  - Which witnesses do we plan to call or depose? Should I line up written witness statements or declarations?
  - Do any witnesses need subpoenas or travel arrangements?
  - Should any testimony be presented by affidavit instead of live witness (if permitted)?
- Court logistics & filing requirements
  - What are the next deadlines (pretrial/exhibit lists, witness lists, motions, prehearing disclosures) in Maricopa County/immigration court for this matter?
  - Are there local rules or immigration-court-specific formatting or translation/certification rules I should follow?
  - How many copies of exhibits does the court require? Do we need electronic copies?
- Authentication & admissibility
  - Are any of our documents at risk of being excluded for lack of foundation or hearsay? If so, how can we authenticate them (custodian affidavits, business records affidavit, stipulations)?
  - Do any documents need certified translations?
- Settlement & alternative steps
  - Should we consider settlement or alternative dispute resolution before the hearing? What are the pros/cons in our case?
- Practical & tactical
  - Do you want me to prepare a chronological timeline or exhibit index for you?
  - Is there anything I should avoid doing (e.g., posting on social media, contacting opposing party) while the case is pending?
  - How should I preserve physical evidence? How should I back up digital evidence?

Prioritized evidence checklist (what to gather now, categorized)
Essential (get these first)
- Lease/rental agreement, terms, and any written amendments
- Rent payment records: receipts, cancelled checks, bank statements, Venmo/PayPal records
- Notices and correspondence: notices to pay/quit, eviction notices, 3-day/5-day/10-day notices, cure notices
- Written communications with landlord, tenant, or opposing party: texts, emails, letters (include dates and participants)
- Court and discovery documents already produced (copies of what was exchanged in discovery)
- Service of process / proof of service documents
- Photos/videos of the property, damages, repairs, or hazardous conditions (include date/time, context)
- Repair requests and repair invoices/estimates
- Move-in/move-out condition reports, inspection reports
- Any police reports or 911 calls related to the premises or parties
- Witness contact info and brief statements (neighbors, maintenance staff, tenants)
Important / strengthens case
- Utility bills or meter reads showing occupancy or nonpayment
- Security deposit accounting and receipts
- Prior eviction notices or prior judgments related to the property
- Building-code or health/safety violation notices from local authorities
- Email/text logs that show attempts to resolve issues
- Bank statements or payroll documentation to support claims about financial circumstances (if relevant)
Immigration- or custody-related evidence (if relevant to your hearing)
- Any immigration filings or notices of hearing relevant to the same parties
- Birth certificates, custody orders, or child-related evidence if the opposing party is a parent and custody issues are connected
- Proof of identity and immigration status documents (certified copies)
- Translated/certified copies for any non-English documents
Forensic / authenticity documentation
- Business-record affidavits or custodian declarations for records (rent ledger, repair invoices)
- Metadata or original files for digital evidence (preserve originals)
- Chain-of-custody notes for any physical items
Optional but useful
- A one-page chronological timeline highlighting key events with dates and short descriptions
- Pre-prepared exhibit list (label exhibits, brief description)
- Short witness declarations/affidavits for non-appearers

How to organize what you have (so Amy can review quickly)
- Create a single PDF folder (or clearly labeled file folder) with:
  - Cover sheet: case name, hearing date (if known), and your contact
  - Table of contents / exhibit index with Bates numbers or file names
  - Chronological timeline (1 page)
  - Documents grouped and labeled (Lease, Payments, Notices, Communications, Photos, Reports, Witness statements)
- Keep originals safe (do not throw away) and keep backups of digital files in at least two places (cloud + local).
- If you have many photos/videos, name them by date and short description (YYYYMMDD_description).

Preservation & conduct tips
- Do not delete any communications relevant to the case.
- Avoid direct contact with opposing party unless your lawyer advises otherwise.
- Freeze social-media posts about the case and save screenshots (with timestamps).
- Get certified translations for any non-English documents now — they can take time.

Questions about next steps you should confirm with Amy
- Do we need to file any pre-hearing motions or exhibits by a specific deadline?
- Should I prepare witness affidavits now or wait to see if the hearing will be evidentiary?
- Do you want me to attempt to obtain any third-party records (phone logs, utility records) or should you handle subpoenas?

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a concise email you can send to Amy with the above questions and a summary of evidence you already have.
- Help you build the one-page timeline and exhibit index if you upload or summarize key dates/documents.

Do you want me to draft that email to Amy Foster for you now? If yes, tell me which documents you already have so I can include a short inventory.

