Thanks — I can help you get organized. Quick clarification before I go further: are these three separate matters (1) a landlord–tenant eviction, (2) an immigration court matter, and (3) a custody dispute — or are some of those details describing the same single case? That will change exactly what evidence to gather. If they’re separate, tell me which one you want to prioritize first.

Below is a ready-to-use set of questions to ask your attorney Amy Foster, plus a prioritized evidence checklist and concrete next steps you can take before the next hearing date.

Suggested questions to ask Amy (copy/paste-friendly)
- Case status & next steps
  - Can you confirm exactly which case(s) you are handling for me and what the next court date or milestone will be?
  - Has discovery officially closed? Are there any outstanding discovery issues we should still pursue?
  - What type of hearing is next (status, pretrial, merits, settlement conference)? What will the court expect from me at that hearing?

- Evidence & what to gather
  - Based on what we already have, what additional evidence do you think the court will need or expect to see at the next hearing?
  - Are there specific documents I should prioritize getting now (e.g., leases, rent ledgers, receipts, medical records, school records, police reports, certified translations)?
  - Do you recommend obtaining witness statements or sworn declarations now? If so, who should I have sign them and what should they include?

- Authentication & admissibility
  - What is the best way to authenticate business records (bank, rent payments, utilities)? Do we need subpoenas or certified copies?
  - Are there hearsay concerns with any of my documents (text messages, social media messages, informal letters)? How should we address those?

- Witnesses & experts
  - Will we need to call witnesses or an expert? If so, what kind (property inspector, pediatrician/therapist, immigration expert, custody evaluator)?
  - If I need witnesses, when do we have to disclose them and do you want written declarations first?

- Motions & tactical items
  - Should we file any pre-hearing motions (motion in limine, motion to compel, protective order, motion to continue)?
  - Are we likely to need subpoenas for phone records, bank statements, or school/medical records? Can you file them, or should I request them now?

- Logistics & deadlines
  - What are all the upcoming filing deadlines and serve-by dates I should calendar now?
  - How do you want documents delivered to you (digital folder, PDFs, originals)? Do you want everything Bates-numbered or indexed?

- Settlement/alternative resolution
  - Are settlement or mediation options appropriate now? If so, what documents should I prepare for settlement discussions?

Prioritized evidence checklist (general — tailor to which case this is)
1. Foundational documents (start here for any case)
   - Government IDs, any immigration documents (if relevant)
   - Names, contact information for all parties and potential witnesses
   - Chronological timeline of events (one-pager with dates and short notes)
   - All correspondence with the opposing party and third parties (emails, texts, certified mail receipts)

2. If this is the landlord–tenant eviction matter
   - Lease or rental agreement (signed pages)
   - Rent payment records (bank statements, receipts, money orders, ledgers)
   - Notices served (eviction notice, 3-day/30-day notices) with proof of service
   - Photos/videos of property condition (with dates) and inspection reports
   - Maintenance requests and landlord responses (texts, emails, repair receipts)
   - Move-in/move-out inspection reports and security deposit documentation
   - Witness contact info (neighbors, property manager) and written statements

3. If there is an immigration court proceeding
   - All immigration filings and receipts (Form numbers, counsel filings)
   - Biographical documents and identity documents
   - Documents supporting any claimed relief (asylum, cancellation, adjustment)
   - Country conditions evidence (reports, news articles) and expert affidavits if relevant
   - Arrest records, convictions, and certified court dispositions (if any)

4. If this involves custody (opposing party = other parent)
   - Child’s school records, attendance, grades, teacher statements
   - Medical records (vaccinations, pediatric visits, psychological reports)
   - Police reports, CPS involvement records (if any)
   - Parenting time logs (dates/times of visitation or missed visits)
   - Communications about custody/parenting (texts, emails) showing requests, denials, threats
   - Any professional evaluations (therapy notes, custody evaluator reports)

5. Records that often require formal processes
   - Bank records, phone records, text logs (consider subpoenas or certified copies)
   - Employment records, paystubs (if relevant to ability to pay or support)
   - Certified translations for any non-English records

How to prepare and organize materials for your attorney
- Create a one-page timeline with the most important dates (incidents, notices, discovery deadlines).
- Make a folder structure (digital and/or physical): Pleadings, Discovery Responses, Communications, Financials, Photos, Witnesses.
- Convert documents to searchable PDFs and name files with dates and a short label (YYYYMMDD_description).
- Prepare a short statement (1–2 paragraphs) describing what you expect the court to learn from the evidence.
- Make a short list of desired outcomes and fallback options (what you want vs. what you’ll accept).

Questions to ask about evidence admissibility & format
- Does the court require exhibits to be pre-marked and exchanged before the hearing? If so, by when?
- Are there local Maricopa County rules or immigration-court procedures I should follow for exhibit submission?
- Do you need original documents or are certified copies acceptable?
- For digital photos/videos, what format and metadata should we preserve to show date/time?

Practical next steps for you (recommended, in order)
1. Clarify for me whether this is one combined case or multiple distinct ones (eviction, immigration, custody).
2. Make the one-page timeline and the folder (digital PDFs).
3. Prioritize getting certified records that take time (court dispositions, school records, bank statements).
4. Identify any witnesses and ask them to draft short signed declarations now (date, place, what they saw).
5. Send Amy the timeline and your prioritized evidence list and ask the specific questions above. If she recommends subpoenas or expert reports, start that process immediately (it can take weeks).

Offer to help
- I can draft a short email to Amy that includes the timeline and the key questions above (I can format it so you can copy/paste).
- Or I can make you a printable checklist and sample witness declaration template.

Which would you like me to do next — draft the email to Amy, make a one-page timeline template for you to fill in, or focus on preparing evidence for one specific case (eviction, immigration, or custody)?

