I can help — this is a good time to get a clear, organized set of questions so your attorney can tell you what else (if anything) you should gather before the hearing. Quick clarification before I make the list tailored to your situation: you mentioned eviction, immigration court, and a custody dispute. Are these three separate matters, or is one the primary case? The kinds of evidence and procedure differ a lot by court (Maricopa County eviction/custody vs. EOIR immigration court). If you tell me which case you mean, I’ll tailor the list. Meanwhile, below is a comprehensive set of questions and an evidence checklist you can use or adapt.

Suggested questions to ask Amy Foster (grouped by topic)

Case overview & strategy
- Based on discovery, what is our strongest evidence and our weakest points?
- What legal standard will the judge apply at the hearing (burden of proof)?
- What is our immediate objective at the next hearing (trial, interim order, scheduling, settlement)?
- Are there any motions you recommend filing before the hearing?

Evidence & documents
- Which documents do you consider essential for the hearing? Which are merely helpful?
- Do you want originals, certified copies, or are scanned copies acceptable?
- Do any documents require notarization or certified translation (and who will pay for translations)?
- Are there specific records you think we should subpoena now (e.g., bank statements, rental ledgers, medical or school records, immigration documents)?
- Are there documents we should request from the opposing party that weren’t provided in discovery?

Authentication & admissibility
- Do you foresee any admissibility problems with these items (hearsay, lack of foundation, late disclosure)?
- Should we prepare affidavits/declarations to authenticate documents or to provide foundation for hearsay exceptions?
- Do we need chain-of-custody documentation (for e.g., surveillance video, evidence of repairs, or drug/medical tests)?

Witnesses & testimony
- Which witnesses do you plan to call and why?
- Do you want me to identify or prepare any witnesses (family, neighbors, employers, teachers, caretakers, repair professionals)?
- Should we get witness statements or sworn affidavits in advance?
- Will any witnesses need subpoenas to compel attendance?
- How should witnesses prepare (what questions should they expect)?

Experts & independent evaluations
- Would an expert report be useful (e.g., property condition/repairs, parenting evaluator, mental-health expert, forensic accountant, country conditions or medical expert for immigration)?
- If so, can we get an expert in time and what will that cost?

Timeline & pre-hearing requirements
- What are the deadlines for exchanging exhibit lists and witness lists in this court?
- Are there pre-hearing briefs, proposed orders, or pretrial statements we must file?
- Do you expect any pre-hearing conferences or settlement conferences?
- When will we receive the hearing date and how long is the hearing likely to last?

Logistics for the hearing
- Will the hearing be in-person or remote? If remote, what platform and technical requirements?
- Are there local Maricopa County or EOIR-specific rules we must follow (exhibit format, number of copies, labeling, electronic filing)?
- Where should I bring originals vs. copies? Do you want binders or PDFs for the judge and opposing counsel?

Risks, defenses & likely objections
- What objections do you expect the other side to raise to our evidence?
- Are there any issues that could surprise us at the hearing (prior records, criminal history, immigration paperwork)?
- What are likely outcomes and fallback options if the judge rules against us?

Preservation & additional discovery
- Should we file any motions to preserve evidence or prevent spoliation?
- Are there outstanding discovery items we can still request or move to compel?
- Do you recommend depositions or further written discovery?

Settlement & negotiation
- Is settlement realistic or advisable at this stage? What would acceptable terms look like?
- If settlement talks are useful, do you want me to collect specific documents or evidence to strengthen our negotiation position?

Costs & fees
- Do any additional evidence steps (experts, subpoenas, translations) have cost estimates?
- What is the cost/benefit of obtaining extra evidence vs. relying on what we already have?

Checklist — potential evidence to gather (tailor to your case type)

General documentation
- Copies of pleadings, discovery responses, notices, and orders
- Chronological timeline of events (dates, times, places) with supporting documents
- All communications between parties (emails, texts, DMs, letters) with dates and sender/recipient
- Photographs and videos (with dates/timestamps if possible)
- Receipts, invoices, payment records, rent ledgers, bank statements
- Lease documents, notices to vacate, repair requests, work orders (for eviction)
- Police reports, 911 calls, protective orders, hospital/ER records (if relevant)
- School records, medical records, therapy notes related to the child (if custody)
- Immigration documents, passports, I-94, I-797, prior filings, certified translations (if immigration)

Witness-related
- Contact info for witnesses, short summary of what each will testify to
- Written witness statements/affidavits summarizing their expected testimony
- Employment verification letters, landlord references, character references

Forensic / expert
- Inspection reports (property condition)
- Parenting evaluations or home study reports
- Toxicology or substance-use testing records
- Medical or psychological expert reports
- For immigration: country condition reports, corroborating affidavits about persecution or hardship

Authentication and foundation
- Metadata for digital files when possible (emails with headers, photo metadata)
- Notarized affidavits to explain how records were obtained and why they are reliable
- Chain-of-custody logs for physical evidence

Practical hearing preparation
- Exhibit list with brief description and exhibit numbers
- Copies of exhibits: court copies, one for opposing counsel, one for you, and one for your attorney
- A succinct one-page factual timeline for counsel and/or judge
- Any demonstrative exhibits or maps/diagrams you’ll use in testimony

Questions about jurisdiction and venue (ask if unsure)
- Why is this matter in immigration court (if you have removal/immigration issues) versus state court?
- Are there overlapping issues (e.g., custody and immigration intersecting)? If so, what’s the plan to coordinate?

How to prioritize what to gather
- Ask Amy: which 3–5 pieces of evidence would most improve our chance at the hearing?
- Focus first on documents that directly prove elements the judge must decide (e.g., proof of service, lease, child’s best-interests factors, immigration eligibility criteria).
- Then gather corroborating material (communications, receipts, photos).
- Only pursue experts if they will materially change the outcome.

Template starter questions you can email Amy (I can draft this into an email draft for you)
- “Can you list the evidence you believe is essential for the upcoming hearing?”
- “Are there any outstanding discovery items or subpoenas we should pursue now?”
- “Do you foresee admissibility issues with X, Y, Z (list the specific documents you have)?”
- “Do you recommend any expert evaluations or witness affidavits before the hearing?”
- “What are the filing deadlines for exhibit/witness lists in this court (and for Maricopa County specifically, or EOIR if it’s immigration)?”
- “Can you outline the hearing logistics (in-person/remote, expected duration, what I should bring)?”
- “What are the likely outcomes and our fallback plan?”

If you want, I can:
- Tailor the list specifically for eviction / custody / or immigration court once you tell me which case you mean.
- Draft a concise email to Amy with these questions and your current evidence list so you can send it quickly.

