- Are there any immigration-specific documents I should gather if the immigration judge will consider housing/custody issues (e.g., rental history, eviction notices, proof of custody arrangements, communication showing care of child)?  
- What is our estimated timeline to trial/hearing and likely outcomes so I can plan steps and gather evidence accordingly?

Document and evidence checklist to confirm or gather now
- Lease or rental agreement (all pages, any amendments).  
- Rent payment records: receipts, bank transfers, money order copies, canceled checks.  
- Notices and communications: eviction notices, cure notices, demand letters, email and text threads (exported and saved with timestamps).  
- Repair and habitability records: work orders, invoices, photos/videos of conditions (with date/time), messages to landlord/property manager, contractor estimates/receipts.  
- Move-in/move-out condition reports, inspection reports.  
- Correspondence with opposing party relevant to custody or tenancy (emails, texts, social media messages).  
- Police reports, 911 calls, or incident reports if any incidents occurred on the property.  
- Photographs and videos: clearly labeled with dates, times, and brief descriptions. Create a master index.  
- Witness contact info and short written statements (friends, neighbors, repair people, property managers) describing what they saw/heard. Ask witnesses to include dates/times and their relationship to the events.  
- Financial records relevant to ability to pay (pay stubs, bank statements) if rent/payment ability is contested.  
- Child custody evidence (if relevant): parenting schedules, school records, pediatric/medical records, child welfare agency documents, proof of who provides primary care.  
- Immigration documents (if immigration court will consider facts from eviction/custody): I-94, receipts, notices, any filings, and any documents that could bear on credibility or discretionary relief.  
- Any prior court orders, settlements, or stipulations between the parties.  
- Expert reports or contact info for potential experts (inspection, valuation, forensic accountant, custody evaluator).  
- Documentation of attempts to resolve the issue (mediation emails, payment plans, offers).

Practical preparation items you should ask Amy about
- Exhibit preparation: how to label exhibits, whether to provide copies to the court/opposing counsel beforehand, and whether exhibits must be exchanged a set number of days before hearing.  
- Witness list & subpoenas: which witnesses we need present, how to subpoena them (deadline and service), and whether remote testimony is allowed.  
- Pretrial filings: whether we should file pretrial statements, witness lists, exhibit lists, or motions in limine. Deadlines and local rules.  
- Authentication: how to authenticate bank records, text messages, photos, or business records in court. Ask if a declaration from a custodian of records or a witness will be required.  
- Hearsay: which of your documents might be hearsay and whether exceptions apply or whether we need to secure live witnesses.  
- Evidentiary strategy: which facts are most important to prove and which pieces of evidence are critical vs. cumulative (so you can prioritize collecting the important items).  
- Preservation: how to preserve electronic messages and files; should you take screenshots, request export files, or request a forensic copy?  
- Logistics: how long the hearing will be, courtroom location (Maricopa County division), and whether any COVID-era or remote procedures are in effect.

How you can organize what you already have (recommended, quick steps)
