Thanks — I can help you get organized. A quick clarification first so my suggestions are focused and useful:

- Is this a single case or multiple cases? You mentioned landlord–tenant eviction, immigration court, and a custody dispute. Which of these are actually part of the matter Amy Foster is handling now?  
- Do you have a hearing date yet or just waiting for the court to set it?  
- What documentation do you already have (list the main items you’ve collected: lease, notices, photos, communications, bank records, declarations, prior orders, immigration or custody paperwork, etc.)?

While you answer, here’s a practical, attorney-ready checklist and a set of pointed questions you can ask Amy Foster so you know whether you need more evidence before the next date.

Top questions to ask your attorney
- Which legal issues will the judge decide at the next hearing (jurisdiction, summary judgment, merits, temporary orders, procedural scheduling)?  
- Based on discovery results, what evidence is still weak or missing for the claims/defenses you intend to prove?  
- Are any of my documents inadmissible or likely to be challenged (hearsay, authentication, relevance)? If so, how can we cure that?  
- Which specific documents or witness testimony do you recommend obtaining before the hearing? Prioritize must-have vs. nice-to-have.  
- Do we need written witness statements/declarations or live witnesses? Who are the highest-value witnesses?  
- Do we need expert testimony (repairs, habitability, child welfare, valuation, translation, mental health, immigration expert)? If so, when must expert reports be served?  
- Can you—or should we—subpoena documents or witnesses (rental records, bank statements, employer records, school records, phone records)? What’s the timeline for issuing subpoenas in Maricopa County?  
- Are there motions we should file now (motions in limine, to exclude evidence, for summary judgment, to compel, to continue)? What are the deadlines?  
- Are any documents that are in another language requiring certified translation or authentication?  
- Do we need certified copies of court orders, prior judgments, or immigration documents?  
- How should I organize and label exhibits for the court and opposing counsel (numbering, electronic vs. hard copies)? How many copies and format required by the court?  
- Do we need a hearing brief, exhibit list, witness list, or pre-hearing statement? When are those due?  
- What are the settlement/mediation options and timing—should we pursue those now or prepare for trial?  
- What should I do right now to preserve evidence and prevent spoliation? (e.g., preserve devices, back up emails/texts, don’t delete messages)  
- What will you (Amy) handle and what do you need me to collect or do?

Evidence checklist — items commonly needed (tailor to the actual case)
If this is a landlord–tenant eviction:
- Lease or rental agreement (signed pages).  
- Rent payment records: rent ledgers, bank transfers, cash receipts, canceled checks.  
- Notices and emails/texts about unpaid rent, repair requests, termination or 5/10/30-day notices.  
- Photographs/videos showing property condition and timestamps (repair issues or evidence of damage).  
- Repair requests and landlord responses; maintenance invoices/estimates.  
- Move-in / move-out condition reports and inspection checklists.  
- Communications with the other party and any witnesses.  
- Witness contact info (neighbors, contractors, property managers).  
- Any police reports or code enforcement citations.  
- Utility bills or proof of who paid utilities (if relevant).  
- Prior court orders/judgments involving the property.

If custody is relevant:
- Child’s school records, attendance, grades, teacher statements.  
- Medical records for the child (appointments, diagnoses, prescriptions).  
- Parenting time logs (dates/times visits occurred or were missed).  
- Communications between parents about custody/time/exchange.  
- Any third-party observations (family, childcare providers).  
- Reports from social services, CPS, or police (if any).  
- Evidence of the other parent’s behavior impacting the child (substance use, arrests, housing instability).  
- Documentation of your caregiving responsibilities and routines.

If immigration issues are involved:
- Immigration filings, receipts, case numbers, notices from immigration court/USCIS/EOIR.  
- Permanent resident/visa/work authorization documentation.  
- Any criminal/court records (certified).  
- Affidavits supporting ties, hardship, or credibility.  
- Expert declarations on immigration consequences (if necessary).

Document authentication and admissibility
- Certified copies: for official records (court orders, birth certificates, immigration docs) get certified copies.  
- Business records: get metadata or an affidavit from the custodian of records if relying on things like rent ledgers or phone records.  
- Emails/texts: preserve originals/screenshots with timestamps; consider exporting message threads as PDFs. Note how you will authenticate them.  
- Chain of custody: if evidence might be questioned (photos, video, seized items), note who handled them and when.

Practical organization steps before the next date
- Create an indexed exhibit binder (or PDF exhibit file): Exhibit 1 = Lease, Exhibit 2 = Notice to Quit, etc. Include a one-page timeline at the front.  
- Prepare a concise timeline of key events (dates, short descriptions, supporting exhibits). Judges and attorneys rely on timelines.  
- Draft short declarations for each witness you plan to rely on; have them sign and notarize if needed.  
- Back up all electronic evidence in at least two places and preserve original devices. Document preservation steps in writing.  
- Label contact info for each witness and note whether you expect them to testify or sign a declaration.  
- Confirm court filing requirements for exhibits/witness lists in Maricopa County (format, number of copies, e-filing rules).

Questions about strategy and timing
- What is the realistic burden of proof at the hearing and which facts must we prove?  
- Are there credibility issues we should prepare for (adverse prior statements, inconsistent testimony)?  
- Do we need to prepare cross-examination points for opposing witnesses?  
- If discovery is closed, can we still seek limited reopening to obtain crucial evidence? What is the process?  
- If evidence is time-sensitive (repairs, deterioration, witnesses moving), should we move faster to secure it?

Preservation and urgency
- Ask the attorney to send a litigation hold/preservation letter to opposing parties if relevant.  
- Identify any upcoming deadlines (subpoena returns, expert disclosures, motions cutoffs) and build a checklist with dates.

Offer to help draft communications
- I can draft a short, professional email to Amy listing these questions and attaching your exhibit index/timeline so you can send it. Would you like me to draft that now? If yes, tell me which documents you already have and whether you want a short or detailed email.

If you confirm which case(s) are active (eviction vs. custody vs. immigration), I’ll tailor the checklist and suggested evidence/actions specifically to that proceeding and can draft the email or a one-page hearing-prep summary you can bring to your next meeting. Which would you like next?

