Top questions to ask your attorney
- Can you confirm which legal issues will be addressed at the next hearing (eviction, immigration status, custody issues)?  
- What is the legal standard the court will apply at the hearing, and what elements of my case do I need to prove?  
- Based on the discovery materials we already have, what are the strongest pieces of evidence and what are the weakest/missing pieces?  
- Do you expect the opposing party to raise certain facts or evidence we should rebut? If so, what specific evidence would be most effective to counter them?  
- Are there any additional documents or records the court commonly requests in our type of hearing? (e.g., certified records, rental ledgers, declarations, custody-related records)  
- Should we prepare witness declarations or live witnesses? If declarations, what should they contain and do they need to be notarized?  
- Are expert witnesses likely to be necessary or helpful? If yes, what type and what deadlines apply for expert disclosures?  
- Do we need certified or original copies of particular documents (rent ledgers, service records, immigration documents, birth certificates, school records)?  
- Do any documents require translation or certification for use in court?  
- Are there local filing or exhibit submission deadlines (Maricopa County rules or immigration court deadlines) we must meet before the hearing?  
- Should we pre-mark exhibits and exchange an exhibit list with opposing counsel/court? If so, what format does the court prefer?  
- Do you recommend any motions before the hearing (motions in limine, to exclude evidence, or to compel)?  
- Any evidence preservation steps I should take right now (save text logs, back up files, request records from third parties, send preservation letters)?  
- Do you want me to subpoena particular documents or witnesses (for example, landlord records, school records, employment records)?  
- What will our courtroom presentation look like — a short summary of how the evidence will be introduced and demonstrated?

Evidence and documents to gather (prioritized)
General / foundational
- Court filings and discovery produced by both sides (complaint, answers, discovery responses)  
- Certified court docket and case numbers for each related matter  
- Chronological timeline of events (dates, times, short descriptions) — one-page timeline for the attorney/court

Landlord–tenant eviction (if this is one of the issues)
- Lease or rental agreement (signed) and any amendments  
- Rent payment records (bank statements, receipts, MoneyGram, Venmo, Zelle) showing payments and dates  
- Notices served and proof of service (eviction notice, 3-day/5-day notices) with dates and method of service  
- Photo/video documentation of property condition, repairs, or alleged lease violations (with timestamps if possible)  
- Communication records with landlord/tenant (emails, texts, letters) — include dates and context  
- Repair requests, maintenance logs, landlord responses  
- Move-in/move-out inventories or condition reports  
- Witness statements (neighbors, property manager) supporting your version

Immigration-related evidence (if relevant to upcoming proceedings)
- Copies of immigration documents (A-files, I-130/I-485 receipts, Notices to Appear) — certified if available  
- Passport, visa, entry stamps, and travel history documents  
- Any criminal/court records, arrest reports, or dispositions (certified)  
- Evidence of continuous residence or work authorization if that's relevant to relief you’re seeking  
- Translations and certified copies of foreign-language documents

Custody-related evidence (if the opposing party is the other parent)
- Child’s birth certificate, school records, daycare/school attendance and performance records  
- Medical records for the child (immunizations, treatment notes) that support fitness or concerns  
- Evidence of parenting time and routines (calendars, photos with dates, messages arranging visits)  
- Any police reports, protective orders, or allegations of domestic violence or substance abuse (certified copies)  
- Witness statements from teachers, pediatricians, or others who observe the child’s welfare  
- Documented concerns about child welfare (emails to DHHS/child protective services and their responses)

Preparing evidence so it’s admissible and persuasive
- Get certified copies when courts expect official records (birth certificates, criminal records, school transcripts).  
- Translate foreign-language documents with certified translators.  
- Create short, labeled exhibit packets (each exhibit numbered, with a one-line description). Ask Amy if the court requires a specific exhibit format.  
- Prepare witness declarations that follow the required format (signed, dated, and if necessary notarized) summarizing what the witness saw/heard and why it matters.  
- For electronic records, preserve metadata where possible (screenshots with timestamps, original emails with headers).  
- Consider using a one-page summary (timeline/facts) for the judge to follow.

Strategic and procedural items to ask about
- Do we need to file any pre-hearing briefs, exhibit lists, or witness lists? What are the local deadlines?  
- Should we try to stipulate to uncontested facts to streamline the hearing?  
- Is settlement/mediation appropriate before the hearing? What are the pros/cons in our situation?  
- If I collect new evidence, how should I deliver it to you (format, organization, deadline)?  
- Are there any privacy or safety concerns (e.g., sensitive records, protective orders) we should address with the court?

