While you confirm, here’s a focused, practical set of questions and tasks you can use with your attorney to make sure you have the evidence you’ll need before the next court date. I’ve grouped them so you can pick those relevant to whichever case(s) apply.

A. Core questions to ask your attorney (use these in a meeting or email)
1. Case status & timeline
   - What is the current status (e.g., discovery closed, waiting for hearing date)? What deadlines are coming up?
   - When will the hearing be, and how long should we expect it to last?
2. Burden and standard of proof
   - What is the legal standard we must meet at the hearing (preponderance, clear and convincing, probable cause, etc.)?
   - Which facts are most important for the judge to find in our favor?
3. Evidence & admissibility
   - Which documents will be admissible and which might be objectionable?
   - Do we need originals, certified copies, or will photocopies/scans be acceptable?
   - Do any documents require authentication or a sponsoring witness?
   - Are there hearsay concerns, and can hearsay be admitted under an exception?
4. Missing or recommended evidence
   - Is there evidence we don’t yet have but should obtain (receipts, contracts, communications, photos, repair records, police reports, school or medical records)?
   - Should we obtain expert reports or declarations (e.g., property damage appraisal, mental health or drug evaluation, custody evaluator)?
5. Witnesses and testimony
   - Which witnesses will the attorney call, and what will they testify to?
   - Should I obtain written declarations/affidavits from witnesses now?
   - Do we need subpoenas for witnesses or records (phone, bank, landlord, employer, schools, medical)?
6. Pre-hearing motions & strategy
   - Are any pre-hearing motions advisable (motions in limine, to exclude evidence, to compel, for summary judgment)?
   - Do you recommend settlement/mediation before the hearing?
7. Logistics and preparation
   - What should I bring to the hearing (copies of exhibits, index, timeline of events)?
   - Will we use demonstratives or a hearing binder? How many copies should I prepare for the judge/opposing counsel?
   - Will an interpreter be needed or available?
8. Next steps if we need more time or evidence
   - If we find additional evidence just before the hearing, what’s the process to submit it? Can we get a continuance?
   - If opposing party produces new evidence, what are our options?
9. Costs and timelines for getting additional evidence
   - What are estimated costs and time to obtain expert reports, subpoenas, certified records, etc.?
10. Questions specific to court and local practice
   - Are there Maricopa County local rules or specific judge preferences we should follow (formatting, exhibit labeling, filing procedures)?
   - Are filings or exhibits due in advance? If so, when and how should they be filed and served?

B. Evidence checklist by possible case type
(Collect those that apply to you.)

1) Landlord-tenant / eviction
   - Lease or rental agreement (signed)
   - Notices (eviction/3‑day/5‑day/30‑day) with proof of service
   - Rent payment history (receipts, bank/transaction records)
   - Communication logs (texts, emails, letters) about rent/repairs/notice
   - Photos/videos of the property condition, dated and geotagged if possible
   - Repair requests and invoices, contractor estimates
   - Move-in/move-out condition reports, security deposit records
   - Police reports or nuisance complaints (if relevant)
   - Witness statements (neighbors, property managers)
   - Any housing code or inspection reports
   - Utility bills or notices showing occupancy

2) Custody / family
   - Current custody/parenting orders and any related filings
   - Communication records between parents (texts, emails)
   - School records, attendance records, report cards
   - Medical records for child(ren) showing care, injuries, diagnoses
   - CPS or child welfare agency reports, police reports
   - Evidence of living situation (photos, lease, utility bills)
   - Work schedules, travel records that affect parenting time
   - Witness statements (teachers, doctors, babysitters, relatives)
   - Evidence of substance abuse or treatment (drug tests, rehab records)
   - Any domestic violence or protection orders

3) Immigration proceedings
   - Notice to Appear and all EOIR/court documents
   - Passports, IDs, entry documents, immigration filings (I-589, I-130, I-485, etc.)
   - Evidence supporting relief sought (e.g., asylum country condition reports, certificates, affidavits)
   - Criminal records or police reports (if any)
   - Family documents linking relationships (birth certificates, marriage certificates)
   - Medical and mental health records if relevant to claims
   - Proof of continuous presence, employment, community ties
   - Translations and certified copies of foreign records

C. Practical preparation & organization
- Create a timeline/chronology of events (one page) — judges like concise timelines.
- Create an exhibit list with numbered exhibit stickers (Exhibit 1, 2, 3). Include a short description for each.
- Prepare a hearing binder: judge’s copy, opposing counsel copy, your copy, and many copies for witnesses (paper + PDFs).
- Keep originals safe; give your lawyer originals and bring copies unless told otherwise.
- Get witness contact info and short written statements now; confirm they can attend.
- Preserve evidence: don’t delete texts/emails; take screen captures and back them up.
- Consider sworn affidavits from witnesses if they may be unavailable at hearing.
- If evidence is in possession of a third party (landlord company, bank, school), ask your attorney about subpoenas now — they take time.

D. Questions about admissibility and foundation to ask Amy
- How do we authenticate these documents? Do we need witness testimony to lay foundation?
- For digital evidence (texts, emails), what form of authentication does the court accept?
- Will hearsay objections be an issue for certain records, and are there exceptions we can use?
- Should we obtain certified records (police reports, hospital records) rather than copies?

E. If you want, I can draft an email to Amy Foster for you
I can draft a concise email listing the key questions above and asking for her guidance on what evidence to collect and deadlines to meet. If you want that, tell me:
- Which case(s) this applies to (eviction, custody, immigration — or more than one)
- Which items from the checklist you already have
- Any deadline or expected hearing date (if known)
Then I’ll draft a professional email you can send.

Finally: confirm which case(s) and any upcoming dates, and I’ll tailor the checklist and the draft email to your exact situation.

