Questions to ask your attorney (high priority)
- Which specific factual issues will the court decide at the next hearing? (i.e., nonpayment, unlawful detainer grounds, credibility, jurisdictional facts, custody parenting-time issues, immigration relief eligibility)  
- Based on those issues, what evidence do you currently lack? Which gaps are most important to fill before the hearing?  
- Of the documents we already have from discovery, is anything weak or likely to be excluded for foundation/authentication reasons?  
- Do we need witness testimony (live or declarations)? If so: who, what will they testify to, and do we need to draft/prepare affidavits now?  
- Should we retain any experts (property condition, habitability, parenting evaluator, immigration country-conditions expert, forensic accountant)? If yes, by when?  
- Are there outstanding discovery items (responses, requests, subpoenas) we should pursue or motions to compel?  
- Are there any pre-hearing filings (exhibit lists, witness lists, pretrial statements, motions in limine, trial brief) required and what are the deadlines?  
- What admissibility issues do you expect (hearsay, authentication, prior bad acts) and how should we address them?  
- Do you recommend filing any motions now (summary judgment, continuance, dismissal, protective order)? What are the pros/cons?  
- What settlement/mediation options should we prepare for and what evidence will we need for settlement discussions?  
- What is our timeline once the hearing date is set (final disclosures, exhibit exchange, witness subpoenas, witness prep)?  
- Are there any strategic steps I should take now to preserve or strengthen evidence (preservation letters, secure devices, obtain copies of records)?

Concrete evidence/document checklist (start collecting & organizing)
General (applies across cases)
- Chronology/timeline of events (one-page timeline with dates and short descriptions)  
- All written communications: emails, texts, direct messages, certified mail receipts, notices — export and preserve originals/screenshots with timestamps  
- Names/contact info for all witnesses and short summary of what each can say  
- Photos/videos with dates (maintain original files and just one working copy)  
- Any police reports, 911 calls, or incident reports  
- Proof of service or receipt of any legal notices

Eviction / landlord-tenant (typical documents)
- Lease/rental agreement and any amendments or addenda  
- Rent ledger showing payments, bank statements, receipts, canceled checks, receipts of money transfers  
- Notices to pay or quit, notices to vacate, eviction notice and proof of delivery/service  
- Maintenance/repair requests and landlord responses, code violation reports, city inspection reports  
- Move-in and move-out condition reports, photos of unit condition with dates  
- Security deposit ledger and documentation of any deductions  
- Communications showing landlord’s statements about termination or reason for eviction  
- Any local housing agency complaints or communications

Immigration court (if this is a connected matter)
- I-94, passports, immigration forms, notices to appear, prior immigration filings and decisions  
- Proof of identity and relationship documents (birth certificates, marriage certificate, etc.)  
- Criminal records or dispositions (if any), police reports, or certificates of rehabilitation  
- Country conditions or expert reports if relevant to relief sought  
- Affidavits supporting claims (e.g., hardship, fear of persecution)

Custody/family (if the opposing party is the other parent)
- Existing custody orders, parenting-time schedules, prior filings in family court  
- School records, attendance logs, medical records, therapy notes relevant to parenting or child welfare  
- Texts/emails showing parenting behavior, missed visits, threats, or cooperation  
- Witness statements about parenting, living situation, child’s statements (careful with age/competency rules)  
- Financial records related to support, household expenses, housing stability

Evidence quality and admissibility tasks
- For each document: note source, date, and who can authenticate it (witness who received/sent it, custodian of records)  
- For electronic evidence: preserve metadata where possible; export original files and store copies in a secure folder  
- If translations are needed, ask about certified translations and timing  
- If business records are needed, ask about subpoenas to obtain certified records (landlord ledgers, bank statements, utility bills)

Practical organization and preparation steps
- Create an indexed exhibit binder or electronic exhibit folder. Number exhibits, include a one-line description, and a reference to where it came from.  
- Prepare a 1–2 page factual chronology for your attorney and for the court (if required).  
- Draft short witness declarations for each witness you expect to call — they can be finalized after attorney review.  
- List potential cross-examination topics for opposing witnesses (helpful for attorney prep).  
- Back up and preserve all originals and create a secure copy set (PDFs/photos) with a naming convention (YYYYMMDD_subject_source).  
- If you anticipate needing subpoenas, give your attorney the names/addresses of custodians now.

Questions to ask about next-step logistics
- What is the likely timeline from discovery close to hearing in this court? Any local rules peculiar to Maricopa County or immigration court we should know?  
- How much lead time does the attorney need to prepare witnesses/experts once the hearing date is set?  
- Will we need to exchange exhibit lists or do pre-hearing conferences?  
- What will happen at the first hearing (status, evidentiary, scheduling)? What should I expect to bring and be prepared to say?

How I can help next
- I can draft a concise email you can send to Amy asking these questions and listing the documents you have.  
- I can help you build the one-page timeline and an exhibit index if you give me the documents you already have.  
- I can draft witness declaration templates or an exhibit numbering template.

Tell me:
1) Which of the three matters (eviction, immigration, custody) are you dealing with right now and is Amy representing you in it?  
2) Do you want me to draft the email to Amy with the prioritized list of questions and the list of documents you already have?

