I can help — first a quick clarification so I give the most useful, specific checklist: you mentioned a landlord‑tenant eviction, immigration court, and a custody case (opposing party = the other parent). Are these all the same matter, or are they separate cases you’re handling at once? Which of these is the one Amy Foster is representing you for, and which court is the next hearing in (state court or immigration court)?  

While you confirm, here’s a focused, practical set of questions and a document/evidence checklist you can use at your next meeting or call with Amy Foster (Caldwell Legal). You can copy these questions to email her or bring them to your meeting.

Questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic)
- Case scope & strategy
  - Which legal issues will be decided at the next hearing? (e.g., possession only, merits of eviction, custody allocation, removal)
  - What is our overall strategy for the hearing and what outcome should I expect?
  - Are there any dispositive motions we should/shouldn’t file before the hearing?

- Evidence & discovery
  - Based on discovery so far, do we have enough evidence to prove each element the court needs?
  - What specific evidence will the court expect to see at the hearing to support our claims/defenses?
  - Are there any gaps you see in the record that the other side might exploit?

- Documents to obtain or preserve
  - Do we need additional documents from third parties (landlord, property manager, school, employer, bank, police, medical providers)? Can you help subpoena them?
  - Are there digital records we should preserve now (texts, emails, social media, security camera footage, meter/utility logs)? How should I preserve them to keep admissibility (timestamps, originals, screenshots plus metadata)?
  - Do we need receipts, rent ledgers, cancelled checks, bank statements, lease, notices to quit, repair requests, or proof of habitability issues?
  - For custody issues: school records, doctor records, communication logs between parents (texts/emails), visitation logs, photos or videos showing conditions or interactions.
  - For immigration issues: passports, I‑94, immigration paperwork, court notices, affidavits about continuous presence, identity documents, and certified translations if any documents are not in English.

- Witnesses & affidavits
  - Which witnesses should we call? Who can corroborate key facts (neighbors, co‑tenants, repair contractors, witnesses to interactions)?
  - Should we get witness affidavits now? Which ones need notarization or sworn statements?
  - Do any witnesses need preparation or a formal witness statement/deposition?

- Authentication & admissibility
  - Are there any concerns about hearsay or authentication for particular documents (text logs, business records, photos)?
  - Do we need a custodian declaration for business records, or an expert to authenticate technical evidence?
  - Any recommended steps to make sure photos/videos are admissible (date metadata, witness to authenticity)?

- Subpoenas & third‑party records
  - Can we subpoena landlord/management records, maintenance logs, CCTV, phone records, or bank records? What’s the timeline to serve subpoenas?
  - Are there privacy or procedural limits in Maricopa County/state or in immigration court I should know about?

- Experts & evaluations
  - Do we need an expert (property inspector, medical expert, social worker, child psychologist, immigration expert)? If so, when must they be disclosed and what reports are required?
  - Will an expert affidavit be enough, or will the expert need to testify in person?

- Motions & pre‑hearing filings
  - Are there motions in limine, evidentiary motions, or other filings you recommend before the hearing?
  - What are the deadlines for exhibit lists, witness lists, and pretrial briefs in this jurisdiction (Maricopa County or immigration court rules)?

- Hearing logistics & preparation
  - What is the format of the hearing (bench trial, evidentiary hearing, status conference)? How long will it likely last?
  - How should exhibits be formatted and labeled for the court? How many copies does the court require?
  - Will we need to exchange exhibit copies with opposing counsel in advance? If so, by when?

- Settlement & alternatives
  - Are settlement or alternative dispute resolution options worth exploring now? What are realistic settlement terms?
  - If settlement talks occur, how would that affect immigration/custody implications?

- Costs, timeline & next steps
  - What is the likely timeline to get a hearing date now that discovery closed?
  - What are estimated costs if we need subpoenas, experts, or depositions?
  - What should I do immediately (next 24–72 hours) to preserve or gather evidence?

Practical document checklist — high priority to gather now
- Lease, rental agreement, addenda, and any signed move‑in/move‑out inspection reports
- All notices (pay or quit, termination, eviction notices) with dates/receipts
- All communications with landlord/tenant or the other parent: texts, emails, messages (export/save originals)
- Rent payment records: receipts, bank statements, Venmo/PayPal logs, cancelled checks
- Repair requests & responses, photos/videos of property conditions with timestamps/metadata
- Police reports, incident reports, 911 records, or code enforcement complaints
- Photographs/videos showing condition or interactions (with metadata)
- Witness names + contact info + short notes on what they can verify
- Custody: court orders, parenting plans, school attendance records, medical records, caregiving logs
- Immigration: I‑94, passports, immigration correspondence, prior case documentation
- Any prior court filings and opposing party’s discovery responses
- Medical records, if relevant (injuries, mental health, substance use)
- Any business records (property management logs, maintenance invoices)

How to preserve digital evidence
- Export and save original message threads (email in .eml or PDF with headers; text messages via phone backup apps)
- Save photos/videos with original metadata; if you must screenshot, note the original file and time
- Back up copies to cloud + offline storage; keep a chain of custody log noting when and how you saved them

Questions about jurisdiction and procedure (confirm with Amy)
- Is this matter actually in immigration court or in Maricopa County state court? (Different rules for evidence, subpoenas, timing)
- If multiple matters interact (e.g., custody + immigration + eviction), how could evidence/admissions in one case affect the others?

Next steps I can help with
- Turn this checklist into a concise email to Amy Foster asking these questions (I can draft it).
- Create a prioritized to‑do list of documents to collect and a template for witness affidavits.
- Help draft preservation wording you can send to third parties (e.g., “please preserve all records relating to…”).

Tell me:
1) Which of the three matters (eviction, immigration, custody) Amy is representing you on and which court the next hearing will be in.
2) If you want, I’ll draft a short email to Amy with the top questions and attach a prioritized evidence list for her review. Do you want me to draft that email now?

