Below are (A) focused questions to ask your attorney Amy Foster, (B) evidence/document checklist by type of case (eviction, custody, immigration), (C) procedural/strategic items to confirm, and (D) organization and next-step suggestions. You can use these directly in a call or email to Amy — say which case if you want, and I can draft the email for you.

A — Key questions to ask Amy Foster
- Which specific case(s) should I prioritize gathering more evidence for before the next hearing(s)? (Eviction / custody / immigration — please confirm.)
- What is the next scheduled deadline or filing I should know about (motion deadlines, exhibit/witness lists, pretrial statement)? When are they due?
- Are there any outstanding discovery issues or requests we still need to resolve? Did the other side disclose everything required?
- What evidence do you consider critical to win at the hearing? Which items are merely “nice to have” vs. essential?
- Are any of our current documents likely to be excluded for form/chain-of-custody/hearsay reasons? If so, how can we fix them (affidavits, authentication)?
- Do we need any witness testimony? If yes: which witnesses, what will they testify to, and do we need sworn declarations or subpoenas to secure them?
- Should we get signed, notarized affidavits/declarations from key witnesses instead of (or in addition to) live testimony?
- Do you recommend any expert witnesses or third-party records (e.g., property manager, repair contractor, social worker, immigration country-condition expert)?
- Do we need to subpoena records (landlord ledger, bank records, utility records, school records, immigration records, law enforcement records)? If so, please prepare the subpoena now?
- Are any records that are not in English that will require certified translation for the court?
- Will the court require pre-marked exhibits or an exhibit list filed in advance? How many copies do we need at the hearing (court + opposing counsel + us)?
- Do you recommend filing any pre-hearing motions (motion in limine, motions to exclude evidence, summary judgment, continuance)?
- What is the standard of proof applicable to each issue we will face at the hearing and how should that shape our evidence? (e.g., eviction may be preponderance; immigration or custody standards differ)
- What are our settlement/negotiation options at this stage? Is the attorney recommending any targeted settlement attempts before the hearing?
- What will you handle and what do you want me to collect or prepare personally? Is there a format you prefer (PDF, original, certified copies)?
- Are there any safety or privacy steps I should take (redacting sensitive info, filing protective orders)?
- What is our estimated timeline to trial/hearing and likelihood of continuance? If the hearing is months away, what should I prioritize collecting now?

B — Evidence/document checklists
(Use the subset that matches your case; bring everything you have — even if you’re unsure.)

1) Landlord-tenant / eviction
- Lease or rental agreement (all pages, signed)
- Any notices served (Notice to Quit, N12/N13 equivalent), dates and proof of service (certified mail receipts)
- Rent ledger: payment history, late fees, receipts, canceled checks, bank statements showing payments
- Communications: all texts, emails, letters between tenant and landlord (date-stamped and saved as PDFs)
- Photos/videos: dated images of property condition, repairs needed, or alleged damages (with file metadata if possible)
- Repair requests and receipts/estimates for repairs, contractor invoices
- Move-in and move-out checklists, condition reports, keys returned receipts
- Security deposit receipts and deductions with itemized charges
- Police reports or code enforcement notices (if applicable)
- Witness statements from neighbors, property managers, or maintenance staff (signed and dated)
- Any prior court judgments or eviction history involving the property or tenant
- Photocopies of identification showing occupancy (if relevant)

2) Custody (if connected)
- Current custody/parenting-time orders
- Records of parenting-time exchanges, missed visits, or supervised visits
- School records, daycare reports, attendance records
- Medical and mental-health records relevant to parental fitness or the child’s needs (get releases if required)
- Police reports, domestic-violence reports, protective orders
- Photos/videos showing living conditions or interactions (dated)
- Texts/emails showing communication about the child (arrangements, threats, admissions)
- Witness declarations (family members, teachers, pediatrician, counselors)
- Evidence relevant to child’s best interest (stability, primary caregiver, special needs)

3) Immigration (if connected or separate)
- Passports, I-94, visas, immigration receipts or filings (A-file numbers, I-130, I-485, docket number)
- Any criminal records or charges; dispositions
- Birth certificates, marriage certificates, identity documents
- Evidence of continuous residence, employment, community ties
- Country conditions documentation (if relief depends on conditions at origin)
- Translations and certified translations of foreign-language documents
- Any prior immigration court orders or Notices to Appear

C — Procedural/strategic items to confirm
- Exhibits: exact format the court prefers, numbering/labeling rules, how many copies to bring.
- Whether the court expects an exhibit list and witness list filed in advance; if so, deadline.
- Whether sworn affidavits are acceptable in lieu of live testimony for certain witnesses.
- Authentication of business records (admission procedures) — do we need foundation witnesses or custodian declarations?
- Whether any motions in limine should be filed to exclude opposing evidence.
- Whether you should prepare a concise timeline/chronology and proposed findings for the judge.
- Whether the court allows electronic evidence (videos, audio) and particular formatting rules.
- Logistics: estimated hearing length, courtroom, remote appearance possibility, required PPE or ID at check-in.

D — Organization and practical next steps
- Create a master evidence folder (digital + physical). Name files with date and short description (YYYY-MM-DD_description.pdf).
- Prepare a concise timeline of events (one-page) showing dates of notices, payments, communications, and relevant incidents.
- Create an exhibit list spreadsheet: exhibit number, short title, date, file name, and who authenticates it.
- Get signed, dated witness statements/affidavits now while memories are fresh. Include contact info and availability.
- If you need documents from third parties (banks, schools, hospitals), request them immediately — subpoenas can take weeks.
- If any documents are in another language, arrange for certified translations now.
- Back up everything in two places (cloud + external drive) and keep originals in a secure location.
- Ask Amy whether she wants hard copies bound for the judge or a USB with PDFs.
- If you expect the other parent or opposing party to bring surprising evidence, ask Amy to file motions to require pre-disclosure of exhibits.

E — Closing / offer to help
If you want, I can:
- Draft a short, clear email to Amy listing these questions and asking for priorities and deadlines.
- Turn your documents into a labeled exhibit list template (you can upload a list or names).
- Help you draft witness affidavit templates or a one-page timeline draft you can fill in.

Would you like me to draft the email to Amy now? Also please confirm which of the three case types to focus on (eviction, custody, immigration) so I can narrow the list to the most relevant items.

