- What possible outcomes should I expect and what are the next steps after each outcome?
- Are there settlement or alternative dispute options to consider before the hearing?
- Any immigration or custody consequences we should plan for (if those are related)?

Detailed evidence checklist — things courts commonly want in eviction/landlord-tenant and related hearings
Bring originals and organized copies (cited where relevant) and ask your attorney which must be certified:
- Lease/rental agreement and any signed addenda or amendments
- Written notices (e.g., pay-or-vacate, notice to cure, termination notices) with dates
- Proof of service/delivery of notices (signed receipts, certified mail tracking, process server affidavit)
- Rent ledger/payment history (ledgers, receipts, bank statements showing deposits/payments)
- Communications between parties (texts, emails, letters, messages) with dates and participants—export to readable PDF and keep phone screenshots
- Work orders, repair requests, maintenance logs, and landlord responses
- Photographs and videos (with dates/timestamps if available) showing condition, damage, repairs or code violations
- Inspection reports (municipal/code enforcement), building permits, contractor estimates, invoices, receipts
- Police reports or 911 calls (if relevant to events)
- Prior court orders, judgments, or case filings (including proof of filing and service)
- Affidavits/declared witness statements (signed under penalty of perjury) from tenants, neighbors, maintenance staff, or experts
- Expert reports (contractor, HVAC, health/building inspector) and their qualifications
- Proof of identity and residence (if disputed)
- Bank records, cancelled checks, or other financial records tied to claims
- Any lease termination correspondence or relocation offers/agreements
- Timeline summary — concise one-page chronology of key dates and events

Evidence specific items to ask your attorney about
- Authentication: which documents require notarization, certified records, or subpoenaed originals?
- Hearsay: whether certain out-of-court statements need a hearsay exception or a witness to testify
- Witness availability: does it make sense to have witness affidavits instead of live testimony?
- Chain of custody: for physical evidence or digital files, how to document chain of custody
- Digital evidence: best formatting for emails/texts (print with metadata, preserve original files, hash if needed)
- Redactions/privacy: whether to redact sensitive personal info and how to file under seal if needed
- Local rules: Maricopa County local rules/filing requirements for exhibits and pre-hearing filings

Practical organization & deliverables to prepare now
- Create an exhibit binder (or flattened PDF exhibit folder) with numbered exhibits and a master exhibit list (EXH 1, EXH 2, etc.). Include a short description and date for each exhibit.
- Prepare a one-page timeline/chronology for the judge and for counsel.
- Prepare short witness summary sheets (name, expected topics, estimate of direct testimony time).
- Prepare signed declarations/affidavits for witnesses you might not want to bring in person.
- Make three sets of exhibits (court, opposing counsel, your file) unless the court has different rules.
- Create a Document/Exhibit index cross-referenced to your claims/defenses.
- Back up everything digitally and preserve originals (don’t discard any physical evidence).

Questions about discovery and outstanding evidence
- Are there still outstanding discovery requests we should serve (documents, interrogatories)?
- Do we need additional depositions or interview transcripts?
- Did the opposing party produce everything we requested or were there gaps or late productions to challenge?
- Should we move to compel or seek sanctions for missing or late discovery?
- Do we need to prepare a discovery response log (what was requested, produced, withheld and why)?

