3. Assemble the key documents (lease, notices, payment history, communications, photos).
4. Prepare witness affidavits for anyone essential who might be hard to bring live.
5. Create the timeline and exhibit list and send to Amy for review.
6. Discuss whether subpoenas or expert reports are needed now and who will prepare them.

If immigration/custody issues are actually part of the same matter
- Tell Amy about any immigration status issues and ask whether this hearing could affect immigration outcomes (or vice versa); immigration consequences can be collateral issues that require special planning.
- If the opposing party is the other parent in a custody dispute, clarify with Amy whether custody evidence is relevant or whether that’s an entirely separate family-court matter (custody evidence and standards are different from eviction claims).

