I can help you get organized and make sure you ask Amy the right questions so you know whether to gather more evidence before the next date. Below is a focused checklist you can take to your attorney (or send by email) and a short suggested plan for organizing documents and witnesses.

Key clarifying question before I draft anything for you: are the eviction, custody, and immigration matters all tied together (same factual dispute), or are they separate proceedings in different courts? If they’re separate, tell me which facts/documents relate to which case so I can tailor the list.

Questions to ask Amy (grouped by topic)
- Case status & timeline
  - What is the next scheduled court event and what is it for (scheduling, motion hearing, trial, status conference)?
  - Do we have any filing deadlines or court rules (Maricopa County local rules and immigration court rules) I should know about?
  - When will you expect to exchange exhibits/witness lists with the other side?

- Evidence: what the court will want
  - Which specific documents would be most persuasive for the court in my eviction/custody/immigration matters?
  - Are there any types of evidence the court typically excludes or treats skeptically in Maricopa County or immigration court?
  - Do you want originals, certified copies, or will scanned copies be OK? Do any documents need notarization or certified translation?
  - Are declarations or sworn affidavits acceptable in place of live testimony for certain witnesses?

- Discovery & gaps
  - Now that discovery has closed, did anything come up that we still need to subpoena (phone records, financial records, landlord repair logs, school/medical records, police reports)?
  - Are there unanswered discovery requests or documents produced by the other side that we should challenge or follow up on?
  - Do we need to reopen limited discovery or seek leave to supplement the record before the hearing?

- Witnesses & testimony
  - Who should testify live versus who can provide a written declaration? Which witnesses are most critical?
  - Should we prepare witness statements or a summary for the court? Do you want me to draft affidavits/declarations for certain witnesses?
  - Do we need expert witnesses (housing inspector, pediatrician, psychologist, property manager, accountant)? If yes, when must experts’ reports be provided?

- Authentication & chain-of-custody
  - How should we authenticate items like photos, videos, text messages, repair receipts, surveillance footage, or social-media posts?
  - For digital evidence (texts, emails, call logs), what format is preferred and how should metadata be preserved?

- Motions & procedural tactics
  - Are there pre-hearing motions we should file (in limine, to exclude evidence, summary judgment, protective orders)?
  - Any strategic moves you recommend (settlement conference, mediation, agreement about exhibits)?

- Immigration-specific questions (if applicable)
  - How does the eviction/custody dispute affect my immigration proceedings? What immigration evidence should I have ready (identity, family ties, hardship, country conditions)?
  - Are declarations for immigration court different in form or content from those for state court?

- Practical & logistical
  - How should I organize and submit exhibits to you and to the court (binder, PDF, exhibit list with numbers)?
  - Who will handle subpoenas and obtaining records — you or me? Are there costs I should prepare for?
  - How long in advance should witnesses be available to meet and prepare?

Concrete document checklist to assemble now
(Adapt to the specific dispute: eviction, custody, immigration)

- Lease and tenancy documents
  - Signed lease(s), addenda, rent ledgers, receipts, records of payments and late fees
  - Any notices (pay or quit, notice to cure, notice to vacate) and proof of service

- Communications
  - Emails, text messages, WhatsApp messages, voicemails, social-media messages between you and the other party or property manager/landlord (include timestamps)
  - Repair requests/complaints and follow-up communications

- Condition & habitability
  - Photos and videos with dates (interior/exterior, damage, repairs needed)
  - Inspection reports, repair invoices, contractor estimates, building code or health department notices

- Payments & financial
  - Bank statements, canceled checks, money transfer receipts, rent payment records
  - Records of security deposit, move-in/move-out condition reports

- Witness material
  - Contact info and short summary of what each witness will testify to (neighbor, property manager, repairperson, co-parent, teacher)
  - Signed witness statements or declarations (if agreed)

- Official records & incident reports
  - Police reports, 911 calls, municipal code enforcement records, eviction filings, court orders (custody or prior rulings)
  - School/medical records for custody matters (attendance, doctor notes)

- Custody-specific
  - Parenting time logs, calendars, photos, messages about parenting, proof of routines, childcare records
  - Any restraining orders or reports of domestic incidents, child welfare involvement

- Immigration-specific
  - ID, immigration filings, prior immigration notices, proof of presence/continuous residence, affidavits about family/community ties, country condition evidence if applicable

How to organize and present materials
- Create a timeline/chronology: one- or two-page timeline with dates and short descriptions of key events. Reference exhibit numbers next to timeline entries.
- Exhibit list: number each document (Ex. 1, Ex. 2) and create a short title and date for each. Keep originals safe and make a PDF bundle sorted by exhibit number.
- Binder + USB: one physical binder with tabs for each exhibit group (lease, communications, photos, repairs, witness statements) and a PDF/USB copy for the attorney and another for court filing if required.
- Highlight/annotate critical passages: mark the portions you want the court to focus on and note why each document matters.
- Preserve metadata: keep original digital files unaltered (don’t crop timestamps off photos; keep exported email headers).

Preparation for witnesses
- Get brief written statements or declarations early, signed under penalty of perjury if needed.
- Schedule practice with each witness: relevant facts, likely cross-examination areas, timelines to be rehearsed.
- Confirm witness availability for the hearing date and whether remote testimony is allowed.

Next-step checklist (what to do now)
- Send Amy the timeline and your exhibit list draft and ask which items she wants before the hearing.
- Identify any records you do not have and ask Amy whether to issue subpoenas now (phone records, employer records, school/medical, landlord logs).
- Get translations/certified copies and notarizations done if needed.
- Ask Amy whether she recommends any experts and when to retain them.

Would you like:
- A short email I can draft for you to send to Amy summarizing these questions and attaching your timeline/exhibit list?
- A template exhibit list or a one-page timeline you can fill in (I can create it based on the facts you give me)?

If you tell me whether the matters are separate or linked (eviction vs custody vs immigration) and any deadline you already know, I’ll draft a concise email/meeting agenda to send to Amy.

