While you confirm, here’s a practical, case-agnostic guide you can use right away. It includes specific questions to ask Amy Foster plus a prioritized evidence checklist and steps to organize everything before the next hearing.

1) Key clarifying questions to ask your attorney (Amy Foster)
- Which exact hearing or proceeding are we preparing for (court name, docket number, date if known)?
- Which claims/relief will the court address at the next hearing? (e.g., eviction hearing, custody hearing, bond/relief hearing, merits hearing)
- After discovery closed, what evidence did the other side disclose? Are there any outstanding discovery issues or motions pending?
- What is our legal theory and burden of proof at the next hearing?
- What specific pieces of evidence does the court expect or typically admit at this stage?
- Do you expect witnesses? If so, who should I prepare and what will they testify about?
- Are expert witnesses needed or advisable (property condition, valuation, custody evaluator, immigration expert)?
- Do we need to obtain certified documents (e.g., rent ledger, medical records, school records, immigration documents, police reports)?
- Are there deadlines for exhibit lists, witness lists, trial briefs, or pre-hearing filings? When are they?
- Any motions we should file now (motions in limine, to exclude evidence, to compel, for protective order, to continue, summary judgment)?
- Do you recommend subpoenas for third-party documents or testimony? If yes, who should we subpoena and when?
- Any authentication or foundation concerns for key evidence (phone records, CCTV, emails, bank records)?
- Will anything need certified translation or notarized affidavit (non-English records)?
- Are there immigration consequences from likely outcomes here that we should address now (if the immigration matter is connected)?
- What would you like me to prepare or bring to the next meeting/hearing (exhibit binders, originals, witness contact list, declarations)?

2) Evidence checklist — bring or ask your attorney about collecting these (pick the items relevant to your case)
- Core documents
  - Signed lease, rental agreement, notices (3-day/30-day notices), termination notices
  - Court filings, complaints, answers, motions, discovery responses
  - Any custody orders or family court filings if relevant
  - Immigration documents (I-94, notices to appear, applications, relief forms) if immigration court is involved
- Payment and financial records
  - Rent payment history: receipts, bank statements, money transfer records, cancelled checks
  - Security deposit records, invoices for repairs, receipts for payments
- Communications
  - Emails, SMS/text messages, messaging app transcripts, recorded voicemails between parties
  - Letters or certified mail receipts
- Photographs / Video
  - Time-stamped photos or videos of property condition, damages, or key events; preserve originals and metadata if possible
- Third-party records
  - Police reports, 911 calls, incident reports
  - Service provider or repair invoices, inspection reports, code-enforcement letters
  - School records, medical records, therapist or counselor notes (for custody)
- Witness materials
  - Names, contact info, short summary of expected testimony for each witness (neighbors, repair workers, roommates, property manager)
  - Signed witness statements or sworn declarations if possible
- Official & certified records
  - Certified copies of judgments, title records, eviction notices filed with the court
  - Certified translations of non-English documents
- Evidence preservation
  - Any electronic evidence (metadata) preserved by exporting original files
  - Written preservation notices to third-parties (if you believe evidence may be destroyed)
- Other
  - Any prior related judgments, restraining orders, or protective orders
  - Communications with lawyers and settlement offers (check with counsel about privilege)

3) Questions about admissibility and foundation you should ask
- How will we authenticate each important document or recording?
- Are text messages or social-media posts admissible here? Do we need third-party witness/phone records to authenticate?
- Do we need business-record certifications to admit rent ledgers or repair invoices?
- How will you handle hearsay risks for certain statements (witness affidavits, police reports)?
- What is the preferred exhibit format for this court (electronic vs paper, numbered exhibits, Bates-stamping)?
- If we rely on photos/videos, do we need a witness to testify to when/where they were taken and preservation of metadata?

4) What to prepare now — organization & workflow
- Make a concise case chronology (one-page timeline of key events with dates and the document references).
- Create an evidence index: exhibit number → title → date → short description → where original is kept.
- Produce two copies of each important document: one for the court/exhibits and one for your attorney. Keep originals safe.
- Prepare short witness summaries (2–4 sentences each) and contact info.
- Prepare a 1–2 paragraph “case narrative” you can give Amy that states the central facts and outcome sought.
- If you have documents from third parties, request certified copies now and ask Amy whether subpoenas are needed.
- Consider drafting short sworn declarations for key witnesses (Amy can advise format/need).

5) Procedural & tactical issues to ask about
- Is it strategic to file any pre-hearing motions or motions to exclude certain evidence?
- Do we want to propose stipulations to admit certain records without live testimony?
- Are settlement or mediation options advisable before the hearing?
- Should we request a continuance if important evidence or witnesses are not yet available?
- Are there safety or confidentiality concerns requiring sealed filings or protective orders?
- If immigration consequences exist, should we coordinate evidence/strategy across cases?

6) Practical items to confirm with Amy
- How do you prefer to receive documents (email PDFs, secure upload, physical drop-off)?
- What’s the best way to communicate urgent items or new evidence?
- Timeline for when she needs materials to prepare exhibits, witness prep, and pre-hearing filings
- Whether she wants originals at the hearing or will accept certified copies

7) If you want, I can:
- Draft an email to Amy with the above questions and your case summary.
- Create the timeline and exhibit index template you can fill in.
- Help you draft witness statements or a short case narrative for Amy to review.

