Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help—this is a good time to get a focused, practical list so your next steps are clear. First: a quick clarification so I tailor this exactly to your situation — which case do you mean here (or are there multiple overlapping cases)?

- The landlord–tenant eviction case?
- The immigration court matter?
- The custody case (other parent)?
- Or do you have more than one of these active and want a combined checklist?

Assuming you want a thorough, ready-to-use set of questions for your attorney (Amy Foster) and a checklist of evidence to consider collecting before the next hearing, below are (A) priority questions to ask Amy, (B) evidence checklist organized by case-type, and (C) practical pre-hearing steps and tips. Use or copy whichever parts apply to your situation.

A. Priority questions to ask your attorney (short, high-impact)
1. Which specific issues will the court decide at the next hearing?
2. Do we need any additional evidence now to address those issues, or can we add evidence later?
3. Are there any upcoming filing deadlines (motions, exhibit lists, witness lists, pretrial statements) I must meet now?
4. Is the hearing going to be evidentiary (live testimony) or limited to legal argument? How long is each side expected to have?
5. Which pieces of evidence are most persuasive for our position? Which are weak or redundant?
6. Do any of our documents need authentication, notarization, or foundation? (e.g., business records, phone records)
7. Do we need declarations or affidavits from witnesses instead of or in addition to live testimony?
8. Should we subpoena any witnesses or records now? If so, who and by when?
9. Are there experts we should retain (e.g., habitability/repair expert, mental health professional, vocational/financial)?
10. Are there expected hearsay issues or other admissibility concerns I should be aware of?
11. Do we need to file any motions now (motion in limine, motion to exclude evidence, motion for summary judgment)?
12. What do you recommend for organizing and labeling exhibits? How many copies and in what format (paper/electronic)?
13. If settlement is possible, what are realistic settlement terms and do you recommend mediation?
14. If evidence is gathered after discovery closed, how will we get it into the record? (stipulation, late disclosure motion)
15. For immigration or custody elements: are there country condition reports, school records, or social-services reports we should obtain?

B. Evidence checklist — bring/ask about these items, depending on which case this is

If the case is a landlord–tenant eviction:
- Lease/rental agreement (signed copies), addenda, move-in/move-out inspections
- Rent ledger (payments, dates), bank statements, cancelled checks, receipts
- Notices served (pay or quit, cure, termination notices) with proof of service
