First, a quick clarification so I give the most useful guidance: you mentioned a landlord‑tenant eviction, immigration court, and a custody case involving the other parent. Are these separate matters or connected (for example, an eviction that’s related to immigration or custody proceedings)? Which specific hearing are you waiting for and in which court exactly (Maricopa County Justice Court / Superior Court, or immigration court)? Also confirm whether Amy Foster is representing you in all of these matters.

Below is a ready‑to‑use checklist of questions to ask Amy and a prioritized list of evidence to consider collecting before your next hearing. You can bring this list to your next meeting or use it to draft an email to Amy — tell me if you’d like me to draft that email for you.

A. Key questions to ask your attorney (sample wording)
- Case status & timeline
  - “What is the next procedural step and when will it happen? Are there any deadlines I must meet before the hearing?”
  - “Do we expect a pretrial conference, settlement conference, or mediation before the hearing?”

- Legal theory & burden
  - “What elements does the court require the plaintiff/defendant to prove in this eviction/custody/immigration context? What is our burden of proof?”
  - “What are the likely legal issues the judge will focus on?”

- Evidence & admissibility
  - “Which documents and types of evidence will be most persuasive for our legal theory?”
  - “Are there likely admissibility issues (hearsay, authentication, relevance) with any of our documents or communications?”
  - “Do any documents need to be notarized, certified, or authenticated before the hearing?”

- Witnesses
  - “Which witnesses should we plan to call or subpoena? Do any witnesses need written declarations or affidavits in advance?”
  - “Do we need expert witnesses (e.g., housing inspector, pediatrician, psychologist, immigration expert)? If so, when must they be retained and do we need reports?”

- Discovery & motions
  - “Discovery closed — do we need any follow‑up motions (for sanctions, to compel, to exclude evidence)?”
  - “Are there any outstanding discovery issues that could affect the hearing?”

- Specific documents & gaps
  - “I have these documents [briefly list what you have]. Do you see any critical gaps or documents I should obtain now?”
  - “Are there specific forms of proof the court will expect for notices, rent payments, custodial arrangements, or immigration status?”

- Practical & procedural
  - “What should I prepare for testimony (my own and witnesses’) — what factual points must be emphasized?”
  - “How should I organize exhibits and do you want a binder, electronic copies, or both? How many copies for the court/opposing counsel/witnesses?”
  - “Will I need to prepare an exhibit list and witness list in advance? Are there formatting requirements for the court in Maricopa County or immigration court?”

- Settlement & strategy
  - “Are settlement discussions advisable? What would be an acceptable settlement from our side?”
  - “If the hearing goes forward, what is our realistic best outcome and worst outcome?”

B. Prioritized evidence checklist — what courts commonly want (start with the top priorities)
Top priority (collect these first)
- Lease/rental agreement and any addenda or amendments
- Rent payment records: bank statements, money order receipts, landlord rent ledger, canceled checks
- Notices: written notice to pay or quit, notice to vacate, eviction filings — with dates and proof of service
- Communications: emails, text messages, WhatsApp messages or other messages between you and the landlord/tenant/other parent (include timestamps)
- Photographs and videos: dated and locationally clear photos showing condition of property, damages, repairs, hazards, or the living conditions relevant to custody or immigration claims
- Move‑in/move‑out inspection reports and condition checklists
- Any repair requests, work orders, invoices, receipts for repairs or maintenance
