I can help you get organized and prepare focused questions to ask Amy Foster so you know whether you need more evidence before the next hearing. Below is a concise, practical checklist you can use in your meeting or email with her, plus a prioritized list of documents to gather and ways to organize everything.

1) Quick clarifying questions to ask Amy first
- Which court exactly will hear the eviction (Justice Court, Superior Court, immigration court mention — are the eviction and immigration matters connected or separate)?
- Has the court set any upcoming deadlines (pre-trial statement, witness/exhibit lists, motions in limine, disclosures)? If so, what are the dates?
- Is there a scheduled hearing date yet and, if not, what is the typical timing in Maricopa County for scheduling now that discovery is closed?

2) Core questions about evidence and what you should collect
- Do we have all documents the court will expect to see for an eviction case here (lease, rental ledger, notices to quit/notice to pay or vacate, proof of service)?
- Are the documents I already have sufficient to prove the specific claims/defenses we will present? If not, what is missing?
- Do any of my documents need authentication, certification, or notarization before filing or presenting (e.g., bank records, business records)?
- Are there admissibility concerns with any of my evidence (hearsay, lack of foundation, improper format)?
- Do you recommend declarations or sworn witness statements from tenants/neighbors/maintenance people? If yes, who should sign them and what should they say?
- Do we need expert evidence (property inspector, contractor estimates, medical or mental health expert if habitability or health issues are in dispute)?
- Should we obtain contemporaneous photos/videos with timestamps, repair invoices, inspection reports, or code-violation records?
- Are text messages, emails, social-media messages, or voicemail recordings acceptable? Do they need to be printed in a specific way or have metadata preserved?
- Should I secure bank statements, canceled checks, money-order receipts, or rent-pay receipts to support payment history?
- If any third-party records are needed (utility records, landlord-tenant authority, code-enforcement), should we subpoena them now or request them informally?
- If opposing party is the other parent in a custody case, are there documents from the custody matter that might be relevant to this eviction case — or vice versa? (Ask whether the matters should remain separate or whether evidence cross-applies.)
