I can help — below is a focused, practical checklist of questions to ask Amy Foster plus a prioritized list of evidence you should consider gathering or preserving before the next court date. I’ll also include short prompts you can copy into an email or ask in your next call. First, two quick clarifying questions so I can tailor this more precisely:
- Which next hearing is this for — the landlord-tenant eviction, the immigration proceeding, or the custody matter? (You mentioned all three.)
- What documents you already have (brief list) and the date of the next scheduled court event (if known)?

Questions to ask your attorney (use these with Amy Foster)
- Case status / deadlines
  - What exactly is the next court event (status hearing, scheduling, merits hearing, pretrial)? What is the date and what will be decided?
  - Are there any deadlines coming up (motions, exhibit lists, witness lists, pre-hearing disclosures, filings)?
  - Has discovery closed for this specific hearing and are there any outstanding discovery disputes I should know about?

- Burden of proof & legal strategy
  - What is our burden of proof at the next hearing and what key facts must we prove?
  - What are the opponent’s strongest claims/arguments and what evidence will they likely rely on?
  - Based on what you’ve seen, what are the two or three highest‑value pieces of evidence we need to present?

- Evidence admissibility & preparation
  - Are the documents I have likely to be admissible as-is, or will we need foundation/ authentication (e.g., business records, certified copies, witness testimony)?
  - Do any documents require certified translation or notarization?
  - If we plan to use photographs, videos, or audio, do we need to preserve metadata or get an expert to authenticate them?

- Witnesses & statements
  - Who should we call as witnesses? Do we need written declarations/affidavits from them now?
  - Should we depose any witnesses or seek sworn statements?
  - How many witness appearances are realistic for this hearing (court time limits)?

- Subpoenas, records, & third-party evidence
  - Should we subpoena landlord/tenant records, phone records, police reports, school records, medical records, bank statements, or surveillance footage? If so, for which providers and on what timeline?
  - Are there privacy/consent issues with obtaining recordings or messages (e.g., recordings from the opposing party)?

- Handling digital evidence & preservation
  - How should I preserve texts, emails, social media posts, photos, and videos (preferred formats, screenshots vs exports)? Where should I store backup copies?
  - Do we need forensics (e.g., to recover deleted messages or verify GPS metadata)?

- Exhibits, timeline, and organization
  - Do you want me to prepare a timeline of events and label exhibits (Exhibit A, Exhibit B...) for you? If so, what format is preferred?
  - Should I prepare a short witness contact list with summaries of what each witness will say?

- Motions & procedural options
  - Are there motions we should consider before the hearing (e.g., motion to exclude evidence, motion in limine, summary judgment, continuance)?
  - If we’re not ready, what is the likelihood and procedure for requesting a continuance?

- Costs, experts, and logistics
  - Would hiring an expert (e.g., property manager, safety inspector, forensic analyst, mental‑health evaluator) strengthen our case? What are approximate costs and timeline for getting an expert report?
  - For immigration court specifically: are there additional immigration‑related documents we must bring (I-94, prior filings, any DHS/ICE notices)? Are there different rules of evidence we must follow?

- Settlement & negotiation
  - Is settlement or mediation an option right now? What terms should I be prepared to accept or reject?
  - If settlement is possible, what documentation will be required to finalize it?

Evidence checklist — prioritize and preserve now
High priority (collect ASAP)
- Lease or rental agreement, addenda, and any signed amendments
- All rent payment records (receipts, bank transfers, checks, Venmo/PayPal logs) with dates
- Notices: notice to vacate, eviction notice, any written demands
- All communications with landlord/tenant/opposing parent: emails, texts, DMs (preserve originals and export full threads)
- Photographs and videos showing condition of property, damages, repairs, with date/time metadata if possible
- Police reports or 911 call records (if any incidents occurred)
- Court filings and discovery responses produced so far (copies of everything exchanged)
- Correspondence from opposing counsel or the other party
- Proof of attempts to repair or requests for repairs (work orders, contractor receipts, emails)

Medium priority
- Bank statements and cancelled checks supporting payments or receipts
- Repair estimates, invoices, and receipts for repairs you paid
- Witness contact info and short written statements (neighbor, co‑tenant, repair person)
- School records or custody‑relevant documents (if custody is relevant)
- Rent ledger (if landlord keeps one) or demand letters

Lower but useful
- Social media posts that corroborate dates/locations (screenshots with URL and timestamps)
- Utility bills or occupancy records
- Moving/relocation costs (if claiming damages)
- Any prior eviction or housing court decisions involving the parties

Evidence integrity & format
- Keep originals and make multiple backups (encrypted cloud + external drive). Don’t alter originals.
- Export text threads rather than just screenshot when possible (to preserve metadata). Save email headers.
- For photos/videos, keep the original file (not just copies pasted into documents) so metadata remains intact.
- If foreign-language materials exist, get certified translations early.

Practical next steps you can do before talking to Amy
- Make a short inventory list of what you already have (1‑page bulleted list of documents and witnesses).
- Create a simple chronological timeline of key events (dates + one-line description).
- Note any missing dates or gaps and whether any evidence might still exist (surveillance cameras, building records).
- Identify any witnesses and their contact info and whether they’ll give a written statement.

Sample email you can send or read to Amy (short version)
Hi Amy — I want to be ready for the next hearing. Could you confirm:
1) Which hearing this is and any upcoming deadlines; 2) the key facts we must prove and the strongest evidence we need; 3) whether you recommend subpoenas or expert reports; and 4) whether you want me to prepare an exhibit list, timeline, and witness statements (I can draft them). Also, do discovery/ admissibility rules differ for this hearing (eviction vs immigration)? I can send you an inventory of documents I already have. Thanks — [Your name]

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a longer email to Amy with more of the specific questions above.
- Help you create the exhibit list and timeline from the documents you have (you can paste a list of what you already possess).
- Create a prioritized “to collect” checklist tailored to whichever hearing is next (eviction, immigration, or custody) once you tell me which it is.

Would you like me to draft the full email to Amy now, or make a customized checklist/timeline based on the documents you already have?

