- Do we have school records, pediatric/mental-health records, police reports, CPS records, or documented parenting-time logs?
- Are there text/email exchanges or calendar logs showing parenting time disputes or safety issues?
- Do we need psychological or social-work evaluations, or a parenting-time study?

Prioritized evidence checklist (what to gather now)
A. Core documentary evidence (collect these first)
- All court filings and docket entries (copies of complaints, answers, motions, discovery responses)
- Lease/rental agreement and any written notices (termination, notices to vacate)
- Rent payment records (bank transfers, checks, receipts, rent ledger)
- All communications (texts, emails, DMs) between you and opposing party or landlord that relate to the dispute
- Photos and videos with dates (property condition, damages, safety hazards)
- Police reports or incident reports (if any) and medical records for injuries
- Certified records you already have (birth certificates, passports, immigration documents)

B. Supporting third-party records to request
- Bank statements showing payments or transfers
- Utility bills or repair invoices
- School attendance and records (for custody matters)
- Employer records if income or housing ability is at issue

C. Witness documentation
- Short written affidavit or chronology from each witness summarizing what they will testify about and dates
- Contact information and availability for subpoena

D. Organization & authentication
- Create a single timeline of events (date → short note → supporting exhibit references)
- Number exhibits and prepare an exhibit list (Exhibit 1: lease; Exhibit 2: rent ledger; etc.)
- Scan and save PDFs; keep originals secured (and note where originals are)
- Prepare certified translations for any non-English documents

Practical prep steps you can do this week
