Questions to ask your attorney (short, clear versions you can use in a message or call)
- Which case is the upcoming hearing in, and which court’s rules apply? (State eviction court, Maricopa County civil calendar, or EOIR/immigration court?)
- What is the exact purpose of the next hearing (status scheduling, pretrial, merits/trial, removal hearing, custody/temporary orders)?
- Are there any upcoming deadlines I should know about (pre‑hearing exhibit list, witness list, motions, pretrial brief)?
- Do you expect we should file any pretrial motions (motions in limine, summary judgment, motion to exclude evidence)?
- From discovery, is there any evidence you think is weak or needs supplementation?
- What specific items or types of evidence will be most persuasive for our claims/defenses in this hearing?
- Do we need witness declarations or in‑court live witnesses? If so, who should I ask and do you want sworn affidavits now?
- Do we need expert witnesses or professional reports (e.g., repairs/health & safety inspectors for eviction; custody evaluators; medical experts)?
- Are there documents I should obtain and authenticate now (certified copies, police reports, hospital records, school records, tax records, immigration records)?
- Should any documents be translated and certified now?
- Do you want originals or certified copies at the hearing, or will copies suffice if properly authenticated?
- Do you recommend subpoenas for any records or witnesses? If yes, who and how soon should I ask you to issue them?
- Are there any chain‑of‑custody concerns for evidence (forensic, physical items, photographs)?
- Do we need to prepare demonstratives (timeline, photo stack, binder) or an exhibit notebook for the judge?
- Do you want me to prepare a chronology/timeline of events? If so, in what format and how detailed?
- Are there privacy/confidentiality or sealing procedures we should use for sensitive documents (immigration, custody, medical)?
- What is the likely hearing length, and how much direct examination time should we expect per witness?
- If settlement or alternate resolution is an option, do you recommend trying to negotiate before the hearing?
- Is there anything I should avoid doing or sending to the opposing party right now?

Evidence checklist — items to gather (grouped by common issues)
General / organizational
- Chronology/timeline of key dates and events (one‑page and expanded version)
- Indexed folder or PDF with numbered exhibits and a cover sheet for each exhibit
- Contact list for witnesses (name, phone, email, relationship, short statement of what they will say)
- Copies (and where possible originals) of every document, with certified copies if requested
- Electronic copies (PDFs) named consistently (e.g., 01_Lease.pdf; 02_PaymentRecords.pdf)
- Notarized/affidavit statements from witnesses if they may not appear live
- Translations and certified translations for any non‑English records

If eviction / landlord-tenant issues
- Lease or rental agreement (all pages, amendments)
- Payment records: receipts, bank statements, money orders, rent ledger
- Notices served: e.g., Notice to Quit, Notice to Pay or Vacate, proof of service (certified mail receipts, process server logs)
- Repair requests / maintenance communications (emails, texts, repair invoices)
- Photographs and videos of the premises, with dates and context
- Building/health code inspection reports, repair estimates, contractor invoices
- Tenant complaints, police calls, or code enforcement complaints (reports)
- Correspondence with landlord/tenant (emails, text logs, recorded calls if lawful)
- Move‑in/move‑out condition reports, security deposit records
- Witnesses: neighbors, property managers, contractors

If custody / parenting issues
- Child’s records: school attendance, teacher statements, counseling/therapy notes, medical records
- Calendar/log of parenting time, missed visits, concerns, exchanges
- Photographs or texts illustrating relevant incidents (dated)
- Police reports, protective orders, medical records (if DV or safety concerns)
- Previous court orders, pleadings, parenting plans, custody evaluations
- Witness affidavits (family, teachers, childcare providers)
- Evidence of stability: housing, employment, childcare arrangements

If immigration / EOIR matters
- Passport(s), I‑94, visa documents, any USCIS or EOIR notices and filings
- Any prior immigration filings (I‑130, I‑485, NTA, responses)
- Criminal records, arrest reports, dispositions, certified court records
- Country‑conditions evidence, country‑specific reports if relevant
- Proof of family/community ties, employment, tax records, paystubs
- Affidavits of support or hardship letters (if relief depends on family hardship)
- Translations and certified translations of foreign documents
- Witness affidavits supporting claims (e.g., credibility, family ties)

Evidence preparation & authenticity issues to confirm with your attorney
- Ask how the court prefers exhibits to be presented and numbered.
- Confirm whether you must provide pre‑marked exhibit lists and exchange them with opposing counsel.
- Ask if originals are required at hearing or copies are acceptable (bring originals anyway).
- Confirm authentication steps (witness testimony, affidavits, business records foundation).
- Confirm whether electronic evidence (texts, social media) needs metadata or third‑party verification.
- Ask about hearsay exceptions that might allow certain documents or statements.

Logistics / procedural questions to ask now
- What is our exhibit and witness disclosure deadline?
- Do we need to file a pretrial brief or witness/exhibit list? If so, when and what format?
- Are there local Maricopa County or EOIR chambers rules I should review?
- If the hearing is remote, what technical arrangements are needed? Are there rules for presenting exhibits over video?
- What is your plan for direct examination and rebuttal? How many witnesses will we call?
- How and when will you want me available to review documents or sign declarations?

How to get organized right now (practical next steps)
- Create a single digital folder and a printed binder with labelled, numbered tabs for each exhibit.
- Draft a concise one‑page timeline of facts and desired outcome for the hearing.
- Start collecting sworn witness statements/affidavits from key people now (so they’re ready).
- Make certified copies of important official records (court records, police reports, medical records).
- Get any necessary translations and notarizations completed early.
- Keep a running log of all communications with opposing party or landlords/guardians (dates/times/content).
- Notify witnesses early of possible hearing dates and get their availability.

What I can do next for you
