Meanwhile, below are ready-to-use questions to ask your attorney (Amy Foster at Caldwell Legal) and a document/evidence checklist you can use for any upcoming hearing. Use the attorney questions at your meeting or in an email; bring the evidence checklist to ensure nothing is missed.

Questions to ask your attorney
1. Which case and court are we preparing for, and what is the next scheduled event or deadline? (Confirm court name, case number, and whether the hearing is local county court, immigration court, or family court.)
2. Given discovery just closed, what outstanding evidentiary deadlines do I need to know (exhibit lists, witness lists, motions in limine, pre-hearing disclosures)?
3. What is the legal standard we must meet at the upcoming hearing? (e.g., proof of rent arrears for eviction, “best interest of the child” for custody, burden for immigration relief)
4. Which items of my current evidence are strongest? What’s weak or missing?
5. Do you see any admissibility problems with my evidence (hearsay, authentication, foundation issues)? What will the court likely exclude?
6. Do we need sworn declarations or live witness testimony to authenticate documents or prove facts? If so, who should testify?
7. Should we obtain subpoenas for documents or testimony (bank records, employer records, phone records, school records, police reports)? What’s the process and timeline?
8. Do we need expert witnesses (e.g., appraiser, forensic accountant, child psychologist, medical expert)? If yes, by when do they need to be retained and disclosed?
9. Are any documents that are currently informal (texts, emails, screenshots, photos) acceptable as-is, or do they need certification, metadata, or notarized affidavits?
10. Do any opposing-party documents or the discovery responses raise new lines of evidence we should pursue?
11. Should we ask the court for a preservation order if there’s risk of destruction of evidence?
12. What pre-hearing filings should we prepare (exhibit list, pretrial statement, witness list, motions)? Are there local court rules or templates you want me to follow?
13. What’s our hearing strategy: what evidence will we present first, what to concede, what to highlight?
14. Do you want me to try settlement/mediation before the hearing? What are acceptable settlement terms?
15. What are the estimated costs and time to obtain more documents or experts? Are they justified given likely outcomes?
16. Is there anything I should not do (e.g., contacting the other party directly) that could hurt our case?
17. Can you review these documents I’ve gathered and confirm which to add to the exhibit notebook and which to drop?
18. Who will call and examine our witnesses at the hearing, and will you prepare witness statements/declarations?
19. Are translations/certified copies required for any foreign-language documents?
20. What is your recommended timeline for finalizing exhibits and witness preparation before the hearing?

Evidence / documents checklist (bring these to your attorney meeting)
A. General / case management
- Case number(s) and court contact info
- All discovery produced (both sides), with dates
- Chronology / timeline of events (one-page, bullet timeline)
- Contact list for witnesses (name, phone, email, relevance)
- Copies (x3) of any document you want used as exhibits
- Originals or certified copies where possible

B. Landlord–tenant eviction (if this is the case)
- Lease/rental agreement and any signed addenda
- Notices served: pay-or-quit, cure-or-quit, 10-day/5-day notices, eviction notice (dates and proof of service)
- Rent ledger and payment records (bank statements, money order receipts)
- Communication log: emails, texts, letters between landlord/tenant
- Photos/videos of property condition with dates/time metadata
- Repair requests/maintenance records and receipts
- Police reports or code-enforcement complaints (if relevant)
- Move-in/move-out condition report, inventory, signed check-in/out
- Witness statements (neighbors, neighbors who saw issues)
- Any attempts at resolution or mediation

C. Custody / family (if applicable)
- Court orders, parenting plans, prior custody/visitation orders
- Child’s school records (attendance, disciplinary notes), teacher statements
- Medical/therapy records for child
- Communication history with the other parent (texts, emails about parenting)
- Calendars/logs of parenting time, missed visits, childcare arrangements
- Evidence of domestic violence, substance abuse, criminal history (police reports, restraining orders)
- Evidence of living situation/stability (housing, sleeping arrangements)
- Financial records (support, child-related expenses)
- Witness contact info: teachers, pediatrician, childcare providers

D. Immigration (if applicable)
- Passports, birth certificates, marriage certificates
- I-94, prior immigration filings, receipts, copies of forms (I-130, I-485, etc.)
- Any notices from immigration court (NTA, hearing notices)
- Criminal records/arrest reports and dispositions (if any)
- Employment records, tax returns, pay stubs
- Proof of residence, community ties, family hardship evidence
- Affidavits from family/community members (signed, dated)
- Country-condition evidence if relevant (reports, news articles)
- Translations and certified copies of foreign documents

E. Digital evidence specifics
- Original files (not just screenshots) for texts, emails, photos/video
- Exported message threads (PDFs with metadata) where possible
- Phone records or bills showing calls/SMS timestamps
- For photos/videos, preservation of metadata or a sworn affidavit describing when/how they were taken

How to prepare the documents and present them to your lawyer
- Make one chronological timeline (1–2 pages) that lists key events with dates — it helps the attorney immediately see gaps.
- Put numbered exhibit copies (e.g., Exhibit 1 = lease, Exhibit 2 = rent ledger). Ask the attorney how they want exhibits labeled.
- Create a short witness summary for each person (what they will say, why they’re credible).
- If you have many files, create a simple folder structure (by topic/date) and provide a digital copy (PDFs) and printed packet if requested.
- Note any urgent documents you fear might disappear (ask your attorney about preservation/subpoena).

