Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help you get organized. First, a quick clarifying question so I tailor this better: are these all part of the same matter (eviction, custody, and immigration issues all tied together), or are you dealing with separate cases (an eviction in county court, a custody case, and a separate immigration proceeding)? If you prefer, I’ll proceed with general guidance that covers all three.

Below are (A) focused questions to ask your attorney (Amy Foster) and (B) a practical checklist of evidence and preparation steps you can take now. I also include short suggested wording you can use if you want to email or ask her directly.

A. Short, essential questions to ask Amy Foster
(Use these in your next call/meeting or email — they’re written to be direct and actionable.)

Case status & next steps
1. What is the current status and expected timeline? When will the hearing likely be scheduled?
2. What is your strategy for the hearing? What outcome are we aiming for?
3. Are there any pending deadlines I need to know about (motions, witness/exhibit lists, pre-hearing briefs)?

Evidence & exhibits
4. Based on what we already produced in discovery, is there any specific additional documentary evidence the court is likely to want or that would strengthen our position?
5. Do you want originals, certified copies, or will good-quality scans/photos be acceptable?
6. Are there particular document formats or labeling rules for exhibits in this court (exhibit numbers, cover sheets, exhibit list)?

Witnesses & declarations
7. Should we have witness declarations or signed affidavits prepared now? If so, who should we ask (neighbors, property managers, repair contractors, teachers, counselors)?
8. Do any witnesses need to be subpoenaed? Will they testify in person or by declaration/remote testimony?
9. Do you recommend expert witnesses (e.g., property inspector, medical/therapist, custody expert)?

Authentication & admissibility
10. Which documents might face hearsay or authentication challenges and how can we prepare to authenticate them (business records affidavits, metadata for photos, preserved text/email logs)?
11. For photos or video: do you want file metadata, timestamps, or sworn statements about when/how they were taken?

Discovery & follow-ups
12. Are there additional targeted discovery requests or motions (e.g., motion to compel, subpoenas for bank/phone records) you recommend before the hearing?
13. Do you want me to gather specific evidence now so we can move quickly if a hearing date is set?

Court-specific rules & filings
14. Are there any local rules for Maricopa County or immigration court procedures that affect exhibit submission, witness lists, or pre-hearing filings?
15. Do we need to file a pre-hearing statement, exhibit list, or proposed order in advance?

Custody / opposing parent issues
16. If the opposing party is the other parent, are there particular records the court will expect re: parenting time, child welfare, communications, or safety (school records, CPS reports, police reports)?
17. Are protective orders, prior custody orders, or DV incidents relevant and should they be introduced now?

Immigration-specific (if applicable)
18. If this matter intersects with immigration proceedings, are there immigration-specific evidentiary or translation requirements (certified translations, certified copies, country condition reports, etc.)?
19. Will any documents or testimony from the eviction/custody matter be used in immigration court? If so, how should they be presented to be admissible?

Settlement & mitigation
20. Are there settlement options we should prepare to discuss? If so, what documentation would I need to support any proposed agreement?

B. Evidence checklist — what to gather now
Documents
- Lease or rental agreement (signed)
- Notices (notice to pay/rent or quit, notice to vacate, any written eviction notices)
- Court filings: complaint/petition, answers, motions, orders
- Payment records: rent receipts, canceled checks, bank statements
- Communications: texts, emails, direct messages, letters (keep originals and screenshots with timestamps/metadata)
- Repair requests / maintenance logs, photos of property condition (with metadata)
- Photos/videos (include filenames, timestamps, and a short written statement about when/how each was taken)
- Police reports, 911 calls, or incident reports
- Any prior custody orders or related family court documents
- School records, medical/therapy records (if custody/children involved)
- Notices from government agencies (code enforcement, health department)
- ID, immigration paperwork, or letters relevant to immigration proceedings

Witness evidence
- Names, contact info, and short summary of what they will say
- Written, signed witness statements or declarations (dated and notarized if possible)
- Expert reports (property inspectors, medical/psych experts); written engagement agreements

Authentication & organization
- Create an exhibit folder with a clear index (Exhibit 1, Exhibit 2…)
- Keep a separate timeline (chronological one-page summary of key dates and events)
- Preserve originals and make multiple copies (one for court, one for your file, one for attorney)
- If evidence is digital, export/snapshot metadata and save raw files (don’t edit)

Practical steps to ask Amy about
- Whether she wants me to collect any of the above documents now and how she prefers receiving them (email, secure portal, hard copies)
- Whether she needs signed declarations from me or witnesses ahead of time
- If any documents need certified translation or notarization for immigration court
- Whether to prepare a short pre-hearing memo outlining facts and legal arguments

Suggested short email to Amy (you can copy/paste)
Subject: Quick: Evidence & prep before hearing date
Hi Amy — The discovery phase just ended and I want to be fully prepared while we wait for a hearing date. Can we confirm (1) any additional documents or witnesses you want me to gather now, (2) whether you need signed witness declarations or certified translations, and (3) any filing deadlines or exhibit-list format for the upcoming hearing? I’ve started compiling lease, rent records, notices, communications, photos (with metadata), police reports, and a timeline. Please let me know next steps or if you want me to send these files to your portal. Thanks, [Your name]

Would you like me to:
- Turn the checklist above into a one-page printable exhibit/timeline template you can bring to your meeting with Amy?
- Draft or send the email to Amy for you now? (If you want me to send, tell me how you want to open the message and whether to use the suggested text.)

If you confirm whether the matters are all one case or separate (eviction vs. custody vs. immigration), I’ll tailor the questions and checklist further (e.g., more immigration-specific evidence or custody-specific records).

