I can help — first, a quick clarification so I give the most useful, targeted list: do you have one case (which type and which court) or multiple cases? Your message mentions a landlord-tenant eviction, immigration court, and a custody opponent — which of those is the matter Amy Foster at Caldwell Legal is handling for you right now? Once you confirm, I’ll tailor the questions and checklist to that matter. 

While you confirm, here’s a practical, ready-to-use set of questions and a prioritized evidence checklist you can bring to Amy Foster to determine whether you need more evidence before the next date. If some items don’t apply, ignore the irrelevant sections.

Questions to ask your attorney
- Case basics & next steps
  - What is the current procedural posture (discovery closed, any pending motions, deadline dates)? Are we waiting only for a hearing date?
  - What is the legal standard/burden of proof the court will apply at the next hearing?
  - What are the key issues the judge will decide at that hearing?
  - What outcome should I realistically expect at the next hearing?

- Evidence & admissibility
  - Based on what we currently have, what evidence is missing or weak for proving the claims/defenses we need?
  - Which pieces of my records are admissible as-is and which need certification, foundation, or witness testimony to be admitted?
  - Are there hearsay concerns with any of my documents (text threads, emails, police reports)? If so, how can we overcome them?
  - Do I need original documents, certified copies, or will clear scanned copies be acceptable?

- Witnesses and statements
  - Do you recommend live witnesses or sworn declarations/affidavits? Which witnesses are most important?
  - Should I prepare witness statements or affidavits now? Do they need notarization?
  - If a witness may be unavailable for the hearing, should we subpoena them now?

- Experts & evaluations
  - Do we need any expert reports or evaluations (property condition experts, appraisers, medical, mental health, country-condition experts for immigration, custody evaluators)?
  - What is the timeline to obtain and disclose any expert reports?

- Discovery closure & follow-ups
  - With discovery closed, can we still seek additional evidence (motions to reopen discovery, subpoenas, requests to produce)? If yes, what’s the procedure and deadline?
  - Are there outstanding discovery items from the other side we should compel or follow up on?

- Court procedure & logistics
  - What documents or exhibit list must be filed or exchanged with opposing counsel before the hearing? Are there local rules for Maricopa County / the specific court (or immigration court rules) I need to follow?
  - How many copies/exhibits does the court want and in what format (paper vs. electronic)?
  - Do exhibits need to be pre-marked and exchanged with opposing counsel? By what date?
  - Are translations needed and do translations require certification?

- Strategy & settlement
  - Given our evidence and status, do you recommend settlement discussions or mediation before the hearing?
  - What are the potential risks of going to hearing vs. settling?

- Practicalities & deadlines
  - What deadlines should I calendar now (filings, exhibit lists, witness lists, subpoenas, motions)?
  - How much lead time do you need from me to finalize witness statements or exhibits?

Prioritized Evidence Checklist (use the sections that match your case)

If this is an eviction (landlord-tenant) case
- Lease/rental agreement (signed originals or certified copies)
- Rent ledger and payment receipts (bank records, cancelled checks)
- Notices served (eviction notices, pay-or-quit, cure notices) — proof of service
- Written communications: emails, texts, letters between landlord/tenant
- Photos/video of property condition (with dates/metadata if possible)
- Repair requests and responses, invoices/receipts for repairs
- Code violation or inspection reports (city/county)
- Security deposit records and itemized deductions
- Witness statements from neighbors, property managers, contractors
- Any local ordinance or statutory citations relied on by landlord/tenant

If this is a custody/family matter
- Court orders and pleadings in the family case (existing custody, support orders)
- Communication logs (text/email, parenting app logs) showing interactions or incidents
- School records and attendance reports
- Medical/mental health records relevant to parenting/capacity
- Police reports, protection orders, domestic violence documentation
- Photographs, videos, timestamps showing conditions or incidents
- Witness statements (teachers, childcare providers, neighbors)
- Calendar of parenting time, missed visits, or concerns
- Evidence of substance abuse or criminal activity if relevant (drug tests, arrest records)

If this is immigration-related (in immigration court)
- Government-issued IDs, passport, I-94, visas, notices to appear
- Copies of prior immigration filings, receipts, notices from EOIR/USCIS
- Documentary evidence supporting relief (work history, family ties, hardship evidence)
- Country conditions reports, news articles, expert affidavits (if asylum or country-condition issues)
- Medical records, police reports, or other corroborating evidence
- Certified translations of anything not in English
- Affidavits from witnesses with personal knowledge

Document preparation & organizational steps (what you can do now)
- Create a master chronological timeline of events (dating each item) — concise 1–2 page timeline.
- Make a numbered exhibit list and label each exhibit (Ex. 1, Ex. 2) with short descriptions and dates.
- Prepare a “facts packet” with key documents (signed lease, notices, payment proofs, timeline).
- Prepare short witness summary bullets for each person (what they will testify about).
- Scan all documents clearly, keep originals safe and accessible.
- Get translations done early and certified if any documents are not in English.
- If you have digital messages, capture full threads and metadata (export if possible).
- Back up everything (cloud + local) and keep an index.

Practical questions about costs & timing to ask
- How long will it take and how much will it cost to obtain an expert or subpoena a witness?
- If we need a certified translation or records from a third party (bank, school, hospital), what’s the typical turnaround?

How I can help next
- I can draft a concise email or list you can send to Amy Foster listing these questions and the documents you have — would you like me to draft that for you?
- If you tell me which documents you already have, I can help prioritize what to gather next and create the exhibit checklist/template for you to bring to Amy.

Tell me which court/case type to focus on (eviction, immigration, custody — or confirm it’s just one), and whether you want a ready-to-send email to Amy Foster summarizing these questions and listing the documents you already have.

