Thanks — I can help you get organized. Before I make detailed suggestions: are you asking about the landlord‑tenant eviction case, the immigration court matter, or the custody case (or more than one of these)? Each court has different rules and different typical evidence needs. If it’s more than one matter, tell me which one you want to focus on first.

Below I’ll give:
- A concise list of general questions to ask Amy Foster about whether you need more evidence before the next date;
- Case‑specific evidence checklists (eviction, immigration, custody) so you can pick what’s relevant;
- Practical steps to organize what you already have; and
- A short email template you can send to Amy to get direction and a meeting.

General questions to ask your attorney (use these at your next call/meeting)
- What exactly is the next scheduled step (status conference / pretrial / hearing)? Do we have a firm hearing date or timeline?
- Are there any upcoming filing deadlines, disclosure deadlines, or court rules I must meet in Maricopa County or with immigration court?
- Based on the discovery results, do you consider our current evidence sufficient to meet our burden (or to defend against their claims)? If not, what’s missing?
- What categories of evidence will the court likely expect or rely on at the hearing?
- Which documents need originals vs. certified copies vs. plain copies? Do any require notarization or translation?
- Do we need witness statements or affidavits now, or can they be provided at hearing? Which witnesses should we try to secure now?
- Should we pre‑mark exhibits and exchange an exhibit list with the opposing party? What is the required format and number of copies for the court?
- Are there any anticipated motions (summary judgment, motions in limine, evidentiary challenges) we should prepare now?
- Do we need expert reports or evaluations (e.g., property inspection, repair estimate, forensic accountant, custody evaluator, country‑conditions expert for immigration)?
- Do you recommend depositions or subpoenas for documents/witness testimony before the hearing?
- Are any documents at risk of being lost or destroyed that we should preserve or subpoena now?
- Do we need to prepare foundation/witnesses to authenticate electronic evidence (text messages, emails, photos, social media)?
- Are there any privilege issues (attorney‑client, work product) we need to manage when producing or using documents?
- What should I do immediately to be best prepared (e.g., collect specific documents, draft witness summaries, prepare timeline)?
- How long will you need to review additional evidence I collect? What’s the best way to deliver documents to you (scanned PDF, portal, physical copies)?

Case‑specific evidence checklist (pick what applies)

If this is a landlord‑tenant eviction:
- Lease or rental agreement (all pages and amendments)
- Rent ledger / receipts, cancelled checks, bank statements showing rent payments or nonpayments
- Notices or demands served (3‑day notice, 10‑day, notice to quit) and proof of service
- Communications between you and landlord/tenant (texts, emails, letters)
- Photographs and videos of the premises (with dates/time, showing condition)
- Work orders, repair requests, invoices/receipts for repairs
- Code violation notices from city/county, inspector reports
- Witness statements (neighbors, building manager, contractors)
- Security deposit records and accounting
- Move‑in and move‑out condition reports
- Any police reports or incident reports relevant to the eviction
- Lease violation documentation (noise complaints, unauthorized occupants, etc.)
- Evidence of attempts to mitigate or resolve the issue (repair requests, payment arrangements)
- Any settlement offers or mediation notes

If this is an immigration court matter:
- Copies of all immigration filings and receipts (NTA, I-485, I-130, asylum application, or other forms)
- Passport, visas, I‑94, work authorization, any prior orders or hearings transcripts
- Birth certificates, marriage certificates, identity documents, and certified translations
- Criminal records (if any), dispositions, court documents
- Employment records, paystubs, tax returns, school records, proof of residence
- Affidavits from friends/family describing ties/community
- Country‑conditions evidence or expert reports (if relief depends on country conditions)
- Medical records (if relevant)
- Any witness statements or declarations you want to present
- Documentation of any filings with EOIR, USCIS, or other agencies and notices received
- Evidence of harm or persecution (if asylum or withholding is claimed)
- Proof of any immigration relief eligibility (length of residence, qualifying family relationships)

If this is a custody/parenting dispute:
- Court orders and parenting plans (existing orders)
- School records, attendance reports, medical records for children
- Communication logs between parents (texts, emails, social media) showing interactions about care/visitation
- Evidence of parental involvement: calendars, photos, receipts showing time spent with the child
- Police reports, protective orders, domestic violence records (if applicable)
- Witness statements from teachers, counselors, family members
- Work schedules and childcare arrangements
- Records of counseling or evaluations (parenting classes, family therapy)
- Financial records if child support/expenses are relevant
- Any evidence of substance abuse (treatment records, test results) if relevant

Practical organization steps (to make document review efficient)
- Create a chronological timeline of events (one page) with dates and short notes — this is often the quickest way for an attorney to see gaps.
- Index your documents: number or label each document (Exhibit 1, Exhibit 2) and create a one‑page exhibit list summarizing each.
- Scan everything to PDF, name files clearly (YYYYMMDD_type_description.pdf), and create a single zipped folder or share via secure portal.
- Keep originals safe and in one place (don’t mail them unless instructed).
- Prepare short witness summaries: name, contact info, expected testimony in 1–2 sentences.
- Note which documents you don’t have but think matter — list them and any leads to get them (who holds them).
- If documents aren’t in English, begin translation/ certification as that can take time.

Things to confirm with Amy about court procedure and logistics
- How the court accepts exhibits (electronic filing, in‑court binder, number of copies)
- Whether we must file an exhibit list or witness list by a certain deadline
- Whether pre‑hearing briefs or declarations are recommended or required
- Whether Amy recommends mediation or settlement talks before the hearing
- Any local rules in Maricopa County (or EOIR rules for immigration court) that affect scheduling or evidence

Quick email template you can send to Amy Foster
Subject: Quick questions re: evidence and next steps — [Your Name]

Hi Amy — thanks for your help so far. Discovery just closed and I want to make sure we’re fully prepared for the next step. Could you please advise on the items below and let me know a good time to discuss?

1) What is the next court step and do we have any upcoming filing or exhibit deadlines?  
2) Based on discovery, do you think our current evidence is adequate, or what additional evidence would you like me to gather? (If helpful, I can send an indexed packet and a one‑page timeline.)  
3) Are there specific documents, witness statements, or expert reports you recommend we obtain now?  
4) What format does the court prefer for exhibits and how many copies should I prepare?  
5) Any immediate steps I should take to preserve or obtain records (subpoenas/depositions)?  

I can compile and upload documents in PDF form — please tell me the best way to get them to you and any deadlines for your review. Thanks — I’d appreciate a short call if you think that would be faster.

Best,  
[Your name]  
[Phone number]

----

If you tell me which case to focus on (eviction, immigration, custody), I’ll tailor the checklist and draft a more detailed exhibit list and timeline template you can fill out and send to Amy. I can also help you prepare the indexed PDF bundle or a polished exhibit list if you want. Which would you like to do next?

