I can help you get organized. Below is a focused checklist of questions to ask Amy Foster plus a prioritized list of documents/evidence to gather and practical next steps so you’re ready for the next hearing. If you’d like, I can also draft an email to Amy with these questions.

Quick clarifying questions before finalizing anything
- Are you represented by Amy in all matters (eviction, custody, and immigration) or only some of them?  
- Which court specifically is the eviction filed in (Maricopa County Justice Court, Superior Court, or another tribunal)?  
- Which “next date” are you expecting (eviction hearing, custody hearing, or immigration hearing)?  

Questions to ask your attorney
(Use or adapt these in a meeting or email — short, specific items get the best answers.)

About evidence and what’s needed
- Based on the judge’s likely issues at the hearing, what evidence will be most persuasive for our claims/defenses?  
- Which documents do you consider essential and which are only “nice to have”?  
- Are there additional specific documents I should collect now? (e.g., lease, notices, payments, communications, repair invoices, police reports)  
- Do you anticipate needing witness testimony? If so, who should we consider (neighbors, co-tenants, repair people, property managers, daycare/school contacts, etc.)?  
- Should witness statements/declarations be prepared now? If so, should they be notarized or signed under penalty of perjury?  

About admissibility & preparation
- Are there any pieces of evidence that are likely to be excluded as hearsay or inadmissible unless authenticated? How should we authenticate them?  
- Do any documents require certified copies (court filings, prior judgments, police reports, official notices)?  
- Do any non-English documents need certified translation before filing?  
- Will emails/texts/photos/videos need metadata or original device preservation for admissibility? How do we preserve chain of custody?  

Pretrial procedure & timing
- Are there pre-hearing filing deadlines (exhibit exchange, witness lists, pretrial statements, motions in limine) in this court? What are the deadlines?  
- Should we file any pretrial motions now (motions to exclude, summary judgment, continuance, discovery disputes)?  
- Do you recommend subpoenaing records (landlord records, bank statements, phone records, school records)? If so, when should subpoenas be issued to ensure timely service?  
- If discovery is closed, are there options to reopen discovery if new evidence arises? What’s the process?  

Strategy & broader implications
- How might this eviction/custody matter affect my immigration proceedings (and vice versa)? Should we keep or submit certain documents in immigration court?  
- Are there settlement or mediation options we should pursue now? Would settlement discussions affect evidence we can present?  
- What are the likely outcomes at the next hearing and what evidence would move the judge most?  

Practical/administrative
- Do you want originals or copies for your office? How should I label/format exhibits? (PDF, single PDFs per exhibit, numbered exhibits, exhibit list)  
- How many copies will we need for the judge/opposing counsel/your office at the hearing?  
- Should I prepare a concise timeline or exhibit index to give to you in advance? If so, what format do you prefer?  
- Is there anything I should avoid saying or doing (social media, contact with opposing party) while proceedings are pending?  

Documents and evidence checklist
(Organize these now; create a master folder and an exhibit index)

Lease, tenancy & eviction paperwork
- Signed lease agreement, addendums, tenancy rules  
- Rent ledger / rent payment receipts, canceled checks, bank transfers, Venmo/PayPal records showing payments or non-payments  
- Security deposit receipts and any correspondence about the deposit  
- Notices served (pay or quit, cure or quit, notice to vacate) and proof of service (signed receipt, certified mail tracking, sheriffs/constable affidavit)  
- Eviction filing and all court documents (complaint, summons, answers, prior orders)

Communications & interactions
- All texts, emails, direct messages, letters between you and the landlord/tenant/opposing parent (save originals and print copies)  
- Maintenance/repair requests and landlord responses (dates, photos, invoices)  
- Any communications showing landlord’s motive or statements relevant to case

Condition & habitability evidence
- Photos, videos of unit condition (date-stamped if possible), before/after images  
- Repair/contractor receipts, inspection reports, code violation notices from city/county  
- Utilities records if relevant (to show responsibility or usage)

Witnesses & statements
- Names, contact info, short summary of what each witness will say  
- Signed witness declarations/affidavits where possible

Other records
- Police reports / incident reports if any events occurred involving law enforcement  
- Medical records if injuries or health issues relate to habitability or interactions  
- School/daycare records or custody-related documents if opposing party is also involved in custody dispute and those matters overlap
- Banking or payroll records that show rent payment capability or history

Immigration-related documents
- Copies of any filings/status updates in immigration court that might be affected by this eviction or custody matter  
- Confirm whether evidence from this civil matter should be included in immigration filings

Practical organization & preservation steps
- Save originals in a fireproof folder and scan everything to a secure cloud folder. Keep a backup.  
- Create a one-page chronological timeline of key events (date — short note — exhibit number). Judges love timelines.  
- Number exhibits consistently (Exhibit 1, Exhibit 2) and prepare an exhibit index (number, short description, date). Provide this to your lawyer in advance.  
- Ask witnesses to draft short signed declarations now so you don’t scramble later.  
- Preserve devices (phones, laptops) that hold messages and photos. Don’t delete anything. Consider taking screenshots with timestamps and saving originals.  
- If evidence may be lost (e.g., landlord could alter records), tell your attorney immediately; they may send a preservation letter or seek emergency relief.

Suggested short email / call script to Amy
(feel free to ask me to draft this into a ready-to-send message)
- “Hi Amy — discovery closed and we’re waiting on a hearing date. I want to be sure we have everything we need. Can we review what additional evidence, witnesses, or filings you recommend I gather now? I’ve compiled a timeline and exhibit index draft and can send it over. Also: are there any specific deadlines (exhibit exchange, subpoenas) I should know about, and do any documents need certification, translation, or notarization? Finally, please confirm if/how these matters could affect immigration proceedings so we handle evidence carefully. Thanks — [Your Name].”

If you want, I can:
- Draft that email to Amy for you (you can tell me any tone or extra details to include).  
- Help build the timeline and exhibit index from the documents you have (you can upload or list the items).  
- Create a one-page checklist you can print and bring to your meeting with Amy.

