Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — this is a good time to get everything organized and to ask Amy the right questions. A couple quick clarifying questions before the checklist so my suggestions are targeted:

- Which specific case(s) do you want to prepare for now? (You mentioned a landlord–tenant eviction, immigration court, and a custody case — are all three active, or did you mean one of these?)
- Do you already have a scheduled hearing date, or are you just waiting for the court to set it?
- Have you already turned all your records over to Amy, or are there documents you still have only personally?

Below is a ready-to-use set of questions to ask Amy Foster and a checklist of evidence to gather/organize. You can send these to Amy or use them in your next call.

Questions to ask your attorney (use/modify as needed)
- Case status & timeline
  - Has the court set a hearing date? If not, approx. how long until it will be scheduled?
  - Are there any upcoming deadlines I need to know about (filings, exhibit lists, witness lists, prehearing statements, motions)?
  - Is discovery closed now? If closed, can we reopen or request additional discovery if needed?

- Exact issues and burden of proof
  - What specific legal issues will the judge decide at the next hearing?
  - What is the burden of proof for those issues and what type of evidence will be most persuasive?
  - Are there any legal standards or local rules (Maricopa County) that affect admissibility or format?

- Evidence you should collect or strengthen
  - Which documents or types of evidence do you think are currently missing or weak in our case?
  - Do you want originals, certified copies, or notarized affidavits for particular items?
  - Do we need expert reports (e.g., habitability/repair expert, child welfare/psychologist, forensic accountant)?

- Witnesses & testimony
  - Which witnesses should we have at the hearing? Do we need witness affidavits now?
  - Should we depose any witnesses or seek declarations from third parties (landlord, neighbors, school staff, social workers, etc.)?
  - How do we handle witnesses who live out of county/state — will we need subpoenas or depositions?

- Authentication & hearsay
  - Will emails, texts, social media posts, photos, videos, or police reports be admissible? If not, what is needed to authenticate them?
  - Are there particular hearsay concerns (e.g., statements by unavailable witnesses) and how can we address them?

- Records & subpoenas
  - Should we subpoena records (landlord ledger, building maintenance, police records, school/medical records, immigration files)?
  - If so, who will draft/serve subpoenas and by when?

- Immigration & custody overlap (if relevant)
  - If immigration consequences are involved, how will evidence in the eviction or custody case affect immigration proceedings (or vice versa)?
  - Are there special documents the immigration court will require (certified translations, government records)?

- Strategy & motions
  - Should we file any prehearing motions (motions in limine, to exclude evidence, for summary judgment, to compel discovery)?
  - Is settlement or alternative dispute resolution an option we should consider now?

- Practical & logistical items
  - How long do you anticipate the hearing will last?
  - What exhibits need to be filed with the court in advance, and in what format (number of copies, electronic exhibits, exhibit list template)?
  - What will YOU (the client) need to prepare (timeline, witness prep, courtroom protocol)?

- Costs & resources
  - What are the estimated costs for subpoenas, experts, copies, translation, travel, deposition fees?
  - Are any cost-saving alternatives available (e.g., sworn affidavits instead of deposition)?

Evidence checklist — what to gather and how to organize
- Administrative / case basics
  - All pleadings, motions, court orders, and notices (file-stamped copies).
  - Correspondence between you and Amy and between counsel and opposing counsel.

- Landlord-tenant specific (if applicable)
  - Lease / rental agreement (signed pages).
  - Rent ledgers, rent receipts, bank statements showing payments, canceled checks.
  - Notices (pay or quit, notice to vacate, eviction notices) with dates and method of service.
  - Repair requests and repair history (emails, maintenance tickets, texts).
  - Photographs and videos of unit condition (include dates/timestamps and short caption for each).
  - Code inspection reports, building certificates, or complaints to housing authorities.
  - Police reports or incident reports (with case numbers).
  - Witness statements from neighbors or building staff about conditions or events.

- Custody-related (if applicable)
  - Court custody orders, parenting plans, prior family-court filings.
  - School records, attendance records, medical records related to child welfare.
  - Communication records about custody/scheduling (texts, emails).
  - Evidence relevant to parental fitness or the child’s best interests (e.g., drug tests, CPS reports, expert evaluations).

- Immigration-related (if applicable)
  - Immigration documents (I-94, visa, notices from EOIR, forms filed, prior immigration judge orders).
  - Certified translations of non-English documents.
  - Documents showing ties/community/rehabilitation as relevant to relief sought.

- Communications & digital evidence
  - Text messages, emails, social media posts (export or screenshot with metadata if possible).
  - Voicemails or recordings (check local law about consent to record; advise attorney before using).
  - Metadata / original digital files where possible (to show authenticity).

- Financial & corroborating records
  - Pay stubs, tax returns, bank statements, W-2s, employment records.
  - Receipts for expenses or repairs, invoices from contractors.

- Official records & certified copies
  - Certified copies of police reports, marriage/custody certificates, birth certificates, eviction judgments, or other public filings.
  - Medical or school records with release/authorization if needed.

- Witness preparation materials
  - Short written witness statements or sworn affidavits that highlight facts.
  - Contact info and availability for each witness.

- Expert evidence
  - Expert reports, CVs, and invoices. Clarify whether they will testify live or by declaration.

How to organize everything
- Create a chronology / timeline (one page) that maps each key event to supporting exhibits.
- Number and label exhibits sequentially (Exhibit 1, 2, …) and prepare an exhibit index with a 1–2 sentence description for each.
- Keep both physical binders (for in-court use) and a clean digital folder with PDFs named with exhibit numbers.
- For digital files, keep originals and a working copy; note file paths and back them up.
- Get important documents certified or notarized as Amy recommends.
- Prepare a short “evidence summary” memo (1–2 pages) that links each legal issue to the evidence you have and the gaps.

Practical next actions for you right now
- Confirm which cases/courts are active so I can tailor further.
- Make a master list of documents you already have; I can help format an exhibit index.
- Decide if you want me to draft an email to Amy with the above questions and a summary of what you have — I can draft that and include an exhibit checklist.

A note: I’m not your lawyer, so these are organizational and preparation suggestions. Amy Foster will advise on legal strategy and which items are required or unnecessary. If you want, I can draft an email to Amy with the questions above and a short summary of the documents you already have — tell me what to include and I’ll draft it.

