Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help you get organized. Below are focused, practical questions to ask Amy Foster and a short checklist of documents/actions to prioritize before the hearing date. You can copy-paste these into an email or use them in a meeting.

Quick context recap (so the questions fit): landlord–tenant eviction case, discovery closed, waiting for hearing in Maricopa County, AZ; opposing party is the other parent in a custody case; immigration court proceedings are also involved. Ask Amy how the eviction and immigration threads interact (if at all) and whether anything from the custody case matters for the eviction hearing.

Suggested questions to ask your attorney (grouped)

1) Case strategy & goals
- What is our main objective at the upcoming hearing? (dismissal, stay, trial, settlement, possession, damages)
- How do you expect the judge to view the strongest points for each side?
- Are there any themes or narratives we should emphasize in court?

2) Evidence & exhibits (exact items and format)
- Which documents are essential for the hearing (must-have) vs. helpful but optional?
- Do you need originals, certified copies, or will plain copies suffice?
- How should exhibits be organized and labeled for the court (exhibit list, pre-marked exhibits)?
- Are there any authentication steps needed (notarization, sworn affidavits, business records certification)?
- Do any records need translation or certified translation (non-English documents)?

3) Documents to gather — ask whether we already have enough:
- Lease/rental agreement, addenda, and signatures
- Rent ledger and payment history (bank records, cancelled checks)
- Notices served (notice to pay/quit, eviction notices) and proof of service (certified mail receipts, process server affidavits)
- Communications: texts, emails, messages, voicemail logs between parties (preserve metadata if possible)
- Photographs and videos (with dates/time if available)
- Repair requests, maintenance logs, code violation notices, municipal inspection reports
- Police reports, incident reports, 911 calls
- Any prior court orders, judgments, filings in Maricopa County
- Receipts for expenses/damages, estimates, invoices
- Documents tying residence to immigration/custody issues (school records, utility bills, mail)
- Affidavits or signed witness statements

4) Witnesses
- Which witnesses should we call? (landlord, property manager, neighbors, repair persons, police, tenants, witnesses to events)
- Do we need written witness statements or sworn affidavits in advance?
- Are any witnesses likely to be hostile or require preparation for cross-examination?
- If a witness is unavailable, can we use their prior deposition or affidavit?

5) Experts
- Do we need an expert (property condition, repair costs, medical, forensic IT)?
- If yes, when must we retain them and disclose their report?
- Will an expert report be persuasive/necessary given the scope of the eviction hearing?

6) Procedural questions & deadlines
- When will the hearing likely be scheduled? What deadlines come before it (pretrial statements, exhibit lists, witness lists, motions in limine)?
- Are there local Maricopa County rules or judge-specific preferences we must follow (format, timeline, electronic submission)?
- Do we need to file any additional motions before the hearing (summary judgment, motion to compel, motion in limine, continuance)?
- Is there any action to preserve evidence (spoliation letters, preservation order)?

7) Subpoenas & discovery follow-ups
- Do we need subpoenas for documents or witnesses (bank records, phone records, employers)?
- What is the timeline/cost for issuing and serving subpoenas in Maricopa County?
- Can we still request additional records post-discovery if new needs arise?

8) Interaction with immigration & custody issues
- Could evidence or orders in this eviction case affect my immigration proceedings or custody case (or vice versa)?
- Should we obtain particular documents for immigration court now (proof of residence, declarations, affidavits) to avoid duplication?
- Any confidentiality or privilege concerns when using custody-related records in the eviction matter?

9) Hearing logistics & preparation
- What will the hearing look like (bench hearing, evidentiary hearing with witnesses, oral arguments only)?
- How long should I expect to speak/for how long will the hearing be set?
- Do you recommend a mock examination or prep session? If so, when?
- Will you handle direct examinations and objections, and will you pre-mark exhibits?

10) Risks, remedies & settlement
- What are the realistic outcomes and consequences (eviction order, monetary judgment, impact on immigration)?
- What settlement options should I consider and what would be acceptable to me?
- If unfavorable, what are the appeal or post-judgment options/timelines?

11) Costs & timing
- What are the likely costs of additional evidence collection (experts, subpoenas, translations)?
- How long will it take to get any requested additional records?
- If we need a continuance to collect more evidence, how does that affect our position?

Prioritized checklist (what to collect / do now)
High priority (collect these first)
- Lease/rental agreement and any amendments
- Rent/payment records (bank statements, receipts)
- All notices and proof of service (certified mail receipts, affidavit of service)
- Photos/videos of property condition (with dates/timestamps)
- Communications (text, email, messenger) — export and back up
- Police reports or incident reports tied to the dispute
- Any existing court orders or filings in Maricopa County and the custody case

Next priority
- Written witness statements/affidavits from supportive witnesses
- Repair invoices/estimates and code/inspection reports
- Utility bills, mail, or other proof of residence
- Translations of any non-English documents

If applicable / later
- Expert evaluation (property damage, habitability, child welfare expert if custody issues intersect)
- Forensic preservation of electronic evidence if contested

Preservation tips
- Save originals, and keep copies in two places (cloud + local).
- Preserve metadata for electronic files (do not edit photos/timestamps).
- Freeze or archive phone records and emails as they are now.
- Avoid deleting messages or documents; disclose to your lawyer anything relevant.

What to ask Amy to do for you
- Review the attached inventory and tell me what’s missing/critical.
- File any necessary subpoenas or preservation letters.
- Prepare an exhibit list and confirm format/deadline for filing.
- Schedule a prep meeting and mock examination before the hearing.
- Tell me whether any documents should be filed with the court now.

