I can help you get organized. First, one quick clarification: you mentioned eviction, immigration court, and a custody opposing party — are these all the same matter or separate cases? That will change some specifics. If you want, confirm which court(s) and which opposing party applies to each case. 

Below is a focused list you can use with Amy Foster (phrased so you can copy/paste), plus a practical evidence checklist and a simple prep plan so you’re ready for the next steps.

Questions to ask your attorney (copyable phrasing)
- Can you confirm which court(s) these proceedings are in and which specific case we should prioritize? (E.g., eviction in Justice/Maricopa County court vs. removal/custody/immigration court.)
- What is the likely scope and date range of the issues the court will consider at the next hearing?
- Based on discovery results, do you see any evidentiary gaps we must fill before the hearing?
- Which documents do you consider essential for our hearing (admissible, weighty) versus “nice to have”?
- Are there any documents we should authenticate or get certified copies of (leases, birth certificates, immigration forms, court orders)?
- Do we need witness statements, sworn declarations, or affidavits from any individuals now? If so, who and what should they include?
- Should we subpoena any records (bank, landlord/tenant records, school, employer, police, property management)? If yes, which and for what time period?
- Are expert witnesses likely to be necessary (e.g., property condition, valuation, parenting evaluator, medical/psych expert)? If so, what scope and when must they be retained?
- Do any documents require translation or certified translation? Who should perform that?
- Are there potential evidentiary objections we should anticipate and how can we shore up those issues now (authentication, hearsay exceptions, foundation)?
- Should we prepare a pre-hearing exhibit list and pre-admit any documents? What format does the court prefer (electronic, paper, exhibit tabs)?
- Are there pending motions we should file before the hearing (motions in limine, to exclude evidence, summary disposition, protective orders)?
- What is our strategy for witnesses at the hearing (order, direct/redirect, cross-examination points I should know about)?
- Are settlement or mediation options advisable before the hearing? If so, what is your recommended approach / timeline?
- What is our expected timeline for the hearing date? Are there deadlines for pre-hearing filings or disclosures that I should note?
- Do you recommend I collect original documents or are copies sufficient? How many copies and in what form?
- Is there anything I should avoid doing or sharing (social media, contacting the opposing party) before the hearing?

Evidence checklist (what to gather now)
Documents — landlord/tenant & eviction-specific
- Lease/rental agreement(s)
- Rent payment history (receipts, cancelled checks, bank transfers)
- Notices served (notice to pay or quit, cure notices, eviction notices) with proof of service
- Communication log: texts, emails, written notices between you and landlord/tenant or property manager
- Photos and videos of the property (dated or with metadata if possible)
- Repair requests and maintenance records, invoices, contractor estimates/receipts
- Inspection reports or code enforcement complaints
- Move-in/move-out checklists and inventory
- Security deposit records

Documents — custody/other family issues
- Court orders, petitions, previous custody agreements
- Parenting time logs, visitation calendar
- Records of communication with the other parent about custody
- School, medical, or childcare records relevant to parenting capacity
- Police reports or CPS contact if applicable
- Witness contact info and short summaries of what they can testify to

Documents — immigration (if relevant)
- Passports, ID cards, immigration forms (I-xx forms), court notices, bond paperwork
- Proof of ties to community (work, lease, family, payments)
- Any prior immigration filings/responses

General documentation & authenticity
- Birth certificates, marriage certificate, divorce decree
- Bank statements, payroll stubs, employment verification
- Receipts that support timeline claims
- Certified translations of any foreign-language docs
- Original documents where possible; scans/backups in PDF
- Chain-of-custody info for anything that might be challenged

Witnesses and statements
- Names, contact info, and short summaries for each potential witness
- Signed witness statements or affidavits prepared and reviewed by counsel
- List of expert witness needs and any preliminary reports

Preparation & organization tasks for you
- Create a clear chronological timeline of events (one-page timeline + detailed backup)
- Label and paginate exhibits; create an exhibit index (Exhibit 1, Exhibit 2, etc.)
- Make paper copies and a searchable PDF folder; keep originals separate and organized
- Back up files securely (encrypted cloud and local)
- Prepare short bullet-point summaries of what you want each witness to confirm
- Avoid posting about the case on social media or contacting the opposing party directly

Practical procedural questions to confirm
- What will the hearing format be (bench trial, contested hearing, settlement conference)?
- How long will the hearing be and how many witnesses are we allowed?
- Does the court accept electronic exhibits and, if so, in what format?
- Are there local Maricopa County forms or procedures we must follow for exhibit submission?
- Any COVID-era or remote-hearing rules still in effect?

What I can do next for you
- Draft a concise email to Amy Foster listing these questions and asking for priorities and deadlines.
- Create a one-page timeline template and exhibit index you can fill in.
- Help you turn witness notes into simple sworn statements for review by your lawyer.

If you want, I can draft that email to Amy now including just the most important questions and asking for next deadlines — tell me if you’d like it short or detailed, and whether to include a brief case summary (court names, case numbers, and your top concerns).

