  - What exact hearing/trial date(s) or deadlines should I expect next? Are there any mandatory filings or disclosures due before the hearing?
  - Are there Maricopa County (or immigration court) local rules or procedures I need to follow (exhibit submission format, pre‑trial statements, exhibit lists, witness lists)?

- Status of discovery and what’s still possible
  - Since discovery just closed, can we still obtain any additional records or depositions? If so, how and within what timeframe?
  - Is it advisable to move to reopen discovery or file any motions to compel if we’re missing key evidence?

- Evidence needs and admissibility
  - Which pieces of my documentation are most helpful and which might be irrelevant or harmful?
  - Will the court accept electronic evidence (texts, emails, photos, videos)? Do they need metadata, timestamps, or certified copies?
  - What’s required to authenticate documents and digital evidence (affidavits, custodial witness, forensic export)?
  - Are there likely hearsay issues with any of my documents or witness statements? If so, how can we address them (exceptions, witness testimony, sworn declarations)?

- Specific items to gather
  - Are rental/lease documents, payment records, repair requests, communication logs, photos, and inspection notices sufficient for the eviction matter? Anything else I should obtain?
  - For custody: what records strengthen my position (school records, medical/therapy records, police reports, text messages with the other parent, parenting logs, witness statements)?
  - For immigration: which immigration documents or country condition evidence do you recommend I collect (I‑94, application receipts, prior filings, birth/marriage certificates, affidavits corroborating claims, country reports)?

- Witnesses and experts
  - Which witnesses should we plan to call? Do we need written witness statements or affidavits in advance?
  - Should we retain an expert (e.g., for child custody evaluations, property condition, forensic analysis of documents)? If yes, what is the timeline and expected costs?
  - Do any witnesses require subpoenas or travel arrangements?

- Pretrial logistics and strategy
  - Do we need to pre‑mark exhibits and exchange exhibit lists with opposing counsel? What format should I provide them in?
  - Should I prepare a short factual chronology and highlight key documents for you to use at the hearing?
  - Are there settlement or alternative resolution options we should consider before the hearing?

- Administrative & protective issues
  - Are any of my documents sensitive (immigration status, medical) and should we file a protective order or seal certain exhibits?
  - What should I do to preserve evidence now (save original documents, take screenshots with timestamps, secure phones/computers)?

- Costs, timing, and next steps
  - What are the likely costs and timeframes if we obtain additional evidence or retain experts?
  - What do you recommend I do in the next 7–14 days to be best prepared?

Practical evidence checklist to bring / gather now
- General organization
  - Create a folder (electronic and physical) with subfolders: pleadings & case numbers, discovery produced, correspondence, timeline/chronology, exhibits, witness contacts.
  - Prepare a one‑page chronology of events (dates, brief facts, and supporting document references).

- For a landlord‑tenant eviction
  - Lease or rental agreement and any amendments
  - Rent payment records (bank statements, receipts, money orders)
  - Notices (pay-or‑quit, eviction notices, inspection notices)
  - Written communications (texts, emails) with landlord/tenant
  - Repair requests and photos/videos showing conditions (with dates)
  - Move‑in/move‑out inventory or inspection reports
  - Police reports, certificates of service, or notices of nonpayment
  - Witness contact info (neighbors, property managers)

- For custody issues (if relevant)
  - School records and attendance
  - Medical records, therapy records, vaccination records
  - Police reports or CPS records (if any)
  - Texts/emails with the other parent about custody/visitation
  - Parenting time calendar/log (dates/times missed or problems)
  - Photos/videos of the child’s environment (dated)
  - Character/witness statements (signed/dated)

- For immigration proceedings
  - All immigration paperwork (receipts, I‑94, notices to appear, application forms)
  - Identification and civil documents (birth certificates, marriage certificate, passports)
  - Affidavits from family/friends supporting claims
  - Country reports, news articles, expert or medical evidence if relevant to relief
  - Proof of ties to community (employment records, lease, school enrollment, affidavits)

How to preserve and format evidence
- Save originals where possible; for digital items, export native files (not just screenshots) and preserve metadata.
- When taking photos/videos, note date/time and keep a separate log describing each item.
- If you have text message evidence, consider exporting with timestamps (apps or screenshots + affidavit).
- Create an exhibit index with exhibit numbers, short descriptions, dates, and file names.

Trial/hearing prep items to ask attorney to confirm
- Exhibit numbering and exchange procedure
- Witness order and short direct exam outlines
- Any pretrial briefs or statements required and their deadlines
- Whether the court allows remote witness testimony (if needed)

Phrased, ready-to-send questions you can use in an email to Amy
- “Can you confirm the exact court(s) and case numbers for each active matter and our next scheduled deadlines/hearing dates?”
- “Now that discovery closed, can we still seek additional records, depositions, or expert reports? If so, what deadlines apply?”
- “Which documents or types of evidence do you consider essential for our position in court, and which should I prioritize collecting now?”
- “Do we need to pre‑mark and exchange exhibits with opposing counsel? If so, what format and by when?”
- “Are there any admissibility concerns with my digital evidence (texts, photos, videos) and how should I authenticate/preserve those?”
- “Do you recommend retaining any expert witnesses or custody evaluators? If yes, what’s the next step and estimated timeline?”

Final suggestions and offer
- Start by assembling the chronology, document index, and a short list of potential witnesses—this will let Amy quickly see gaps.
