I can help you get organized and prepare a focused list of questions to ask Amy Foster so you know whether you need more evidence before the next hearing. Below is a practical, attorney-ready checklist (documents & evidence to gather), followed by targeted questions you can ask your lawyer, suggested next steps, and sample wording you can copy/paste into an email or message.

Quick clarifying question before you send anything to Amy: are the eviction, immigration, and custody matters all connected (same case or overlapping facts), or are those separate matters handled by different courts/attorneys? If they’re separate, you may need to coordinate evidence between lawyers.

1) Evidence checklist — What to gather now
Mark priority: Must-have / Helpful / Optional

Must-have
- Lease or rental agreement (signed pages).  
- Rent payment history (ledgers, receipts, canceled checks, bank statements showing payments).  
- Any written notices served or received (notice to pay or vacate, termination notices), including proof of service (certified mail receipts, process server affidavit).  
- Communications with landlord/tenant/opposing party: emails, texts, messages, messaging app screenshots (with timestamps).  
- Court pleadings and the discovery you already exchanged (copies of what was served).  
- Any prior court orders involving the property or parties.  
- Photo/video evidence of the property condition with timestamps (or metadata) showing relevant dates.  
- Identification documents you might need to prove identity/status in immigration or custody matters (passport, I-94, ID).

Helpful
- Repair requests, maintenance records, invoices, or contractor estimates.  
- Police reports or incident reports, if applicable.  
- Witness contact information and short notes about what they’d testify to.  
- Utility bills or occupancy records (to prove who lived there when).  
- Bank transfer screenshots, money order receipts, or other proof of payments.  
- Copies of communications with property management company.  
- Any correspondence or records relating to custody that mention housing or eviction (relevant if custody is tied to housing stability).

Optional / Specialized
- Expert reports (e.g., housing inspector, property condition, valuation) — only if advised.  
- Phone metadata or geolocation records (only if relevant and your attorney thinks admissible).  
- Subpoenaed records (bank, phone, third parties) — ask attorney if necessary.

2) How to organize it
- Create a timeline (one page) of key dates (lease start, payments missed, notices, discovery deadlines, discovery responses, date discovery closed, next hearing—if you know it).  
- Number your exhibits and keep originals separate. Make a PDF folder with named files (e.g., 01_Lease.pdf, 02_RentLedger.pdf) and a printed binder with the same index.  
- Prepare short witness summaries (1 paragraph each: who they are, what they will say, contact info).  
- Make a single “evidence summary” document listing each piece and why it matters.

3) Important legal/evidentiary topics to ask Amy
(Use these to structure a meeting or an email. I’ve grouped them so you can pick what’s relevant.)

Evidence & admissibility
- Do the documents I have need to be certified, notarized, or otherwise authenticated before the hearing?  
- Are electronic communications (texts, WhatsApp, Facebook messages) admissible as-is, or should I prepare screenshots + metadata + witness affidavit?  
- Should I prepare sworn declarations from witnesses now? If yes, what should those declarations include and how should they be formatted?  
- Which documents do you plan to use at the hearing, and which just for negotiation/settlement?

Gaps & additional discovery
- Based on discovery responses, do you think we need to move to compel any documents or get additional information from the opposing party?  
- Are there specific documents you recommend subpoenaing (bank records, phone records, property manager logs)? Is a subpoena likely to be granted quickly enough before the hearing?  
- Do we need expert evidence (property inspector, habitability expert, accountant) to establish damages or condition? If so, when would that expert need to be hired and their report completed?

Strategy & procedure
- Is Maricopa County’s local procedure or the specific court likely to require any particular form or exhibit format? (e.g., Justice Court vs. Superior Court distinctions)  
- Given discovery just closed, are there standard pre-hearing filings we should prepare (pre-trial statement, exhibit list, witness list, motions in limine)?  
- What is our plan if the hearing date comes up and you still want more evidence — can we request a continuance, and on what basis is that likely to succeed?

Witnesses & testimony
- Which witnesses should I prioritize getting signed declarations from now?  
- How should I prepare witnesses (what to expect, how to dress, what not to say)? Do you handle witness prep or do I need to bring them to you?  
- If a witness cannot attend, is a sworn declaration or deposition testimony accepted by the court?

Custody & immigration overlap
- If the eviction/custody facts overlap, how could landlord-tenant evidence affect the custody case? Should we preserve any evidence specifically for the custody matter?  
- Does anything in the eviction/court record risk affecting my immigration proceedings? Do you recommend coordinating with my immigration attorney about any documents or testimony? (If you want, I can draft an email to your immigration counsel asking what they need.)

Practical logistics & costs
- Which items are most urgent to obtain now? What will you prioritize collecting/filing?  
- What are the estimated costs and timeline for any necessary subpoenas, expert reports, or witness fees?  
- What is the expected timeline from now until the hearing, and are there deadlines I should calendar?

Settlement & alternative options
- Are we ready to discuss settlement or mediation at this point? If so, what documents would strengthen our negotiating position?  
- If a settlement is reached, what documentation do we need to protect me in the custody or immigration matters?

