I can help — this is a good moment to get an organized list of focused questions and a document checklist so your meeting with Amy Foster is efficient and you know exactly what to gather before the hearing. First, two quick clarifying questions so I tailor the list correctly:

1. Are these separate matters (an eviction/landlord-tenant case, a custody case, and an immigration matter), or is the eviction tied into the custody/immigration proceedings?  
2. Do you already have a tentative hearing date (or any scheduling deadlines) from the court(s)?

If you prefer, I can proceed with a general, combined checklist and question list that covers landlord-tenant, custody, and immigration aspects. Below is a ready-to-use set of questions to ask your attorney and a prioritized evidence checklist and next steps.

Suggested questions to ask Amy Foster (organized by topic)
- Case status & timeline
  - What exact court(s) and case numbers should I be tracking for each matter? (Maricopa County eviction/custody vs. immigration court are different systems.)
  - Has the judge set any deadlines or pre-hearing filings we must meet before the hearing date?
  - Do we expect a scheduling order or pretrial conference? When will we receive the hearing calendar?

- Evidence sufficiency & gaps
  - Based on discovery, do you believe the evidence we have is sufficient to prove our claims/defenses at hearing?
  - What specific evidence or types of proof do you think would strengthen our position?
  - Are there any holes in the opponent’s case we should exploit, or any weaknesses in our case we should shore up?

- Document & exhibit questions
  - Which documents do you want me to bring as originals vs. copies?
  - Do you want documents organized and Bates-numbered or in a trial/exhibit notebook?
  - Should we prepare sworn declarations/affidavits from witnesses to authenticate documents now?

- Witnesses & testimony
  - Which witnesses should we plan to call? Do you recommend written witness statements or live testimony?
  - Do we need to subpoena any third-party witnesses (landlord, property manager, neighbors, school staff, medical providers)?
  - Are there expert witnesses we should consider (e.g., property condition, appraiser, social worker)?

- Discovery follow-ups & motions
  - Is there any outstanding discovery we should pursue (supplemental responses, production of records)?
  - Do we need to file a motion to compel, motion in limine, or any other pre-hearing motions now?
  - If new evidence appears, what is the deadline to disclose it to the other side and the court?

- Admissibility & authentication
  - How should we authenticate electronic evidence (texts, emails, social media)?
  - Do we need business records affidavits or certified copies for records (rent ledgers, clinic records, school records)?
  - Are there likely hearsay objections, and how can we prepare to admit those items?

- Strategy & settlement
  - What’s our primary objective at the hearing (dismissal, timeline, custody arrangement, eviction relief)?
  - Should we attempt settlement/mediation now or wait until after hearing?
  - What are realistic outcomes and what evidence would most influence the judge?

- Logistics & preparation
  - How should I label and deliver exhibits to the court and opposing counsel?
  - Will you prepare a pre-hearing memo or exhibit list I should review?
  - How long will our oral presentation likely be and how should I prepare to testify?

Prioritized evidence checklist (what you should gather now)
A. Documents common to multiple matters
  - Court papers and docket: complaints, responses, discovery responses, motions, hearing notices, immigration notices.
  - Correspondence: all emails, texts, written messages between you and the other party, landlord, or anyone relevant (keep originals/screenshots).
  - Timeline: a clear dated chronology (one-line per event) of key events relevant to each case.

B. Landlord–tenant / Eviction (Maricopa County)
  - Lease agreement, addendums, and any move-in/move-out checklists.
  - Rent payment records: receipts, bank statements, canceled checks, online payment history.
  - Notices: eviction notices, 3-day/5-day pay-or-quit, repair requests, certified mail receipts.
  - Photographs/video showing condition of the property (with dates/time if possible).
  - Repair invoices, estimates, communication with maintenance/property manager.
  - Police reports or incident reports if safety/behavioral issues are involved.
  - Witness statements from neighbors or property managers.

C. Custody-related (if the opposing party is the other parent)
  - Parenting logs: dates/times of custody exchanges, missed visits, concerns about care.
  - School records, attendance, special needs documentation.
  - Medical/therapy records relevant to the child’s well-being.
  - Photos, text messages or recordings showing interactions or incidents (ensure admissibility).
  - Witness contact info: teachers, babysitters, doctors who can corroborate facts.

D. Immigration-related (if proceedings in immigration court affect the case)
  - All immigration filings, receipts, notices (NTA, hearing notices).
  - Identity documents, passports, I-94, visas, work authorization.
  - Any documents showing ties to community, employment, or hardship evidence you plan to submit.
  - Certified translations for any foreign-language documents.

E. Authentication & logistics
  - Originals vs copies: collect originals of critical records (leases, receipts, official certifications).
  - Certified copies where required (court orders, birth certificates).
  - If you have electronic evidence, export as PDFs, with metadata if possible; preserve devices.

Practical preparation steps to take now
1. Create a single, dated index (Excel or doc) of every document you have: title, date, short description, where stored (physical or folder name), and whether original or copy.
2. Make two organized exhibit notebooks (or labeled digital folders): one “Attorney copy” and one “Court/Exhibit copy” with clear exhibit numbers.
3. Prepare a concise one-page timeline of the facts for each case (eviction, custody, immigration).
4. Flag any documents that may require certification or affidavits for admissibility and ask Amy whether she will prepare those affidavits.
5. Identify and list potential witnesses, their role, contact info, and a one-line summary of what they would say.
6. Preserve evidence: don’t delete messages, remove social media privacy changes, and preserve devices if needed.
7. Ask Amy if she wants you to draft witness statements or declarations to be signed now.

Questions about costs, subpoenas, and experts
- Ask about the cost and timing to issue subpoenas or retain an expert and whether the firm will handle that.
- Ask whether any expert reports (medical, psychological, housing) should be obtained now and who would pay for them.

Pre-meeting agenda to bring to your call with Amy (so the meeting is focused)
- Brief status update / what was produced in discovery.
- What we believe the judge’s key issues will be at hearing.
- Review of evidence checklist and gaps.
- Witness plan and subpoenas needed.
- Pre-hearing filings and deadlines.
- Settlement / negotiation options.
- Action items for you with deadlines.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a short email or meeting agenda you can send to Amy that contains the above questions and asks for specific guidance?  
- Or produce a printable checklist/exhibit index template you can fill in and bring to your meeting?

If you confirm whether these are separate matters (eviction vs. custody vs. immigration) I’ll tailor the list more precisely to avoid overlap and point out any special local Maricopa/immigration rules you should ask about.

