While you confirm, here’s a focused, practical checklist of what to ask Amy Foster and what evidence to gather so you’re ready for the next steps. You can copy/paste any of these questions when you speak or email her.

Questions to ask your attorney (grouped and with short example phrasing)
- Case status & deadlines
  - “Please confirm exactly which hearing(s) are pending, the hearing type, and the deadline(s) I must meet before then.”
  - “When will the court set the hearing date, and are there any filing deadlines (pre-hearing statements, exhibit lists, motions) I should know about?”

- Scope of proof & key issues
  - “What are the specific legal issues the court will decide at the next hearing (e.g., grounds for eviction, immigration relief, custody factors)?”
  - “What elements of proof does the court expect me to show for each issue?”

- Evidence you already have vs. what’s missing
  - “Based on the discovery produced so far, what evidence do you think is still missing or weak?”
  - “Are there particular document types or witnesses that would strengthen my case?”

- Documents to collect (ask if any additional copies, dates, notarization, or certified records are required)
  - Landlord-tenant specific: lease/rental agreement, rent ledger, receipts, voided checks, payment history, notices to pay/quit or cure, written communications (texts/email), repair/maintenance requests, photos/videos of property condition (with dates), move-in/move-out inspection reports, code-violation notices, receipts for repairs, police or incident reports, witness statements (neighbors/maintenance staff).
  - Immigration-specific (if relevant): passport/ID, immigration paperwork/forms, receipts, I-94, immigration filings, criminal/court records (if any), country condition reports, medical records, employment and tax records, school/enrollment records for children, affidavits from employers/community members.
  - Custody-specific (if relevant): evidence of parenting time, school records, medical records, communications with the other parent, affidavits from witnesses about parental fitness, evidence of abuse/neglect if alleged, records of domestic incidents or protective orders.
  - Ask Amy: “Which of the document types above would be most persuasive given our theory of the case?”

- Witnesses and testimony
  - “Which witnesses should we plan to call at the hearing? Can you summarize what each witness should testify about?”
  - “Do we need written witness statements/affidavits or live testimony? If live, can you help prepare a witness outline or script?”
  - “Do we need expert witnesses (e.g., property inspector, medical expert, mental health, immigration expert)? If so, what are the deadlines and costs?”

- Authentication & admissibility
  - “How should I prepare documents so they’re admissible (originals, certified copies, notarized affidavits)?”
  - “How should I authenticate digital evidence — e.g., text messages, emails, social-media posts, photos/videos (metadata, timestamps)?”
  - “Are there evidentiary issues we should anticipate (hearsay, best-evidence rule, chain-of-custody)?”

- Subpoenas & third-party records
  - “Should we subpoena any third-party records (bank, phone company, property management, landlord, school)? If so, which ones and what’s the timeline?”
  - “Do you recommend issuing subpoenas for witnesses who may not cooperate voluntarily?”

- Discovery follow-up and motions
  - “Discovery just closed — are there any follow-up discovery motions or motions to compel we should file now that could produce more evidence?”
  - “Should we consider a motion in limine or other pre-hearing motions to exclude opposing evidence?”

- Strategy & burden of proof
  - “Given the evidence we have, what is our likely strategy at hearing (settlement posture vs. full hearing)?”
  - “What is the burden of proof for the claims/defenses in my case and how do we meet it?”

- Court procedure & logistics (Maricopa County / immigration court specifics)
  - “Are there local rules in Maricopa County (or specific immigration-court procedures) about exhibit exchanges, pre-marking exhibits, or filing lists? If so, what are those deadlines?”
  - “Will the hearing be in-person, by phone, or by video, and what technical/logistics should I prepare for?”
  - “Do we need interpreters or accommodations for any witnesses or me?”

- Settlement / negotiation
  - “Do you recommend attempting settlement or alternative dispute resolution before the hearing? If so, what terms would be acceptable to me?”
  - “If settlement talks are likely, what documentation should I have prepared to support or evaluate offers?”

- Practical & financial questions
  - “What are the likely costs associated with gathering additional evidence (expert fees, subpoena fees, travel) and how soon do we need to commit?”
  - “If we need more time to collect evidence, can we ask for a continuance? What’s the chance the court would grant it?”

- Next steps & action list
  - “Please give me a prioritized to-do list: what should I collect immediately, what can wait, and what you will handle.”
  - “When should I check back with you, and what format should I use to send evidence (scanned PDF, originals by mail, cloud folder)?”

Checklist: documents & items to gather now (prioritized)
1. All written agreements: lease, contracts, parenting agreements, immigration filings.
2. Payment records: rent ledger, bank records, receipts, canceled checks.
3. All notices: eviction notices, pay/quit, cure notices, emails from landlord/tenant or from the other parent re custody.
4. Communications log: export email threads, screenshots of texts (include timestamps), social-media posts; keep originals if possible.
5. Photographic evidence: dated photos/videos (ideally with metadata) showing property condition, damage, or anything relevant.
6. Repair/inspection records: invoices, receipts, service company reports, code-violation letters.
7. Official records: police reports, hospital records, school attendance records, court orders, certified filings.
8. Witness contact info and short written statements/affidavits (date, location, what they saw).
9. Expert reports or contact info for potential experts (inspectors, therapists, immigration experts).
10. Identity & status docs: IDs, passports, immigration receipts, I-94, work records, tax returns (if applicable).

How to organize what you bring to Amy or the court
- Use a cloud folder (Google Drive/Dropbox) named by case + date. Put originals in a labeled binder and scanned copies in the folder.
- Create an exhibit index (Exhibit 1, Exhibit 2, …) with short descriptions and dates; keep both a digital and printed exhibit notebook for the hearing.
- Prepare a one-page timeline (chronology) of key events with dates — very helpful for lawyers and the court.
- Keep an evidence log that notes where each item came from and whether it’s an original or copy.

