I can help — first, a quick clarification so I tailor this correctly:

- Which specific hearing is the next one you’re waiting for (eviction, immigration, or custody)? You mentioned all three — are these separate cases or is one proceeding tied into another (for example, immigration consequences from a landlord/tenant or custody matter)?
- Is Amy Foster the attorney for all of these matters, or just one of them?  

Once you confirm, I’ll tailor the list. Below is a thorough, ready-to-use list you can use now when you talk with Amy Foster (or any attorney) — it covers general preparation, questions to ask about whether you need more evidence, and a practical evidence checklist organized by common case types (eviction, immigration, custody). You can copy the parts that apply.

Questions to ask your attorney
- Case & timeline
  - Which specific court and judge will handle the next hearing? (Confirm docket/venue and local rules.)
  - What is the legal issue the court will decide at the next hearing? (E.g., merits, calendar call, evidentiary hearing, status conference.)
  - What is the timetable from now until the hearing (deadlines for filing exhibits, witness lists, motions in limine, pre-trial statements)?
- Burden of proof & key legal issues
  - What elements/facts does the side bearing the burden need to prove at the hearing?
  - Which facts or documents does the attorney consider decisive vs. helpful but not essential?
- Evidence & discovery
  - From the discovery that just closed: is anything missing or weak that we should supplement?
  - Do we need additional discovery tools (subpoenas, deposition, interrogatories, requests for admission) to get essential evidence?
  - Are any pieces of evidence likely to be excluded as inadmissible? If so, can we fix or authenticate them now?
- Witnesses
  - Which witnesses are essential? Which are corroborative?
  - Should we get written witness statements/affidavits now? Are witness declarations acceptable or do we need live testimony?
  - Are any witnesses likely to be unavailable at hearing? Should we subpoena them?
- Experts & evaluations
  - Does the case need an expert (e.g., property condition, habitability, child custody evaluator, immigration country conditions expert)?  
  - If yes, what deadline applies for expert reports and disclosure?
- Authentication & chain of custody
  - Which documents need extra steps to authenticate (e.g., business records affidavits, certified copies, original receipts)?
  - Do any records require notarization, certified copies, translations, or apostilles?
- Strategy & settlement
  - What is the likely courtroom strategy and fallback positions?
  - Should we explore settlement or alternative dispute resolution now? If so, what evidence or proposals should we prepare?
- Risks & consequences
  - What are the immediate practical consequences if we lose the next hearing (eviction timeline, immigration detention/removal, custody changes)?
  - Are there any collateral consequences to be aware of (immigration, housing records, criminal)?
- Logistics & courtroom procedure
  - How long will the hearing likely last? How many witnesses will be called?
  - Are there filing format rules (exhibit numbering, electronic filing, courtesy copies)?
  - Does the court require pre-marked exhibits, exhibit lists, or binder copies for the judge/opposing counsel?
- Next steps & action items
  - Based on what we have, what specifically should I gather/prepare by X date?
  - Who will handle gathering or requesting specific records (you, me, investigator)? Any forms or authorizations you need from me?

Evidence checklist — general items to gather now
(Keep originals and provide copies; create a clear exhibit list and timeline.)

General documentation
- Case file: all pleadings, discovery responses, and court notices
- Timeline: a one-page chronological timeline of key dates/events with references to documents
- Communication logs: texts, emails, messages, call logs with dates/times (screenshots + originals)
- IDs: government IDs, immigration documents, social security card, passports
- Photographs/videos: dates, times, and context notes; store originals and copies
- Witness list: name, contact info, summary of expected testimony, availability
- Receipts, invoices, bank statements showing payments or expenses
- Police reports, 911 calls, incident reports (if relevant)
- Medical records or school records (if custody), with release/consent as needed
- Any prior court orders, judgments, or related case documents

Eviction / landlord-tenant specific
- Lease or rental agreement (signed)
- Rent ledger showing payments, late payments, returned checks
- Notices served (notice to vacate, cure notices) — originals and proof of service
- Repair requests and landlord responses (maintenance requests, photos showing defects)
- Habitability evidence: photos, inspection reports, utility shutoff notices
- Move-in condition report and move-out checklist (if any)
- Communications where tenant/landlord discusses terms, notices, or offers
- Witnesses (neighbors, maintenance staff)
- County/local eviction forms and proof of service, if already filed

Immigration-specific (if relevant)
- All immigration filings and notices from EOIR or USCIS
- Passport, I-94, visa, work authorization, any prior removal orders
- Country conditions evidence (reports, news articles) if relevant to relief
- Criminal records or arrests, dispositions, plea agreements, if any
- Documents demonstrating eligibility for relief (family ties, hardship, asylum-related evidence)
- Translations and certified copies of foreign documents
- Any expert or corroborating witness who can speak to country conditions, credibility, or hardship

Custody / family law specific
- Child’s birth certificate, school records, medical records
- Parenting time records (calendar of days/hours, missed exchanges)
- Evidence of parental fitness (therapy, counseling, domestic violence records)
- Photos, messages, records relevant to safety or well-being of the child
- Reports from CPS or other agencies, prior custody orders
- Any supervised-visit notes or evaluator reports
- Witnesses: teachers, pediatricians, family members

Admissibility & authentication steps
- Make sure business records include a custodian affidavit if necessary
- Get certified copies where required (court orders, criminal records)
- Prepare witness affidavits signed under penalty of perjury where live testimony may be unavailable
- Translate and certify non‑English documents
- Label exhibits consistently (Exhibit 1, Exhibit 2) and keep a master exhibit list with Bates or exhibit numbers

Practical organization & preparation
- Create a folder (physical + digital) with a cover sheet and table of contents
- Create a one-page case summary for quick reference for Amy at the hearing
- Prepare an exhibit binder for the judge and one for opposing counsel — pre-marked and tabbed
- Prepare concise bullet-point witness examination outlines (direct and cross)
- Prepare short witness statements or affidavits summarizing their expected testimony
- Backup everything in cloud storage and keep local copies; timestamped files are helpful
- Confirm hearing logistics (arrival time, courtroom location, parking, security)

