Thanks — I can help you get organized. Quick clarification before I go deeper: are these all part of the same matter (eviction + immigration + custody connected), or are you preparing for one specific hearing (eviction, immigration court, or a custody hearing)? That will let me tailor the checklist precisely. If you're not sure, no problem — below is a comprehensive, prioritized set of questions and a document checklist you can use with Amy Foster to determine what else to gather before the next date.

Suggested questions to ask your attorney (Amy Foster)
- Case status & next procedural steps
  - Can you confirm exactly which court(s) and docket numbers are active and which hearing(s) we are preparing for?
  - Has discovery officially closed? If so, are there any outstanding discovery disputes or motions still pending?
  - When should I expect the hearing date/notice and how much time will I have to prepare once it's scheduled?
- Evidence sufficiency & gaps
  - Based on the material we already have, what evidence do you think is missing or weak for our theory of the case?
  - Are there particular facts the judge tends to focus on in these types of hearings that we should strengthen with documents or witnesses?
- Admissibility & foundation
  - Which of my documents are likely admissible as-is, and which will need witness testimony, authentication or certified copies?
  - Do any documents require certified translations or notarization for this court?
- Witnesses & statements
  - Should we obtain written declarations/affidavits from witnesses now, or will oral testimony at the hearing be enough?
  - Do we need to subpoena any witnesses or records (landlord, property manager, school, employer, medical provider, etc.)? If so, please start subpoenas now — what’s the timeline?
- Records & third-party evidence
  - Which third-party records should we subpoena or request now (e.g., phone carriers, bank records, repair invoices, property management records, school or medical records)?
  - Is there a need for expert reports (e.g., habitability/repairs, forensic accounting, mental health, immigration country condition expert)?
- Strategy & motions
  - Should we file any pre-hearing motions (motions in limine, to exclude evidence, to compel, or for a protective order)?
  - Do you recommend any settlement or interim measures while we wait for the hearing?
- Practicals for hearing day
  - How many copies of exhibits do we need for the judge, opposing counsel, and the court clerk? Are electronic submissions accepted in this court?
  - Do you want me to bring original documents, or are certified copies sufficient?
- Timeline, deadlines, and follow-up
  - What are the deadlines for exhibit lists, witness lists, and pretrial disclosures in this court?
  - If we discover new evidence after discovery closed, what is the process for submitting it or reopening discovery?
- Cost/risk/time assessment
  - What’s the estimated timeline and likely costs if we pursue additional discovery or experts? What are the tradeoffs?
  - What key outcomes should I expect and what is our plan if the hearing is continued/delayed?
- Communication & responsibilities
  - What should I collect personally and send to you (format, labeling), and what will you or your staff obtain?
  - Who will handle subpoenas, certified documents, and translations — you or should I arrange them?

Documents and evidence checklist (prioritized)
Note: Collect originals where possible and make clearly labeled copies. Keep a master index.

General documents (use these for any of the three case types)
- ID documents: passports, driver’s license, birth certificates, I-94, immigration documents (if applicable)
- Chronology/timeline: one-page timeline of key dates and events
- Communications: emails, text messages, voicemail transcripts, social media messages, saved screenshots with metadata (dates/times)
- Photos/videos: timestamped, with a short note of context (what, when, who took it)
- Receipts/bank records: payments, transfers, rent payments, repairs, invoices
- Official reports: police reports, incident reports, medical records, school records
- Prior court orders, pleadings, and filings: all docket entries, orders, service records

Eviction / landlord-tenant specific
- Lease agreement and any amendments / written notices (notice to vacate, 5-day/10-day notices, etc.)
- Rent payment history: receipts, cancelled checks, online payment confirmations, bank statements
- Notices from landlord or notices you provided (with dates and proof of service)
- Photographs of unit condition at relevant dates; repair requests and landlord responses
- Records of habitability complaints (calls, emails), city or county code enforcement complaints
- Witness contact info (neighbors, building staff) and written statements
- Move-in/move-out inspection reports, security deposit receipts, repair invoices
- Any correspondence about attempts to cure or resolve the issues

Custody / family specifics (if applicable)
- Existing custody orders, parenting plans, or temporary orders
- Child records: school attendance records, teacher statements, medical records
- Communications about custody/visitation (texts, emails)
- Evidence of parental involvement (calendars, visitation logs, photos)
- Police or CPS reports, if any; restraining orders
- Witnesses who can speak to parenting or household conditions

Immigration proceedings (if applicable)
- All immigration filings and receipts (A-number, forms submitted)
- Passport pages, visas, I-94
- Affidavits supporting claims (personal declarations)
- Country conditions evidence (State Dept reports, human rights org reports) — ask your attorney which reports are best
- Criminal records and dispositions (if any)
- Medical or mental health records supporting any claims of hardship
- Certified translations for any documents not in English

Evidence quality and authentication
- Originals or certified copies for official documents (birth certificates, court orders)
- Certified translations for foreign-language documents
- Metadata for electronic evidence (showing dates/times) where available
- Chain of custody documentation if evidence could be challenged

Questions to ask about witnesses and subpoenas
- Which witnesses are most persuasive for our case? Which require subpoenas?
- What’s the schedule to issue subpoenas in your jurisdiction (Maricopa County/immigration court)?
- Will witness statements be taken under oath/affidavit or do you want depositions?

Preparing exhibits and presentation
- Ask how the attorney wants exhibits numbered and labeled
- Ask whether the court accepts electronic exhibits and the file format
- Ask how many paper copies to bring and whether originals should be provided to the clerk
- Ask for a template for an exhibit index and witness list (I can draft one for you)

Practical organizing tips (so you’re ready right away)
- Create a folder (digital and physical) structured by category: Pleadings, Discovery, Communications, Financial, Photos, Witnesses, Expert reports
- Make a one-page timeline and a short “key facts” summary for your lawyer
- Maintain a master exhibit list (exhibit number, short description, date, original location)
- Keep a log of any new evidence gathered with date and how/where obtained

Suggested phrasing you can use when contacting Amy
- “Hi Amy — discovery has closed and we’re waiting for a hearing date. Could you please confirm what additional evidence you think we need before the hearing and which items I should gather now? I can provide: [brief list]. Also please tell me deadlines for exhibit lists, whether the court requires certified copies/translations, and whether you plan to subpoena any records or witnesses. Thanks.”
I can draft a full email to Amy with the detailed checklist above if you’d like.

Next step
- Tell me which of these proceedings you want to focus on (eviction, immigration, custody), and whether the matters are related. I’ll produce a focused, prioritized checklist and — if you want — a short draft email you can send to Amy Foster asking these specific questions.

