Thanks — I can help you get organized. Before I give a tailored checklist, quick clarification so I don’t miss anything:

- Are these separate matters (a landlord–tenant eviction case, a custody case, and an immigration matter), or is one proceeding tied to another?  
- Is Amy Foster handling all of these, or only one (which one)?  
- Do you already have a hearing date or just “waiting for a date”?

If you want, answer those and I’ll tailor everything. Meanwhile, here’s a ready-to-use set of questions and a document checklist you can bring to Amy Foster (or use in a meeting/email) so you can confirm whether you need more evidence before the next date.

Questions to ask your attorney (high-priority)
- Which court and docket is the upcoming hearing in (state eviction court, family court, or immigration court)? What is the exact hearing type (pretrial, merits, status, removal, custody trial)?  
- What is the hearing date/deadline we should be preparing to meet? Are there any interim deadlines (exhibit lists, witness lists, pre-hearing statements, motions in limine, filings)?  
- After discovery closed, is any additional discovery still possible or advisable (e.g., late disclosures, emergency subpoenas, admissions requests)?  
- Are there any specific categories of evidence the court in Maricopa County typically requires for this hearing (authentication, certified records, translated documents)?  
- Which documents or facts does the lawyer see as strongest for our position — and which weak spots need more support?  
- Do you recommend witness testimony or affidavits/ declarations for specific items? Who should we get statements from (neighbors, property manager, repair contractors, teachers, doctors)?  
- Does any evidence we have risk being excluded (hearsay, relevance, authentication issues)? If so, how can we cure that (certified records, foundation, stipulations)?  
- Should we obtain any certified records now (bank records, rent payment history, medical records, school records, immigration file, phone records)?  
- Do we need expert reports or an independent inspection (property condition, habitability, parenting expert, mental-health expert)?  
- Are translations and certified copies required for any foreign-language documents? If so, who should do the translations?  
- Do we need subpoenas served for documents or witnesses? If yes, please list which and who will serve them.  
- Should we prepare exhibits in a particular format or numbering for Maricopa County or for immigration court (electronic vs. paper; number of copies)?  
- What is our plan for cross-examining likely opposing witnesses and for impeaching unfavorable evidence?  
- Are there administrative remedies or settlement/mediation options we should pursue now?  
- Are there collateral consequences to the eviction/custody outcome that affect immigration case? If so, what evidence will matter most for immigration?  
- Confidentiality/protective order: do we need to file one for sensitive docs (medical, immigration, minor’s records)?  
- What do you want me to do before the next meeting (collect X items, obtain affidavits from Y, schedule inspection)?

Checklist — documents & evidence to gather (bring copies + originals if possible)
General organization
- Chronological timeline (one-page) of key dates and events you can hand to your lawyer.  
- Index / exhibit list and short summary for each doc.  
- Contacts for potential witnesses (name, phone, relationship, short note of what they observed).

Landlord–tenant / eviction-specific
- Lease or rental agreement (signed pages).  
- Rent payment records: check stubs, bank transfers, money order receipts, ledgers showing payment history.  
- Any written notices (pay/quit, cure/quit, 5/10/30 day notices), proof of service (certified mail receipt, process server affidavit).  
- Communications with landlord/tenant: texts, emails, repair requests, messages showing agreement or dispute. Screenshot metadata if possible.  
- Photographs / video of the property with date/time if relevant (damage, condition, repairs).  
- Repair invoices, contractor estimates, receipts for repairs, work orders.  
- Code violation notices / inspection reports from municipal authorities (City of Phoenix / Maricopa County).  
- Security deposit receipts and correspondence about return.  
- Witness statements from neighbors, other tenants, property manager.  
- Any prior court orders or eviction filings concerning the property.

Custody-related (if applicable)
- Court pleadings and prior custody / parenting orders.  
- Parenting plan proposals, visitation records, calendars showing exchanges.  
- Texts / emails between parents relevant to custody or parenting.  
- School records, attendance, teachers’ notes, reports about the child’s welfare.  
- Medical/mental-health records for child or either parent, with releases for attorney to obtain certified copies.  
- Photos, messages, or other evidence of abuse, neglect, or care concerns (if relevant).  
- Police / CPS reports (certified copies), if any.  
- Witness statements from caregivers, teachers, pediatricians.

Immigration-related (if applicable)
- Identity documents: passports, birth certificates, IDs. Certified copies if available.  
- Any USCIS / EOIR / A-file notices, forms, prior applications, decisions.  
- Criminal records or disposition documents (if any) — certified court records.  
- Evidence of residence, work, family ties, hardship (affidavits, paystubs, lease, school records).  
- Country condition reports, news articles, expert declarations relevant to relief.  
- Translations with certified translator statements for non-English docs.

Authentication & admissibility
- Certified copies where possible (bank statements, court records, police reports).  
- Chain-of-custody or origin documentation for photos / video (who took it, when).  
- Notarized affidavits from witnesses if they may not appear live.  
- Signed declarations under penalty of perjury (common in immigration matters).

Practical preparation items
- Prepare witness affidavits in draft for Amy to review/approve.  
- Identify and contact likely witnesses now to confirm availability.  
- Make backups: scanned PDFs of all documents, saved in a secure folder and on a flash drive.  
- Ensure translations are sworn/certified and attach translator certification.

Questions about Maricopa County / court logistics to ask Amy
- What are the local filing requirements and exhibit rules for this court? (number of copies, exhibit tabs/labels, electronic filing)  
- Will the hearing be in-person or remote? If remote, what platform and what exhibits procedure?  
- How many copies of exhibits does the judge want, and do we need courtesy copies for the judge/clerk/opposing counsel?  
- Are there courtroom time limits, and how long should my direct examination be?  
- Does the court require proposed orders or findings submitted in advance?

If discovery just closed — immediate things to check now
- Confirm there are no outstanding discovery responses or documents we must still produce — a failure to produce can be harmful.  
- Ask whether we should file any motions now (for sanctions, motions to exclude evidence, motions in limine).  
- Get any records that take time to obtain (school, medical, government-certified records) started immediately because they can take weeks.

How I can help now
- I can convert this into a concise email to Amy Foster listing the questions and the documents you’ll bring — want me to draft that?  
- Or I can make a prioritized “to-do” checklist (what to collect first) based on which hearing you want to focus on once you confirm which matter is the priority.

Which would you like: tailored checklist for one of the matters (eviction, custody, or immigration), a drafted email to Amy, or both? Also confirm the clarification questions at top and I’ll make the next list specific to your situation.

